var tafsirData_4 = {
  "1": "'O humanity! Be mindful of your Lord Who created you from a single soul, and from it He created its mate, and through both He spread countless men and women. And be mindful of Allah—in Whose Name you appeal to one another—and ˹honour˺ family ties. Surely Allah is ever Watchful over you.' (Quran 4:1). The opening verse of Surah an-Nisa, and it sets the foundation for everything the surah is about to legislate.\n\n'O humanity' — ya ayyuha al-nas. Not 'O believers'. The surah will go on to regulate marriage, inheritance, orphans and family disputes, and it begins by addressing every human being.\n\n'Created you from a single soul' — min nafsin wahidah. One origin. Every person the surah is about to discuss — the orphan, the widow, the wife, the heir — comes from the same source, which means no one is a lesser class of being. The same statement appears in 'He created you ˹all˺ from a single soul, then from it He made its mate' (Quran 39:6).\n\nThen the command is repeated: 'be mindful of Allah — in Whose Name you appeal to one another'. People invoke Allah's name when asking a favour: 'for the sake of Allah, help me'. The verse notes how easily His name is used in requests, and asks that He be equally present when the request is granted or refused.\n\n'And honour family ties' — wa al-arham, literally 'and the wombs'. Abu Hurayrah reported that the Prophet, peace be upon him, said: \"The word 'ar-rahm' (womb) derives its name from ar-Rahman, and Allah said: 'I will keep good relation with the one who keeps good relation with you, and sever the relation with him who severs the relation with you'\" (Sahih al-Bukhari 5988).\n\nFamily law begins with taqwa and kinship, not with clauses.",
  "2": "'Give orphans their wealth ˹when they reach maturity˺, and do not exchange your worthless possessions for their valuables, nor cheat them by mixing their wealth with your own. For this would indeed be a great sin.' (Quran 4:2). The surah's first specific ruling, and it concerns the most defenceless person in society.\n\n'Give orphans their wealth' — atu al-yatama amwalahum. The property is already theirs. The guardian is a custodian, not an owner, and the verse frames the transfer as returning something rather than granting it.\n\nThen two forms of theft are named, and both are subtle rather than blatant.\n\n'Do not exchange your worthless possessions for their valuables' — wa la tatabaddalu al-khabitha bi al-tayyib. The guardian swaps his poor-quality animal for the orphan's good one, his damaged goods for the orphan's sound ones, and keeps the books balanced by count while pocketing the difference in quality.\n\n'Nor cheat them by mixing their wealth with your own' — wa la ta'kulu amwalahum ila amwalikum. Once the accounts are merged, no one can prove what belonged to whom, and the guardian spends freely without ever committing an identifiable act of theft.\n\nBoth are ways of stealing while remaining technically defensible. The Quran names them precisely because they are the forms the crime actually takes.\n\n'A great sin' — huban kabira. 'Hub' is a rare word in the Quran, used only here, meaning grave sin. The vocabulary itself is unusual, matching the seriousness of the offence.",
  "3": "'If you fear you might fail to give orphan women their ˹due˺ rights ˹if you were to marry them˺, then marry other women of your choice—two, three, or four. But if you are afraid you will fail to maintain justice, then ˹content yourselves with˺ one or those ˹bondwomen˺ in your possession. This way you are less likely to commit injustice.' (Quran 4:3). One of the most discussed verses in the Quran, and its context is usually left out.\n\n'Urwah ibn az-Zubayr asked 'Aisha about this verse. She said: \"O son of my sister! An orphan girl used to be under the care of a guardian with whom she shared property. Her guardian, being attracted by her wealth and beauty, would intend to marry her without giving her a just mahr, i.e. the same mahr as any other person might give her. So such guardians were forbidden to do that unless they did justice to their female wards and gave them the highest mahr their peers might get. They were ordered to marry women of their choice other than those orphan girls\" (Sahih al-Bukhari 4574).\n\nSo the subject is still orphans. A guardian was marrying his ward to keep control of her property while underpaying her dowry, and the verse closes that route: if you cannot deal justly with her, marry someone else.\n\nThe permission that follows is bounded at both ends. 'Two, three, or four' sets a ceiling where none had existed before. And immediately: 'if you are afraid you will fail to maintain justice, then one'.\n\n'This way you are less likely to commit injustice' — dhalika adna alla ta'ulu. The stated purpose of the whole ruling is the avoidance of injustice, not the expansion of male appetite. Read in context, the verse restricts far more than it permits.",
  "4": "'Give women ˹you wed˺ their due dowries graciously. But if they waive some of it willingly, then you may enjoy it freely with a clear conscience.' (Quran 4:4). A short verse that transferred an entire category of wealth from one set of hands to another.\n\n'Their due dowries' — saduqatihinna. The word comes from 'sidq', truthfulness: the dowry is the proof that the man's intention is genuine. And the pronoun is decisive — it is theirs. Before Islam the bride's father or guardian commonly took the payment. This verse makes the woman the owner.\n\n'Graciously' — nihlah. Commentators including al-Tabari explain this as a gift given freely and without expectation of return, paid with a good heart rather than grudgingly negotiated.\n\nThen the second half: 'if they waive some of it willingly'. She may choose to return part or all of it, and if she does, the husband may use it without discomfort. But the condition is 'tibna lakum 'an nafsin' — of their own accord, from themselves. Pressure invalidates it.\n\nThat clause is easy to abuse and the Quran guards it carefully. A dowry surrendered because a wife was made to feel she was being unreasonable has not been waived; it has been taken.\n\nThe same principle of graciousness runs through the divorce rulings: 'unless the wife graciously waives it or the husband graciously pays in full. Graciousness is closer to righteousness' (Quran 2:237).",
  "5": "'Do not entrust the incapable ˹among your dependants˺ with your wealth which Allah has made a means of support for you—but feed and clothe them from it, and speak to them kindly.' (Quran 4:5). A practical instruction about who should hold money.\n\n'The incapable' — al-sufaha', those lacking sound judgment in financial matters. It covers the young, and anyone else who would lose or squander what they were given. It is a judgment about competence, not about worth.\n\n'Your wealth which Allah has made a means of support for you' — allati ja'ala Allahu lakum qiyama. 'Qiyam' means what you stand on, the support that keeps a household upright. Money is described as structural, which is why it cannot be handed to someone who will scatter it.\n\nBut the ruling comes with three obligations attached, and they prevent the guardian from turning custody into control. Feed them from it. Clothe them from it. And 'speak to them kindly' — wa qulu lahum qawlan ma'rufa.\n\nThat last clause is the one most often ignored. Withholding money from someone who cannot manage it is easily done in a way that humiliates them. The verse forbids that. The refusal must be accompanied by decent speech, an explanation rather than a dismissal.\n\nNote also that the verse says 'your wealth' even though it is discussing the property of dependants. Commentators including al-Qurtubi explain the phrasing as expressing the shared interest of a household: a family's finances are one body, and damage to one part harms all.",
  "6": "'Test ˹the competence of˺ the orphans until they reach a marriageable age. Then if you feel they are capable of sound judgment, return their wealth to them. And do not consume it wastefully and hastily before they grow up ˹to demand it˺. If the guardian is well-off, they should not take compensation; but if the guardian is poor, let them take a reasonable provision. When you give orphans back their property, call in witnesses. And sufficient is Allah as a ˹vigilant˺ Reckoner.' (Quran 4:6). The full procedure for handing over an inheritance.\n\n'Test the orphans' — wabtalu al-yatama. Do not simply wait for a birthday. Give them small responsibilities and observe how they handle them, so that the handover is based on evidence.\n\nTwo conditions must both be met: reaching marriageable age, and being 'capable of sound judgment' — rushd. Age alone is not enough; competence alone is not enough.\n\n'Do not consume it wastefully and hastily before they grow up' — the word 'bidaran' means hurriedly, racing the clock. The picture is of a guardian spending the money faster because the day of reckoning is approaching.\n\nThen a rule of remarkable fairness in both directions. A wealthy guardian must take nothing for his trouble. A poor guardian may take 'a reasonable provision' — bi al-ma'ruf, a customary and modest amount. Guardianship is not made into a burden that only the rich can carry, nor into an income stream.\n\n'Call in witnesses' — good intentions are not a system. Documented handovers protect the orphan from theft and the honest guardian from suspicion, the same principle as the Quran's longest verse on writing down debts (Quran 2:282).",
  "7": "'For men there is a share in what their parents and close relatives leave, and for women there is a share in what their parents and close relatives leave—whether it is little or much. ˹These are˺ obligatory shares.' (Quran 4:7). A single sentence that overturned the inheritance custom of an entire society.\n\nBefore Islam, inheritance in Arabia generally went to men who could ride and fight. Women and children were commonly excluded, and in some cases a widow was herself treated as part of the estate — a practice this surah forbids explicitly at 4:19.\n\nThe verse establishes the principle before any of the arithmetic. Men have a share. Women have a share. The sentence is deliberately built in parallel, using the same words for both, so the equivalence of entitlement is unmistakable even where the amounts will differ.\n\n'Whether it is little or much' — mimma qalla minhu aw kathur. This closes the most common excuse. Small estates were exactly where families argued that it was not worth dividing, and the woman's portion quietly disappeared. The verse pre-empts that: the size of the estate does not affect the existence of the right.\n\n'Obligatory shares' — nasiban mafruda. 'Mafrud' means fixed, imposed, apportioned by decree. Not a recommendation, not subject to family agreement, and not the head of the family's to allocate as he sees fit.\n\nAnyone tempted to treat inheritance as a matter of custom or generosity should note the word. It is a duty.",
  "8": "'If ˹non-inheriting˺ relatives, orphans, or the needy are present at the time of distribution, offer them a ˹small˺ provision from it and speak to them kindly.' (Quran 4:8). Placed between the principle of inheritance and its detailed shares, this verse concerns a moment rather than a rule.\n\nPicture the scene: the estate is being divided, and in the room are people who will receive nothing. A cousin outside the line of heirs. Orphaned children of a distant relative. A poor neighbour. They are watching wealth move and none of it is coming to them.\n\nThe verse does not create a legal share for them. It asks for 'a provision from it' — something given from the estate before the division is finalised, at the discretion of the heirs.\n\n'And speak to them kindly' — wa qulu lahum qawlan ma'rufa. Again the pairing of a material act with a verbal one, exactly as in 4:5. The Quran keeps insisting that how you speak to someone is part of how you treat them.\n\nCommentators including Ibn Kathir note the disagreement over whether this is obligatory or recommended, with a number of the early generation holding it binding. Either way, the moral purpose is plain: a windfall should not be enjoyed in front of the excluded as though they were not there.\n\nInheritance law is precise and impersonal by necessity. This verse ensures that precision does not become coldness.",
  "9": "'Let the guardians be as concerned ˹for the orphans˺ as they would if they were to ˹die and˺ leave ˹their own˺ helpless children behind. So let them be mindful of Allah and speak equitably.' (Quran 4:9). The Quran's method for producing honesty in someone who cannot be supervised.\n\nThe instruction is an act of imagination. Picture yourself dying and leaving small children behind. Picture the man who will be handling their money. Now behave as you would want him to behave.\n\n'Helpless children' — dhurriyyatan di'afa, weak offspring. The word chosen is 'weak', which is exactly what makes the theft possible and exactly what should make it unthinkable.\n\nNo auditor is mentioned, no penalty, no enforcement mechanism. The verse works by changing the guardian's position in his own mind, because guardianship is largely unpoliceable. The only reliable safeguard is a man who genuinely fears the outcome.\n\n'So let them be mindful of Allah' — fal yattaqu Allah. Taqwa is the internal supervisor, and the verse names it directly as the substitute for external control.\n\n'And speak equitably' — wa qulu qawlan sadida, upright, straight, accurate speech. For the third time in this passage speech is legislated alongside money (4:5, 4:8, and here). Commentators including al-Tabari include under this the advice given to a dying man about his will: do not steer him toward disinheriting his own children in your favour.\n\nThe test of your treatment of another family's orphan is what you would want for your own.",
  "10": "'Indeed, those who unjustly consume orphans' wealth ˹in fact˺ consume nothing but fire into their bellies. And they will be burned in a blazing Hell!' (Quran 4:10). After several verses of instruction, the warning.\n\nThe image is deliberately physical. They are not told that they will be punished later for eating the money; they are told that what they are swallowing is fire, now, at the moment of the act. The Quran collapses the distance between the deed and its consequence so that the reader sees them as one thing.\n\n'Unjustly' — zulman. The qualifier matters, because 4:6 already permitted a poor guardian to take a modest provision. The condemnation is of unjust consumption, not of every use of the property.\n\nAbu Hurayrah reported that the Prophet, peace be upon him, said: \"Avoid the seven great destructive sins.\" They asked, \"O Messenger of Allah! What are they?\" He said, \"To join others in worship with Allah, to practise sorcery, to kill the life which Allah has forbidden except for a just cause, to consume riba, to consume an orphan's wealth, to flee from the battlefield at the time of fighting, and to accuse chaste, innocent believing women\" (Sahih al-Bukhari 2766).\n\nConsuming an orphan's wealth is listed alongside shirk and murder. That placement is the measure of how Islam views the exploitation of the defenceless.\n\nThe reason is not hard to see. The orphan cannot complain, cannot litigate, and often does not know what he was owed. A society is judged by what it does when no one can hold it accountable.",
  "11": "'Allah commands you regarding your children: the share of the male will be twice that of the female...' (Quran 4:11). The first of the detailed inheritance verses, and the most misread.\n\nJabir reported: The Prophet, peace be upon him, and Abu Bakr came on foot to visit me during my illness. He found me unconscious, so he called for water, performed ablution and sprinkled some on me. I came to my senses and said, \"O Messenger of Allah! What do you order me to do as regards my wealth?\" So there was revealed: \"Allah commands you as regards your children's inheritance\" (Sahih al-Bukhari 4577).\n\nOn the two-to-one ratio, the point usually omitted is the corresponding financial duty. Under the same law, the man is obliged to provide the dowry (4:4), to maintain his wife and children entirely, and to support relatives in need. The woman's inheritance is hers to keep, with no obligation to spend a coin of it on anyone. The larger share carries the larger liability.\n\nNote also that the ratio is not universal within the verse. Mother and father each take one-sixth when there are children — equal shares. In several other configurations a woman inherits the same as, or more than, a man.\n\n'After the fulfilment of bequests and debts' — the estate is not free wealth; obligations come first. And bequests are capped. Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas asked about giving two-thirds of his property away, then half, then a third. The Prophet said, \"One-third, and one-third is much. It is better for you to leave your offspring wealthy than to leave them poor, begging from people\" (Sahih al-Bukhari 6733).\n\n'You do not fully know who is more beneficial to you' — which is precisely why the shares were not left to human judgment.",
  "12": "'You will inherit half of what your wives leave if they are childless. But if they have children, then ˹your share is˺ one-fourth... And your wives will inherit one-fourth of what you leave if you are childless. But if you have children, then your wives will receive one-eighth...' (Quran 4:12). The shares of spouses, and of siblings from the mother's side.\n\nNotice first that the wife inherits at all. In pre-Islamic Arabia she frequently did not, and in several legal systems for centuries afterwards a widow's position depended on charity. Here it is a fixed entitlement.\n\nNotice second that the shares move with the presence of children. Where there are children, the surviving spouse's portion drops — a half becomes a quarter, a quarter becomes an eighth — because the wealth is now flowing to a next generation who also have claims. The system is not a set of arbitrary fractions; it distributes according to who else is being provided for.\n\nThe clause 'after the fulfilment of bequests and debts' appears four times in this single verse. Repetition on that scale is emphasis. Debts are not the heirs' inconvenience; they are the estate's first obligation, and no share exists until they are paid.\n\n'Without harm ˹to the heirs˺' — ghayra mudarr. A bequest cannot be used as a weapon. Writing a will designed to punish a particular heir, or to squeeze someone out through a technicality, is named and prohibited.\n\nIbn 'Abbas reported that the Prophet, peace be upon him, said: \"Give the fara'id — the prescribed shares — to those entitled to them. Then whatever remains goes to the closest male relative of the deceased\" (Sahih al-Bukhari 6732).\n\n'And Allah is All-Knowing, Most Forbearing' — the law is knowledge, and its Giver is patient with those learning to keep it.",
  "13": "'These ˹entitlements˺ are the limits set by Allah. Whoever obeys Allah and His Messenger will be admitted into Gardens under which rivers flow, to stay there forever. That is the ultimate triumph!' (Quran 4:13). The closing seal on the inheritance rulings.\n\n'The limits set by Allah' — hudud Allah. A 'hadd' is a boundary marker, the line at the edge of a property. The word tells you exactly how the shares are to be treated: not as suggestions to be negotiated within a family, but as a fence you must not cross.\n\nThat framing matters because inheritance is where otherwise decent people quietly cheat. A brother persuades a sister to sign away her portion 'for the good of the family'. A son takes control of the house because he is the eldest. Everyone remains on speaking terms, and a boundary has been crossed.\n\n'Whoever obeys Allah and His Messenger' — the reward attached here is Paradise, and the obedience in question is the correct division of an estate. That should adjust anyone's sense of how significant these rulings are. Getting the arithmetic right is treated as an act of worship.\n\n'To stay there forever' — khalidina fiha. Permanent, in contrast to the property being divided, which was temporary for the deceased and will be temporary for the heirs.\n\n'That is the ultimate triumph' — al-fawz al-'azim. The Quran keeps redefining success in these terms, exactly as it did at the end of Surah Ali 'Imran: 'Whoever is spared from the Fire and is admitted into Paradise will indeed triumph' (Quran 3:185).",
  "14": "'But whoever disobeys Allah and His Messenger and exceeds their limits will be cast into Hell, to stay there forever. And they will suffer a humiliating punishment.' (Quran 4:14). The other half of the pair, and it uses the same vocabulary in reverse.\n\n'Exceeds their limits' — wa yata'adda hududah. The same boundary image as 4:13, now with someone stepping over it. The Quran uses this construction in family law repeatedly: 'These are the limits set by Allah, so do not transgress them. And whoever transgresses the limits of Allah, they are the true wrongdoers' (Quran 2:229).\n\nWhat is striking is the severity attached to what looks like a paperwork offence. Two verses ago the subject was fractions of an estate. Here the consequence is Hell.\n\nThe reason is that the crime is never only financial. Taking someone's inheritance means overruling a distribution that Allah made explicit, which is a claim to know better. It also means robbing a specific person — usually a sister, a widow, a minor — who often cannot fight back.\n\n'A humiliating punishment' — 'adhabun muhin. The word 'muhin' means degrading. It is a fitting response to a sin whose whole method was the degradation of someone weaker: treating a person's legal right as though it were yours to allocate.\n\nThe pairing of 4:13 and 4:14 leaves no middle ground. Divide the estate as commanded, or answer for it. There is no third category for those who meant well.",
  "15": "'˹As for˺ those of your women who commit illegal intercourse—call four witnesses from among yourselves. If they testify, confine the offenders to their homes until they die or Allah ordains a ˹different˺ way for them.' (Quran 4:15). An early ruling, explicitly marked as temporary.\n\n'Until... Allah ordains a way for them' — aw yaj'ala Allahu lahunna sabila. The verse announces its own replacement. It is a holding measure while the community was being formed.\n\nThat replacement came. 'Ubadah ibn as-Samit reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, said: \"Receive from me, receive from me. Allah has ordained a way for those women. When an unmarried male commits adultery with an unmarried female, one hundred lashes and banishment for one year. And in the case of a married male with a married female, one hundred lashes and stoning\" (Sahih Muslim 1690a). The phrase 'Allah has ordained a way' is a direct reference back to this verse.\n\nThe permanent ruling on the unmarried is stated in the Quran itself: 'As for female and male fornicators, give each of them one hundred lashes' (Quran 24:2).\n\nThe clause that survives untouched is the evidential one: 'call four witnesses'. Four adult witnesses to the act itself. This is an impossibly high bar, and it is meant to be. The same surah that punishes the offence makes conviction almost unattainable, and Surah an-Nur adds eighty lashes for anyone who accuses without producing them (Quran 24:4).\n\nIslam treats the sin with utmost seriousness and treats the accusation of it with equal seriousness. Rumour, suspicion and 'everyone knows' carry no weight whatsoever.",
  "16": "'And the two among you who commit this sin—discipline them. If they repent and mend their ways, relieve them. Surely Allah is ever Accepting of Repentance, Most Merciful.' (Quran 4:16). The companion ruling to 4:15, and it too belonged to the early period before the punishment in Surah an-Nur was revealed.\n\nTwo features are worth noting even though the specific penalty was later superseded.\n\nFirst, 'the two among you' — alladhani. Both parties are addressed. The verse does not single out the woman, which was the near-universal practice of the surrounding cultures and remains the instinct of many societies today. The Quran assigns the offence and its consequence to both.\n\nSecond, and more importantly, the exit: 'If they repent and mend their ways, relieve them' — fa a'ridu 'anhuma, literally turn away from them. Stop. Leave it. Let the matter drop.\n\nThat instruction is aimed at the community rather than the court. Once a person has repented and corrected their conduct, the file is closed, and continuing to raise it becomes a new sin belonging to whoever raises it.\n\n'Surely Allah is ever Accepting of Repentance, Most Merciful' — tawwaban rahima. 'Tawwab' is an intensive form: the One who accepts repentance again and again.\n\nThe practical lesson survives the change in the legal penalty entirely. A community that never lets a person move past their worst act is not applying Allah's law; it is contradicting it.",
  "17": "'Allah only accepts the repentance of those who commit evil ignorantly ˹or recklessly˺ then repent soon after—Allah will pardon them. And Allah is All-Knowing, All-Wise.' (Quran 4:17). A verse that has frightened people unnecessarily, because both of its conditions are broader than they look.\n\n'Ignorantly' — bi jahalah. Commentators including Ibn Kathir and al-Tabari report the position of a number of the Companions that every person who disobeys Allah does so in a state of jahalah, whether or not he knows the ruling. Knowing better and doing it anyway is itself a form of ignorance — the ignorance of someone who briefly forgot who he was dealing with. So the phrase does not exclude the informed sinner.\n\n'Then repent soon after' — min qarib. Near. The natural reading is 'before it is too late', and 4:18 defines what too late means: the arrival of death. Ibn 'Umar narrated that the Prophet, peace be upon him, said: \"Indeed Allah accepts the repentance of a slave as long as his soul has not reached his throat\" (Jami' at-Tirmidhi 3537, graded hasan by Darussalam).\n\nSo the window is your whole life. But 'soon' is still the word used, and it carries a warning: nobody knows how much of that window remains.\n\n'Allah will pardon them' — the outcome is stated as a fact, not a hope. 'Ala Allahi' — it is upon Allah, an obligation He has taken upon Himself.\n\nNote the placement. This verse follows immediately on a passage about sexual sin. The Quran legislates severely and then, in the very next breath, holds the door open.",
  "18": "'However, repentance is not accepted from those who knowingly persist in sin until they start dying, and then cry, \"Now I repent!\" nor those who die as disbelievers. For them We have prepared a painful punishment.' (Quran 4:18). The boundary of the mercy just described.\n\nTwo groups are excluded, and both are excluded for the same underlying reason.\n\nThe first are those who repent at the moment of death. 'Until, when death comes to one of them, he says, \"Now I repent\"'. The problem is not the timing as such; it is that this is not repentance at all. Repentance means turning away from something you could still do. At that moment there is no longer any choice being made. It is simply the arrival of certainty, and certainty is not faith.\n\nThe Quran gives the definitive case: 'as Pharaoh was drowning, he cried out, \"I believe that there is no god except that in whom the Children of Israel believe\"' (Quran 10:90). The answer came: 'Now ˹you believe˺? But you always disobeyed and were one of the corruptors' (Quran 10:91).\n\nThe second group are those who die in disbelief. The reason is the same: death ends the period in which choices count.\n\n'Knowingly persist' — ya'maluna al-sayyi'at, they go on working evil, right up to the end. The word describes a life, not a lapse.\n\nRead together with 4:17, the message is exact. The door is open for as long as you are alive and able to choose. It is not open afterwards. Delay is the one strategy that cannot work.",
  "19": "'O believers! It is not permissible for you to inherit women against their will or mistreat them to make them return some of the dowry ˹as a ransom for divorce˺—unless they are found guilty of adultery. Treat them fairly. If you happen to dislike them, you may hate something which Allah turns into a great blessing.' (Quran 4:19). A verse that abolished a custom and then went further.\n\nThe custom: when a man died, his heirs treated his widow as part of the estate. A relative could throw his cloak over her and claim the right to marry her, marry her off to someone else for the dowry, or simply prevent her from remarrying so that her property stayed in the family. The verse ends all of it in one clause.\n\nThen a second abuse, subtler: 'mistreat them to make them return some of the dowry'. A husband who wanted out but did not want to lose the dowry would make his wife's life miserable until she asked for a divorce and bought her freedom with her own money. This is named and forbidden. Compare 4:20-21, which forbid taking any of it back.\n\n'Treat them fairly' — wa 'ashiruhunna bi al-ma'ruf. 'Mu'asharah' is the word for daily companionship, living alongside someone. It covers tone of voice, ordinary courtesy and the hundred small things that no court could enforce.\n\n'If you happen to dislike them, you may hate something which Allah turns into a great blessing.' Dislike is not treated as a sin, and the verse does not demand that a man feel what he does not feel. It demands only that he not act badly on it, and it suggests he may be wrong about what is good for him.\n\n'Aisha reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, said: \"The best of you is the best to his wives, and I am the best of you to my wives\" (Jami' at-Tirmidhi 3895, graded sahih by Darussalam).",
  "20": "'If you desire to replace a wife with another and you have given the former ˹even˺ a stack of gold ˹as a dowry˺, do not take any of it back. Would you ˹still˺ take it unjustly and very sinfully?' (Quran 4:20). The dowry rule at the point where men most want an exception.\n\nThe scenario is stated without euphemism: a man wants to end one marriage and begin another. The Quran does not moralise about the decision. It legislates about the money.\n\n'˹Even˺ a stack of gold' — qintaran. A qintar is a huge quantity, the same word used in Surah Ali 'Imran for hoarded treasure (Quran 3:14). The size is mentioned precisely because a large dowry is where the temptation lives. Nobody argues over a small one.\n\n'Do not take any of it back' — fa la ta'khudhu minhu shay'a. Not a portion, not a negotiated share, not 'what is fair given the circumstances'. Nothing.\n\nThen the question: 'Would you still take it unjustly and very sinfully?' — buhtanan wa ithman mubina. 'Buhtan' means a slander so brazen it leaves the victim stunned. The Quran anticipates the standard method: a man who wants the dowry back invents a fault in his wife to justify it. Naming the slander in the same sentence as the theft shows they usually travel together.\n\nThe financial logic of Islamic marriage is consistent throughout. The dowry was given to her, it is hers, and the end of the marriage does not reverse the gift. 'Umar ibn al-Khattab is reported by al-Tabari, commenting on this verse, to have used it to stop men from limiting dowries at all.",
  "21": "'And how could you take it back after having enjoyed each other intimately and she has taken from you a firm commitment?' (Quran 4:21). The reason behind the ruling in 4:20, and it is an appeal to conscience rather than to law.\n\n'How could you take it' — wa kayfa ta'khudhunah. A rhetorical question, not a new prohibition. The Quran has already banned the act; here it asks the man to look at what he would be doing.\n\nTwo grounds are given.\n\n'After having enjoyed each other intimately' — wa qad afda ba'dukum ila ba'd. The verb 'afda' means to reach out to, to come to be alone with, to have all barriers removed between two people. It is one of the most delicate expressions in the Quran. Two people have shared a private life. Demanding your money back afterwards treats that history as a transaction that can be reversed.\n\n'She has taken from you a firm commitment' — mithaqan ghaliza. This phrase is used only a handful of times in the Quran, and elsewhere it describes the covenant Allah took from the prophets and from the Children of Israel. The marriage contract is placed in that category of language.\n\nNotice the direction of the taking: she has taken it from you. She is the party holding the commitment, and he is the one bound by it.\n\nThe verse is a reminder that a marriage can end without a man behaving disgracefully. How a person conducts himself at the exit reveals what the whole thing was worth to him.",
  "22": "'Do not marry former wives of your fathers—except what was done previously. It was indeed a shameful, despicable, and evil practice.' (Quran 4:22). The first of the marriage prohibitions, and it addresses a specific pre-Islamic custom.\n\nA man's sons could inherit his widows along with his goods, and a son might marry his father's wife — not his own mother, but a stepmother. Al-Tabari, commenting on this verse, records that this was known among the Arabs and that children of such unions were called by a name of contempt.\n\nThree words condemn it: 'fahishah' — shameful, an act of gross indecency; 'maqtan' — despicable, provoking loathing; and 'sa'a sabila' — an evil way. The Quran rarely stacks three descriptions like this, and the intensity is the point.\n\n'Except what was done previously' — illa ma qad salaf. Unions formed before the prohibition are not retroactively made into crimes. This mercy clause appears again at the end of 4:23. Islam draws a line at the moment of revelation rather than demanding that converts unpick their entire past.\n\nNote the placement, immediately after 4:19 forbade the inheriting of women. The two verses close the same door from different directions: a widow is not property, and she is not part of an estate.\n\nThe underlying principle is that marriage creates permanent relationships, not temporary ones. A woman who was your father's wife has a standing in the family that does not lapse when he dies.",
  "23": "'˹Also˺ forbidden to you for marriage are your mothers, your daughters, your sisters, your paternal and maternal aunts, your brother's daughters, your sister's daughters, your foster-mothers, your foster-sisters, your mothers-in-law, your stepdaughters under your guardianship...' (Quran 4:23). The complete list of prohibited degrees, and it is worth reading as a structure rather than a catalogue.\n\nThree categories appear. Those forbidden by blood: mother, daughter, sister, aunt, niece. Those forbidden by breastfeeding: foster-mother and foster-sister. And those forbidden by marriage: mother-in-law, stepdaughter, daughter-in-law.\n\nThe category of foster relations is distinctive to Islam. A woman who nursed an infant becomes his mother in law, and her children become his siblings. Ibn 'Abbas reported that the Prophet, peace be upon him, said about Hamzah's daughter: \"She is not lawful for me. What is forbidden by blood relations is forbidden by foster relations. She is the daughter of my foster brother\" (Sahih al-Bukhari 2645).\n\nNote the qualification on stepdaughters: 'under your guardianship if you have consummated marriage with their mothers—but if you have not, then you can marry them'. The law is precise rather than sweeping.\n\n'Nor two sisters together at the same time' — closing an arrangement that would poison the relationship between them. The Prophet extended the principle: Abu Hurayrah reported that he said, \"A woman and her paternal aunt should not be married to the same man; and similarly, a woman and her maternal aunt should not be married to the same man\" (Sahih al-Bukhari 5109).\n\nThe thread running through the whole list is the protection of relationships that must remain free of rivalry.",
  "24": "'Also ˹forbidden are˺ married women... ˹But˺ lawful to you are all beyond these—as long as you seek them with your wealth in a legal marriage, not in fornication. Give those you have consummated marriage with their due dowries. It is permissible to be mutually gracious regarding the set dowry. Surely Allah is All-Knowing, All-Wise.' (Quran 4:24). The list of prohibitions closes, and the default reopens.\n\n'Lawful to you are all beyond these' — wa uhilla lakum ma wara'a dhalikum. This is the governing principle of Islamic law in matters of this kind: what is forbidden is specified, and everything else is permitted. The prohibitions in 4:22-24 are a short list; the permission is everything outside it.\n\nTwo conditions attach. 'That you seek them with your wealth' — a dowry must be paid, so the commitment is real and costly. And 'in a legal marriage, not in fornication' — muhsinina ghayra musafihin. 'Ihsan' here means fortification: marriage is described as a protected structure. 'Musafih' comes from a root meaning to pour out, to spill — an apt picture of the alternative.\n\n'Give those you have consummated marriage with their due dowries' — for the third time in this surah (4:4, 4:20, and here) the dowry is insisted upon. The repetition tells you how often it was withheld.\n\n'It is permissible to be mutually gracious regarding the set dowry' — after the obligation is established, the couple may adjust it by genuine mutual agreement. The order is deliberate: the right first, the flexibility second. Flexibility offered before a right is secured is simply pressure.\n\nMarriage in this passage is consistently described in terms of what it costs the man and what it guarantees the woman.",
  "25": "'But if any of you cannot afford to marry a free believing woman, then ˹let him marry˺ a believing bondwoman possessed by one of you. Allah knows best ˹the state of˺ your faith ˹and theirs˺. You are from one another... But if you are patient, it is better for you. And Allah is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.' (Quran 4:25). A ruling for a man who cannot afford marriage, addressing a social class the world of that time had written off.\n\nThe context is a society built on slavery. Islam's approach was to dismantle it gradually — by making emancipation the expiation for numerous sins, by encouraging contracts of freedom (Quran 24:33), and by verses like this one, which insists on the human standing of the enslaved.\n\n'Allah knows best the state of your faith' — ba'dukum min ba'd, 'you are from one another'. The same phrase used in Surah Ali 'Imran to establish that men and women are of one origin (Quran 3:195). A man tempted to feel superior is told that the difference between him and her is one of circumstance, not of substance.\n\nHer rights are protected in the same terms as any wife's: 'giving them their dowry in fairness'. The dowry goes to her.\n\n'This is for those of you who fear falling into sin' — the permission exists to keep a man from wrongdoing, not to indulge him.\n\n'But if you are patient, it is better for you' — wa an tasbiru khayrun lakum. The verse ends by recommending restraint over taking the concession. The Quran often grants a permission and then points beyond it, which is a useful principle in itself: not everything permitted is what is best for you.",
  "26": "'It is Allah's Will to make things clear to you, guide you to the ˹noble˺ ways of those before you, and turn to you in mercy. For Allah is All-Knowing, All-Wise.' (Quran 4:26). After twenty-five verses of detailed law, a verse explaining why the law exists.\n\nThree purposes are named, and none of them is control.\n\n'To make things clear to you' — li yubayyina lakum. Clarity is a mercy. Rules that are vague leave the weak at the mercy of whoever is best at arguing, which is exactly how orphans and widows lose their property. Precision protects the person without a voice.\n\n'Guide you to the noble ways of those before you' — sunan alladhina min qablikum. The word 'sunan' means established paths. Islam is not presenting itself as a novelty. It is restoring the practice of the righteous who came before, which is the same claim Surah Ali 'Imran made repeatedly about Ibrahim.\n\n'And turn to you in mercy' — wa yatuba 'alaykum. This is the striking one. Allah turning to His servants is named as a purpose of legislation. The law is not merely a set of demands; it is an approach, a movement toward the people being addressed.\n\n'All-Knowing, All-Wise' — 'Alim, Hakim. He knows what we are, and He arranges accordingly. Knowledge without wisdom would produce rules that are correct but unbearable. Wisdom without knowledge would produce rules that are kind but wrong.\n\nWhen a ruling feels heavy, this verse gives the frame to read it in: it was written by someone who knows you and intends good for you.",
  "27": "'And it is Allah's Will to turn to you in grace, but those who follow their desires wish to see you deviate entirely ˹from Allah's Way˺.' (Quran 4:27). The second of three verses that each begin 'Allah wills' (4:26, 4:27, 4:28), stating the purpose behind the laws just given.\n\n'To turn to you in grace' — yuridu Allahu an yatuba 'alaykum. Allah's will regarding you is repentance and return. That is the direction He is moving in relation to His servants.\n\nSet against it: 'those who follow their desires wish to see you deviate entirely'. Two wills, two intentions, pulling in opposite directions.\n\n'Those who follow their desires' — alladhina yattabi'una al-shahawat. Notice the description. They are not called disbelievers or enemies here; they are named by what drives them. A person governed by appetite cannot leave others alone, because other people's restraint is a standing rebuke to his own conduct.\n\n'Deviate entirely' — an tamilu maylan 'azima. A huge swerve. The Arabic doubles the word for inclination to intensify it. They do not want you to slip slightly. They want the whole structure gone, because a partial standard still judges them.\n\nThat is worth understanding about pressure to abandon religious limits. It rarely stops at the point that would make life convenient for the person applying it. The direction of travel is always further.\n\nThe verse also tells you what to compare. On one side, a Lord whose stated intention is to forgive you. On the other, people whose stated intention is that you fall as far as possible. Read the two wills side by side and the choice looks different.",
  "28": "'And it is Allah's Will to lighten your burdens, for humankind was created weak.' (Quran 4:28). The shortest verse in this passage, and the most consoling.\n\n'To lighten your burdens' — an yukhaffifa 'ankum. Allah's purpose in legislating is to reduce the load, not to increase it. That is a remarkable thing to place at the end of a passage full of prohibitions, and it invites the reader to reconsider what the prohibitions are for.\n\nEvery restriction in 4:19-25 removed a burden from someone. Ending the inheriting of widows lifted a burden. Forbidding the extortion of dowries lifted a burden. Capping the number of wives and conditioning it on justice lifted a burden. What looks like restriction from one side is relief from the other.\n\n'For humankind was created weak' — wa khuliqa al-insanu da'ifa. Not 'humankind has become weak'. Created that way, by design, from the beginning.\n\nThis is one of the most honest sentences in scripture about what we are. We are weak in the face of desire, weak under pressure, weak when tired, weak when nobody is watching. Allah knows this because He built us, and the law was written with that knowledge in it.\n\n'Allah does not require of any soul more than what it can afford' (Quran 2:286) states the same principle from the other side.\n\nThe practical consequence is twofold. Stop expecting yourself to be made of iron, and stop expecting it of others. Weakness is the raw material Allah chose to work with, and He legislated accordingly.",
  "29": "'O believers! Do not devour one another's wealth illegally, but rather trade by mutual consent. And do not kill ˹each other or˺ yourselves. Surely Allah is ever Merciful to you.' (Quran 4:29). From family law to the marketplace, and then to something even more fundamental.\n\n'Do not devour one another's wealth illegally' — bi al-batil. 'Batil' means false, void, without a valid basis. It covers theft, fraud, gambling, bribery, deception in a sale, and any transfer of money that has no genuine ground. The same prohibition appears at 'Do not consume one another's wealth unjustly' (Quran 2:188).\n\n'But rather trade by mutual consent' — 'an taradin minkum. This short phrase is one of the foundations of Islamic commercial law. What makes a transaction lawful is genuine willingness on both sides, which is why concealing a defect, pressuring a buyer, or exploiting someone's desperation corrupts a sale even when both parties sign.\n\nThen the leap: 'And do not kill yourselves' — wa la taqtulu anfusakum. The Arabic carries both readings, and commentators including al-Tabari note them: do not kill one another, since the believers are one body, and do not destroy yourselves.\n\nAbu Hurayrah reported that the Prophet, peace be upon him, said: \"Whoever purposely throws himself from a mountain and kills himself will be in the Fire, falling down into it and abiding therein forever; and whoever drinks poison and kills himself with it, he will be carrying his poison in his hand and drinking it in the Fire...\" (Sahih al-Bukhari 5778).\n\n'Surely Allah is ever Merciful to you' — the reason the prohibitions exist. Both clauses protect the same thing: a life you did not give yourself.",
  "30": "'And whoever does this sinfully and unjustly, We will burn them in the Fire. That is easy for Allah.' (Quran 4:30). The consequence attached to the two crimes named in 4:29.\n\n'Sinfully and unjustly' — 'udwanan wa zulma. Two words, and both are needed. ''Udwan' is aggression, crossing into what belongs to someone else. 'Zulm' is putting something in the wrong place, injustice. Taken together they exclude the accidental, the mistaken and the coerced. This verdict is for the person who knew and chose.\n\n'That is easy for Allah' — wa kana dhalika 'ala Allahi yasira. A phrase that appears when the reader might quietly doubt whether something will really happen. Powerful people who consume others' wealth are usually beyond reach; that is precisely why they do it. The verse notes that the difficulty of holding them accountable is a purely human problem.\n\nNotice again what the surah is treating with this severity. Not idolatry, not murder in war, but eating someone's money wrongly and taking a life. The Quran does not divide sins into 'religious' ones that matter and 'social' ones that do not.\n\nThere is also an implied comfort here for anyone who has been defrauded and has no recourse. Courts fail, contracts are broken, the guilty retire comfortably. The one certainty the verse offers is that the case is not closed and the file has not been lost.\n\nFor everyone else, the warning is simple. Wealth taken wrongly does not become yours by the passage of time.",
  "31": "'If you avoid the major sins forbidden to you, We will absolve you of your ˹lesser˺ misdeeds and admit you into a place of honour.' (Quran 4:31). A verse of enormous relief, and a workable strategy for an imperfect person.\n\nThe Quran distinguishes 'kaba'ir' — major sins — from 'sayyi'at', lesser misdeeds. Staying clear of the first category brings automatic coverage of the second.\n\nWhat counts as major? The Prophet, peace be upon him, listed some. Abu Hurayrah reported that he said: \"Avoid the seven great destructive sins... to join others in worship with Allah, to practise sorcery, to kill the life which Allah has forbidden except for a just cause, to consume riba, to consume an orphan's wealth, to flee from the battlefield at the time of fighting, and to accuse chaste, innocent believing women\" (Sahih al-Bukhari 2766).\n\nAbu Bakrah reported that the Prophet said three times: \"Shall I inform you of the greatest of the great sins?\" They said, \"Yes, O Messenger of Allah!\" He said, \"To join others in worship with Allah, and to be undutiful to one's parents.\" Then he sat up from reclining and said, \"And I warn you against giving false witness\" — and he kept repeating it until they wished he would stop (Sahih al-Bukhari 2654).\n\nThe Quran uses the same category elsewhere: 'those who avoid major sins and shameful deeds, despite ˹stumbling on˺ minor sins. Surely your Lord is infinite in forgiveness' (Quran 53:32).\n\nThis is not a licence for small sins; persisting deliberately in a small sin is itself a serious matter (Quran 3:135). It is a design for a life: guard the perimeter, and the daily failures inside it are absorbed.",
  "32": "'And do not crave what Allah has given some of you over others. Men will be rewarded according to their deeds and women ˹equally˺ according to theirs. Rather, ask Allah for His bounties. Surely Allah has ˹perfect˺ knowledge of all things.' (Quran 4:32). A verse about envy, revealed in the middle of a passage on rights.\n\nCommentators including Ibn Kathir report that it came after questions about the differences between men and women in inheritance and in the obligation to fight. The subject is therefore not general jealousy but the specific unease that comes from comparing your allotted position with someone else's.\n\n'Do not crave' — la tatamannaw. The word means to wish for a thing, to long for what someone else has been given. It is not a prohibition on ambition, and the second half of the verse proves it.\n\n'Men will be rewarded according to their deeds and women equally according to theirs' — lil rijali nasibun mimma iktasabu wa lil nisa'i nasibun mimma iktasabn. The word 'iktasab' means earning through effort. Whatever differences exist in circumstance, the return on effort is personal and unaffected by anyone else's portion.\n\nThen the redirection, which is the heart of the verse: 'Rather, ask Allah for His bounties' — was'alu Allaha min fadlih. Do not envy; ask. Envy fixes your attention on a fixed quantity someone else is holding. Asking Allah opens a supply that is not limited by what anyone else has received.\n\nThat distinction is worth internalising. Envy assumes the world is a divided pie. The believer's position is that the Giver is still giving, and the correct response to seeing someone blessed is to ask, not to resent.",
  "33": "'And We have appointed heirs to what has been left by parents and next of kin. As for those you have made a pledge to, give them their share. Surely Allah is a Witness over all things.' (Quran 4:33). A return to inheritance, closing a transitional issue in the early community.\n\n'We have appointed heirs' — mawali, those who stand in a relation of nearness and mutual support. The word covers relatives who inherit, and the verse confirms that Allah, not custom, assigns them.\n\n'Those you have made a pledge to' — alladhina 'aqadat aymanukum, literally those with whom your right hands have contracted. In pre-Islamic Arabia and in the earliest years of Madinah, men entered into formal pacts of mutual inheritance and mutual defence. The Prophet also established brotherhood between the Muhajirun and the Ansar, and for a period they inherited from one another.\n\nThat arrangement was later ended in favour of blood relations: 'But only blood relatives are now entitled to inherit from one another, as ordained by Allah' (Quran 8:75), and 'blood relatives are more entitled ˹to inheritance˺ than ˹other˺ believers and immigrants' (Quran 33:6). Commentators including al-Tabari and Ibn Kathir discuss whether 4:33 was abrogated by these or whether it now refers to bequests and help given voluntarily.\n\nEither way the instruction survives in principle: 'give them their share' — atuhum nasibahum. Honour what you contracted. A promise made is a debt, and the fact that it was informal does not make it optional.\n\n'Surely Allah is a Witness over all things' — the guarantee behind every agreement made without paperwork.",
  "34": "'Men are the caretakers of women, as men have been provisioned by Allah over women and tasked with supporting them financially... And if you sense ill-conduct from your women, advise them ˹first˺, ˹if they persist,˺ do not share their beds, ˹but if they still persist,˺ then discipline them ˹gently˺. But if they change their ways, do not be unjust to them. Surely Allah is Most High, All-Great.' (Quran 4:34). One of the most debated verses in the Quran, and the details of its wording matter greatly.\n\n'Caretakers' — qawwamun. The word means one who stands over an affair to maintain it, as a manager is responsible for a business. And the verse gives the reason immediately: because he is 'tasked with supporting them financially' — bi ma anfaqu min amwalihim. The authority described is tied to a duty, and where the duty is absent the basis is absent.\n\nThe three steps address 'nushuz' — serious ill-conduct and rebellion against the marriage, not disagreement, not a wife having an opinion. The steps are deliberately sequential and de-escalating in intent: speak to her, then withdraw from the bed, and only then the third.\n\nOn that third step, the Prophet's own practice is the binding interpretation. 'Aisha reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, never struck anything with his hand, neither a woman nor a servant, except when fighting in the cause of Allah (Sahih Muslim 2328a). A man who never once did it cannot be understood as recommending it.\n\n'But if they change their ways, do not be unjust to them' — fa la tabghu 'alayhinna sabila. The moment the problem stops, the process stops entirely.\n\n'Surely Allah is Most High, All-Great' — the verse ends by naming the only one who is genuinely above anyone. A husband reading this as a grant of superiority has misread the final sentence.",
  "35": "'If you anticipate a split between them, appoint a mediator from his family and another from hers. If they desire reconciliation, Allah will restore harmony between them. Surely Allah is All-Knowing, All-Aware.' (Quran 4:35). What happens when a marriage reaches breaking point, and it is addressed to the community rather than the couple.\n\n'If you anticipate' — wa in khiftum. Plural, and it is not the husband or wife being spoken to. Relatives, neighbours and community elders are being told to act before the marriage collapses, not after.\n\n'A mediator from his family and another from hers' — hakaman min ahlihi wa hakaman min ahliha. One from each side, deliberately balanced so that neither party faces a panel. And the word 'hakam' means arbiter, someone with standing to judge, not merely a friend offering opinions.\n\nThe choice of family members is practical. They know the history, they have an interest in the outcome, and they can be reached without a court.\n\n'If they desire reconciliation, Allah will restore harmony between them' — in yurida islahan yuwaffiqi Allahu baynahuma. The success of the process is made to depend on intention. Two arbiters who genuinely want the marriage saved will be given help. Two who want to win an argument on behalf of their relative will not.\n\nNote what the verse does not do. It does not tell the couple to endure in silence, and it does not treat marital breakdown as a private embarrassment to be hidden. It builds a mechanism, and it commands the people around the couple to use it.\n\nIslam permits divorce. But this verse comes first.",
  "36": "'Worship Allah ˹alone˺ and associate none with Him. And be kind to parents, relatives, orphans, the poor, near and distant neighbours, close friends, ˹needy˺ travellers, and those ˹bondspeople˺ in your possession. Surely Allah does not like whoever is arrogant, boastful—' (Quran 4:36). A single verse containing the whole shape of a life.\n\nIt begins where everything begins: 'Worship Allah and associate none with Him'. Then, without a pause, it moves to how you treat people. The Quran keeps joining these two, because a claim to worship Allah that leaves your conduct toward His creation unchanged is not a claim He accepts.\n\nThen the list, and its order is a map of concentric circles. Parents first. Then relatives. Then orphans and the poor — those with a claim based on need rather than blood. Then neighbours, near and far. Then 'close friends' — al-sahib bi al-janb, literally the companion at your side, which commentators including al-Tabari take to include a travelling companion, a colleague, or a spouse. Then the traveller, who has no one. Then those in your household service.\n\nThe circle widens steadily from the people who raised you to the person passing through your town who you will never see again.\n\n'Aisha reported that the Prophet, peace be upon him, said: \"Gabriel kept advising me about the neighbour until I thought he would make him an heir\" (Sahih al-Bukhari 6014).\n\n'Allah does not like whoever is arrogant, boastful' — mukhtalan fakhura. The verse ends by naming the attitude that makes every item on that list impossible. A person impressed with himself cannot be kind to a servant or a stranger, because he does not see them.",
  "37": "'those who are stingy, promote stinginess among people, and withhold Allah's bounties. We have prepared for the disbelievers a humiliating punishment.' (Quran 4:37). The description of the arrogant person from 4:36 continues, and it moves to his relationship with money.\n\nThree stages are described, and they escalate.\n\n'Stingy' — yabkhalun. He holds on to what he has. That alone is a fault, but it is his own.\n\n'Promote stinginess among people' — wa ya'muruna al-nasa bi al-bukhl. He is not content to be tight-fisted; he encourages it in others. This is what a miser does, because generous people around him make his behaviour visible. He talks others out of giving, warns them they are being taken advantage of, and reframes his own meanness as prudence.\n\n'Withhold Allah's bounties' — wa yaktumuna ma atahumu Allahu min fadlih. 'Yaktumun' means to conceal. He hides what he has been given, so that no one asks. Some commentators, including al-Qurtubi, extend this to concealing knowledge as well as wealth: any gift from Allah that is buried rather than shared.\n\nNotice that the same phrase — 'what Allah has given them of His bounty' — was used in Surah Ali 'Imran of those who hoard (Quran 3:180). Both verses insist on the same fact: the wealth was given, and a person who hides a gift is behaving as though he produced it.\n\n'A humiliating punishment' — 'adhaban muhina. Humiliation is the exact reversal of what the boaster of 4:36 spent his life protecting.",
  "38": "'Likewise for those who spend their wealth to show off and do not believe in Allah or the Last Day. And whoever takes Satan as an associate—what an evil associate they have!' (Quran 4:38). The opposite failing, and it receives the same verdict.\n\nVerse 4:37 condemned the man who will not spend. This verse condemns the man who spends freely — and it condemns him just as firmly. That symmetry is the point. The issue was never the quantity of money leaving his hand.\n\n'To show off' — ri'a' al-nas, to be seen by people. The spending is real, the beneficiaries are real, and the whole thing is worthless, because the purpose was an audience.\n\nAbu Sa'id reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, came out while they were discussing the Dajjal and said: \"Shall I not tell you of that which I fear more for you than the Dajjal?\" They said, \"Yes.\" He said, \"Hidden shirk — that a man stands to pray and makes his prayer look good because he sees a man looking at him\" (Sunan Ibn Majah 4204, graded hasan by Darussalam).\n\nThe Quran gives the image for it: charity given to be seen is 'like a hard barren rock covered with a thin layer of soil hit by a strong rain—leaving it just a bare stone' (Quran 2:264).\n\n'Whoever takes Satan as an associate' — qarin, a constant companion who walks beside you. The relationship is described as a partnership, which is exactly how showing off works: you and he are collaborating on the presentation.\n\nThe safeguard is not secrecy for its own sake, but the habit of asking, before each act, who it is actually for.",
  "39": "'What harm could have come to them if they had believed in Allah and the Last Day and donated from what Allah has provided for them? And Allah has ˹perfect˺ knowledge of them.' (Quran 4:39). After describing the miser and the show-off, the Quran asks them a question.\n\n'What harm could have come to them' — wa madha 'alayhim. What would it have cost them? What exactly was the loss they were guarding against?\n\nIt is a devastatingly simple challenge. Both characters in 4:37 and 4:38 were protecting something. The miser was protecting his wealth. The boaster was protecting his image. Neither of them would have lost anything real by believing and giving sincerely, and both would have gained everything.\n\n'From what Allah has provided for them' — mimma razaqahumu Allah. The phrasing removes the last excuse. It was never their own production being requested. It came from Him, and a portion of it was to be passed on.\n\n'And Allah has perfect knowledge of them' — wa kana Allahu bihim 'alima. He knows what they had, what they gave, and why. Nothing about the arrangement was hidden from Him — not the miser's private calculations, not the donor's audience.\n\nThe question is worth putting to oneself in the moment of hesitation, because that is when it does its work. Standing at the point of giving something away, with the familiar resistance rising: what would it actually cost me?\n\nUsually the honest answer is: less than I think, and nothing I will miss.",
  "40": "'Indeed, Allah never wrongs ˹anyone˺—even by an atom's weight. And if it is a good deed, He will multiply it many times over and will give a great reward out of His grace.' (Quran 4:40). The accounting principle behind everything the surah has said about reward and punishment.\n\n'Even by an atom's weight' — mithqala dharrah. A 'dharrah' is the smallest thing the Arabs could name: a speck of dust visible in a shaft of sunlight, or the smallest ant. The unit is chosen for its tininess, because the claim being made is about the smallest possible margin of unfairness.\n\nNothing you did will be undercounted. Nothing you did not do will be charged to you.\n\nThen the asymmetry, which is the good news: 'if it is a good deed, He will multiply it many times over' — yuda'ifha. Justice is exact in both directions, but grace runs only one way. Bad deeds are recorded singly; good deeds are multiplied.\n\nIbn 'Abbas reported that the Prophet, peace be upon him, narrating from his Lord, said: \"If someone intends to do a good deed and does not do it, Allah will write for him a full good deed; and if he intends it and does it, Allah will write for him from ten to seven hundred times, up to many more times. And if someone intends a bad deed and does not do it, Allah will write for him a full good deed; and if he intends it and does it, Allah will write one bad deed\" (Sahih al-Bukhari 6491).\n\n'Out of His grace' — min ladunhu ajran 'azima. The multiplication is not owed. It is a gift added to a system that was already fair.\n\nThe Day of Judgment is described in the same terms: 'whoever does an atom's weight of good will see it' (Quran 99:7).",
  "41": "'So how will it be when We bring a witness from every faith-community and bring you ˹O Prophet˺ as a witness against yours?' (Quran 4:41). A question about the Day of Judgment, and it made the Prophet weep.\n\n'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud reported: The Prophet, peace be upon him, said to me, \"Recite the Quran to me.\" I said, \"O Messenger of Allah, shall I recite to you while it was revealed to you?\" He said, \"Yes.\" So I recited Surah an-Nisa, and when I reached this verse — 'So how will it be when We bring a witness from every faith-community and bring you as a witness against these people' — he said, \"Enough for now.\" I looked at him, and his eyes were overflowing with tears (Sahih al-Bukhari 5050).\n\nThat reaction is the best commentary the verse has. He was not afraid for himself. He was to stand as witness over the people he had spent his life warning, and he already knew what some of them would be shown to have done.\n\n'A witness from every faith-community' — min kulli ummatin bi shahid. Each prophet testifies about the people sent to him. Nobody will be judged by someone who does not know them.\n\n'So how will it be' — fa kayfa. The sentence is left unfinished, which is what makes it powerful. The Quran does not describe the scene. It asks you to imagine standing there while the man who delivered the message to you gives his account of what you did with it.\n\nThe same role is given to this community in Surah al-Baqarah: 'so that you may be witnesses over humanity and that the Messenger may be a witness over you' (Quran 2:143).",
  "42": "'On that Day, those who denied ˹Allah˺ and disobeyed the Messenger will wish they were reduced to dust. And they will never be able to hide anything from Allah.' (Quran 4:42). The answer to the question asked in 4:41.\n\n'Will wish they were reduced to dust' — law tusawwa bihimu al-ard, literally that the earth were levelled over them. They will want to be buried, flattened, made into ground — anything rather than remain visible and accountable.\n\nCompare 'the disbelievers will cry, \"I wish I were dust\"' (Quran 78:40). The wish is for non-existence, which tells you what the alternative feels like.\n\n'And they will never be able to hide anything from Allah' — wa la yaktumuna Allaha hadith. Not a single statement will be concealed. The word 'hadith' means speech, a piece of talk. Even conversations will be produced.\n\nNotice the connection between the two halves. The wish to be dust arises precisely because nothing can be hidden. In this life every wrong can be managed: explained, excused, forgotten, denied. On that Day the entire apparatus of concealment stops working, and the exposure is what is unbearable.\n\nThe verse also quietly answers a question people ask about the Hereafter. Punishment is not described here as something imposed from outside. It begins with a wish — their own wish, to disappear.\n\nThere is a practical implication. Anything you would be ashamed to have played back in public is already recorded. The only version of privacy that exists is the one where you had nothing to hide.",
  "43": "'O believers! Do not approach prayer while intoxicated until you are aware of what you say, nor in a state of ˹full˺ impurity—unless you merely pass through ˹the mosque˺—until you have bathed. But if you are ill, on a journey, or have relieved yourselves, or been intimate with your wives and cannot find water, then purify yourselves with clean earth, wiping your faces and hands. And Allah is Ever-Pardoning, All-Forgiving.' (Quran 4:43). Two rulings in one verse: a stage in the prohibition of alcohol, and the concession of tayammum.\n\nOn intoxication: this was one step in a gradual process. Wine was not banned outright at first. This verse removed it from the hours around prayer, which for a Muslim praying five times a day left very little room. The final prohibition came later in Surah al-Ma'idah. The method is instructive — Allah changed a deeply rooted habit in stages rather than by decree.\n\n'Until you are aware of what you say' — hatta ta'lamu ma taqulun. The reason is given: prayer is speech directed at Allah, and speaking to Him without knowing what you are saying is the problem. That reasoning extends beyond alcohol to anyone praying while barely conscious with exhaustion.\n\nThen the concession. If water cannot be found, clean earth substitutes for it. Jabir ibn 'Abdullah reported that the Prophet, peace be upon him, said: \"I have been given five things which were not given to anyone before me... The earth has been made for me a place of prayer and a means of purification, so any man of my community can pray wherever the time of prayer overtakes him\" (Sahih al-Bukhari 335).\n\nNotice what this means: the excuse of not being able to pray was removed. The full procedure is given in Surah al-Ma'idah, ending 'It is not Allah's Will to burden you, but to purify you' (Quran 5:6).",
  "44": "'Have you ˹O Prophet˺ not seen those who were given a portion of the Scriptures yet trade it for misguidance and wish to see you deviate from the ˹Right˺ Path?' (Quran 4:44). The surah turns to a group it will discuss for the next several verses.\n\n'A portion of the Scriptures' — nasiban mina al-kitab. Not the whole. Commentators including al-Tabari note the precision: what they hold is a share, a part, some of which has been kept and some lost or altered. The Quran does not say they have nothing; it says they have a portion.\n\n'Trade it for misguidance' — yashtaruna al-dalalah. They buy error with the guidance they were given. The transaction image runs right through the Quran's discussion of religious leaders who compromise, and it appeared in Surah Ali 'Imran at 3:187: they 'traded it for a fleeting gain'.\n\nWhat is being sold here is not gold but direction. They had a map, and they exchanged it for a wrong one because the wrong one led somewhere more convenient.\n\n'And wish to see you deviate from the Right Path' — wa yuriduna an tadillu al-sabil. The same wish attributed in 4:27 to 'those who follow their desires'. The two groups are different in profile — one religious, one appetite-driven — and their desire regarding you is identical.\n\nThat is a pattern worth noting. A person who has abandoned a standard rarely wants to be the only one who has abandoned it. Company is the cheapest form of justification, which is why the pressure to join is often stronger than the pressure to approve.",
  "45": "'Allah knows best who your enemies are! And Allah is sufficient as a Guardian, and He is sufficient as a Helper.' (Quran 4:45). A short verse of reassurance, placed exactly where it is needed.\n\n'Allah knows best who your enemies are' — wa Allahu a'lamu bi a'da'ikum. This is a comfort of a particular kind. The believers in Madinah were surrounded by shifting alliances: some of the People of the Book were sincere, some hostile, some pretending. Hypocrites within the city looked identical to believers. Working out who was who was exhausting and often impossible.\n\nThe verse does not promise to identify them. It says Allah knows, which relieves you of the need to be certain. You are not required to see through everyone.\n\n'Sufficient as a Guardian' — wa kafa billahi waliyya. 'Wali' is the protector who takes charge of your affairs, the same word Surah Ali 'Imran used at 3:150 when it said Allah is 'your Master'.\n\n'And sufficient as a Helper' — wa kafa billahi nasira. 'Nasir' is the one who comes to your aid in a fight.\n\nThe repetition of 'sufficient' — kafa — is the point. Not 'Allah is a helper among others'. Sufficient, meaning you may stop looking for a supplement.\n\nThis is the practical answer to living among people whose intentions you cannot verify. Do your part honestly, deal with people fairly, and leave the assessment of hidden motives to the One who does not have to guess.",
  "46": "'Some Jews take words out of context and say, \"We listen and we disobey,\" \"Hear! May you never hear,\" and \"Ra'ina!\" [Herd us!]—playing with words and discrediting the faith. Had they said ˹courteously˺, \"We hear and obey,\" \"Listen to us,\" and \"Unzurna,\" [Tend to us!] it would have been better for them and more proper. Allah has condemned them for their disbelief, so they do not believe except for a few.' (Quran 4:46). A verse about a particular kind of mockery.\n\n'Take words out of context' — yuharrifuna al-kalima 'an mawadi'ih, they shift words from their places. The same charge appears at 'They distorted the words of the Scripture' (Quran 5:13).\n\nThe examples given are all wordplay. 'Sami'na wa 'asayna' — we hear and we disobey — a deliberate corruption of 'sami'na wa ata'na', we hear and we obey. 'Isma' ghayra musma'in' — hear, may you not hear — which can be said in a tone that sounds respectful.\n\nAnd 'ra'ina', a word that in Arabic means 'pay attention to us' but which, twisted slightly on the tongue, becomes an insult in Hebrew. Muslims were told to avoid it entirely and to use 'unzurna' instead: 'O believers! Do not say, \"Ra'ina.\" But say, \"Unzurna,\" and listen attentively' (Quran 2:104).\n\n'Playing with words' — layyan bi alsinatihim, twisting with their tongues. This is not honest disagreement. It is the practice of saying something offensive in a form that permits denial.\n\nThe verse also notes what it would have cost them to speak straight: nothing. 'It would have been better for them and more proper.'\n\n'Except for a few' — the qualification the Quran never drops, exactly as in 3:113.",
  "47": "'O you who were given the Book! Believe in what We have revealed—confirming your own Scriptures—before We wipe out ˹your˺ faces, turning them backwards, or We condemn the defiant as We did to the Sabbath-breakers. And Allah's command is always executed!' (Quran 4:47). A direct appeal, with a warning attached.\n\nNotice the basis of the appeal: 'confirming your own Scriptures' — musaddiqan li ma ma'akum. The Quran does not ask them to abandon what they have. It presents itself as the confirmation of it, which is the argument Surah Ali 'Imran made repeatedly.\n\nThe warning uses two images. The first — faces wiped out and turned backwards — is understood by commentators including al-Tabari and Ibn Kathir in more than one way: as a literal disfigurement, or as a description of being turned back to where they came from, or as a picture of a people whose direction is reversed so that they walk backwards into the past.\n\nThe second image is historical: 'as We did to the Sabbath-breakers', the people of the town by the sea who devised a scheme to fish on the day they had been forbidden (Quran 7:163). Their offence was not open rebellion but clever circumvention, which connects directly to the wordplay described in 4:46.\n\n'And Allah's command is always executed' — wa kana amru Allahi maf'ula. Whatever He decides is done.\n\nWhat is easy to miss is that this is an invitation before it is a threat. 'Believe' comes first, and the word 'before' — min qabli an — means there is still time. Warnings in the Quran are almost always issued while the door is open.",
  "48": "'Indeed, Allah does not forgive associating others with Him ˹in worship˺, but forgives anything else of whoever He wills. And whoever associates others with Allah has indeed committed a grave sin.' (Quran 4:48). One of the most important verses in the Quran on the limits and reach of forgiveness.\n\nRead the two halves carefully, because people frequently take away the opposite of what is said.\n\nFirst: shirk is not forgiven. Associating a partner with Allah is the one sin excluded, because it strikes at the foundation of everything else. 'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud reported: I asked the Prophet, peace be upon him, \"What is the greatest sin in the sight of Allah?\" He said, \"That you set up a rival to Allah, though He alone created you\" (Sahih al-Bukhari 4477).\n\nSecond — and this is the half that gets forgotten — 'but forgives anything else of whoever He wills'. Anything else. There is no second exclusion. No sin a person can name is outside the scope of that sentence.\n\nThe crucial qualification is that this concerns dying upon shirk. A person who abandons it is forgiven, because the door of repentance is open in this life: 'Say, ˹O Prophet, that Allah says,˺ \"O My servants who have exceeded the limits against their souls! Do not lose hope in Allah's mercy, for Allah certainly forgives all sins\"' (Quran 39:53).\n\n'A grave sin' — ithman 'azima. The Quran uses 'great' of very few things.\n\nThe practical lesson is a balance. Guard tawhid as the one thing that cannot be compromised, and never conclude that any other sin has placed you beyond reach.",
  "49": "'Have you ˹O Prophet˺ not seen those who ˹falsely˺ elevate themselves? It is Allah who elevates whoever He wills. And none will be wronged ˹even by the width of˺ the thread of a date stone.' (Quran 4:49). A verse about self-declared holiness.\n\n'Those who falsely elevate themselves' — yuzakkuna anfusahum, literally those who declare themselves pure. 'Tazkiyah' is purification, and the same root was used in Surah Ali 'Imran of what the Prophet does for the believers (Quran 3:164). The difference is who is doing it. Purification granted by Allah through His Messenger is real; purification a man awards himself is a claim.\n\nCommentators including Ibn Kathir report that the verse refers to those who said they were the children of Allah and His beloved, and that no fire would touch them except for a few days — a claim Surah Ali 'Imran answered at 3:24.\n\n'It is Allah who elevates whoever He wills' — bal Allahu yuzakki man yasha'. The authority to certify a person as pure belongs to one party only, and it is not the person himself.\n\nThe Quran states the principle bluntly elsewhere: 'So do not ˹falsely˺ elevate yourselves. He knows best who is ˹truly˺ righteous' (Quran 53:32).\n\n'The thread of a date stone' — fatila, the fine filament running along the groove of a date pit. The Quran chooses these vanishingly small units deliberately — an atom's weight in 4:40, this thread here, the speck on a date stone in 4:53 — to insist that the accounting is exact.\n\nSelf-certification changes nothing in that ledger. It merely makes a person stop working on the things he has decided he no longer has.",
  "50": "'See how they fabricate lies against Allah—this alone is a blatant sin.' (Quran 4:50). A one-line verdict on the self-praise described in 4:49.\n\n'See how' — unzur kayfa. The Prophet is told to look at it, as one looks at something remarkable. The invitation is to notice the audacity rather than merely to note the error.\n\n'Fabricate lies against Allah' — yaftaruna 'ala Allahi al-kadhib. Note the escalation from the previous verse. Declaring yourself pure sounds like vanity, a personal failing. The Quran reclassifies it: since only Allah can certify a person, claiming that certification is putting words in His mouth. It is not boasting about yourself; it is reporting a verdict He never issued.\n\nThat reframing applies widely. Whenever someone says 'Allah is pleased with me', 'Allah has chosen us', 'people like him are destined for the Fire' — a claim is being made about the judgment of Allah on the basis of nothing.\n\n'This alone is a blatant sin' — wa kafa bihi ithman mubina. 'Kafa bihi' means it is enough by itself. Whatever else they may or may not have done, this single item is sufficient to condemn them.\n\n'Mubin' means clear, obvious, needing no argument. The Quran often has to construct a case; here it simply points.\n\nThe safeguard for a believer is a phrase used constantly by the pious of the early generations: 'Allah knows best.' Hope for yourself, fear for yourself, and leave the verdicts unissued.",
  "51": "'Have you ˹O Prophet˺ not seen those who were given a portion of the Scriptures yet believe in idols and false gods and reassure the disbelievers that they are better guided than the believers?' (Quran 4:51). An accusation of striking inconsistency.\n\nThe people described hold scripture from Allah and are committed monotheists in principle. Yet here they are endorsing idolatry — not adopting it themselves, but validating it in others when it suits an alliance.\n\n'Idols and false gods' — al-jibt wa al-taghut. Two words. 'Jibt' is a rare term covering sorcery, divination and worthless objects of veneration. 'Taghut' comes from a root meaning to exceed all bounds, and it denotes anything that is obeyed in place of Allah.\n\n'And reassure the disbelievers that they are better guided than the believers' — this is the sharpest part. Men who possessed revelation told the pagans of Makkah, who worshipped stone idols, that their religion was better than the religion of Muhammad.\n\nCommentators including Ibn Kathir record the occasion: after Uhud, a delegation from Madinah went to Makkah seeking an alliance against the Muslims, and when asked whose way was better, they gave the answer this verse quotes.\n\nThe lesson is about what political alignment does to religious judgment. These were not ignorant men. They knew perfectly well that idol worship was false, and they said otherwise because they had chosen a side first and adjusted their theology afterwards.\n\nAnyone can do this. The moment loyalty to a group outranks loyalty to the truth, a person will find himself defending things he does not believe.",
  "52": "'It is they who have been condemned by Allah. And whoever is condemned by Allah will have no helper.' (Quran 4:52). The consequence of the conduct described in 4:51.\n\n'Condemned by Allah' — la'anahumu Allah. 'La'nah' means to be driven away and excluded from mercy. It is not an emotional outburst; it is a description of distance.\n\nWhy this outcome? Because of what they did in the previous verse. They saw the truth clearly and testified against it for political advantage. A person who does that has not made an intellectual mistake; he has decided that the truth is worth less than the alliance.\n\n'Will have no helper' — fa lan tajida lahu nasira. The refrain that ran through Surah Ali 'Imran (3:22, 3:56, 3:91, 3:192) returns here.\n\nThere is a bitter appropriateness in it. They sought help by cultivating the Quraysh, telling them what they wanted to hear in exchange for support. The verse says they will find no helper — the very thing the whole exercise was meant to secure.\n\nThat is the recurring shape of these warnings. Whatever a person sacrificed his integrity to obtain is precisely what he ends up without. The one who cheated for wealth loses it; the one who lied for protection is left unprotected.\n\nThe Prophet is addressed directly — 'you will not find' — which also serves as a caution to the Muslims: do not expect these alliances to hold. They were built on nothing.",
  "53": "'Do they have control over shares of the kingdom? If so, they would not have given anyone so much as the speck on a date stone.' (Quran 4:53). A question with a sting in it.\n\n'Shares of the kingdom' — nasibun mina al-mulk. Do they own any portion of Allah's dominion? The question is rhetorical, but it exposes what their behaviour assumes. They were acting as gatekeepers of prophethood, ruling on whether Allah could have sent a messenger from outside their lineage.\n\nThen the Quran answers with a devastating observation about their character: 'If so, they would not have given anyone so much as the speck on a date stone.'\n\n'Naqir' is the tiny indentation on the back of a date pit — a mark, effectively nothing. If these people had any control over the distribution of Allah's favours, they would release nothing at all.\n\nThat is a judgment about the psychology behind their objection. Their complaint was never really about whether the Prophet's credentials were valid. It was that a gift had gone to someone else, and they could not bear it.\n\nThe verse also implies a comparison. Allah, who owns the entire kingdom, distributes generously — prophethood, scripture, wisdom, provision. These people, who own none of it, would distribute nothing. The contrast between the actual Owner and the would-be gatekeepers is the argument.\n\nIt is worth turning this on oneself. When we resent someone's success, we are quietly claiming a share in a kingdom we do not own, and we would probably run it far worse.",
  "54": "'Or do they envy the people for Allah's bounties? Indeed, We have given the descendants of Abraham the Book and wisdom, along with great authority.' (Quran 4:54). The Quran names the motive directly.\n\n'Or do they envy' — am yahsuduna al-nas. After several verses describing behaviour, the underlying cause is stated in a single word: hasad. Everything in 4:44-53 — the wordplay, the self-praise, the endorsement of idolatry, the gatekeeping — traces back to this.\n\nEnvy is the diagnosis the Quran gives repeatedly for opposition to the Prophet: 'Many among the People of the Book wish they could turn you ˹believers˺ back to disbelief because of their envy, after the truth has been made clear to them' (Quran 2:109). Note the phrase 'after the truth has been made clear'. Envy operates downstream of knowledge; it does not require confusion.\n\nThen the reply, and it is an argument from their own history: 'We have given the descendants of Abraham the Book and wisdom, along with great authority'. Their ancestors received exactly this kind of favour, and no one objected then. Prophethood in the line of Ibrahim was acceptable while it ran through their branch of the family. The complaint arose only when it went to another.\n\n'Great authority' — mulkan 'azima, the kingdom given to Dawud and Sulayman. Allah has given lavishly to this family before.\n\nThe practical use of this verse is diagnostic. When you find yourself objecting to someone's position, and the objections keep changing while the objection remains, the problem is usually not the reasons. It is that they have something you wanted.",
  "55": "'Yet some believed in him while others turned away from him. Hell is sufficient as a torment!' (Quran 4:55). The conclusion of the argument begun in 4:54 about the family of Ibrahim.\n\n'Some believed in him' — the pronoun refers to what was just mentioned: the revelation given to Ibrahim's line, and by extension the Prophet who now stands in that line. The response was always divided.\n\nThat division is the answer to the complaint in 4:53-54. Those objecting to Muhammad on the grounds of lineage should look at their own history. Prophets came to them, and their ancestors split into those who accepted and those who refused. The pattern is not new, and it is not evidence against the messenger.\n\n'Others turned away from him' — wa minhum man sadda 'anh. The verb 'sadda' carries two senses in Arabic: to turn away oneself, and to turn others away. Commentators including al-Tabari note both. A person who rejects rarely rejects quietly; he becomes an obstacle in the road for others.\n\n'Hell is sufficient as a torment' — wa kafa bi jahannama sa'ira. The construction 'kafa bi' — sufficient — has appeared repeatedly in this surah. Allah is sufficient as a Guardian (4:45), sufficient as a Helper (4:45), sufficient as a Reckoner (4:6). Here the same form is used of the punishment: it is enough by itself, nothing more needs to be added.\n\nThe verse also carries a warning for anyone in the Muslim community who assumes that possessing the truth is the same as accepting it. Every previous community had the truth. Half of them turned from it.",
  "56": "'Surely those who reject Our signs, We will cast them into the Fire. Whenever their skin is burnt completely, We will replace it so they will ˹constantly˺ taste the punishment. Indeed, Allah is Almighty, All-Wise.' (Quran 4:56). One of the most graphic descriptions of punishment in the Quran, and the detail it chooses is significant.\n\n'Whenever their skin is burnt completely, We will replace it' — kullama nadijat juluduhum baddalnahum juludan ghayraha. The skin is where pain is felt. Burnt skin eventually stops transmitting sensation, which is the body's own mercy. The verse describes that mercy being withheld.\n\nThis is deliberately hard to read. The Quran does not soften descriptions of the Fire, because the purpose of the description is to prevent people from arriving there. A warning delivered gently is not a warning.\n\n'Those who reject Our signs' — alladhina kafaru bi ayatina. The condemnation is tied to a specific act: the rejection of evidence that was presented and understood. It is not aimed at those who never received it.\n\n'So they will constantly taste the punishment' — li yadhuqu al-'adhab. 'Taste' is the same verb used in Surah Ali 'Imran of death: 'Every soul will taste death' (Quran 3:185). There, tasting was brief. Here it is renewed indefinitely, and the contrast between the two is intentional.\n\n'Almighty, All-Wise' — 'Azizan Hakima. Power and wisdom together. The power makes the punishment possible; the wisdom guarantees it is not arbitrary.",
  "57": "'As for those who believe and do good, We will admit them into Gardens under which rivers flow, to stay there for ever and ever. There they will have pure spouses, and We will place them under a vast shade.' (Quran 4:57). Immediately after the Fire, the Garden — the Quran's standard method of holding both before the reader at once.\n\n'Those who believe and do good' — alladhina amanu wa 'amilu al-salihat. The pairing is constant in the Quran. Belief without action is a claim; action without belief has no anchor.\n\n'To stay there for ever and ever' — khalidina fiha abada. The doubling is emphatic, and it answers the anxiety that anything this good must eventually end.\n\n'Pure spouses' — azwajun mutahharah. 'Tahir' means free of impurity, and the classical commentators including al-Qurtubi understand it broadly: free of physical impurity, and free of the moral faults that damage relationships — jealousy, resentment, deceit. The promise is not only of companionship but of companionship without its usual difficulties.\n\n'A vast shade' — zillan zalila. Literally 'a shading shade', an Arabic construction that intensifies by repetition: dense, deep, complete shade. To an audience living in the Arabian sun, this was not a decorative detail. Shade meant relief, safety and rest.\n\nNotice how physical these promises are: water, shade, company. The Quran describes Paradise in terms the human body understands, because a purely abstract description would move no one.\n\nPlaced here, after 4:56, the two verses form the choice the whole surah has been building toward.",
  "58": "'Indeed, Allah commands you to return trusts to their rightful owners; and when you judge between people, judge with fairness. What a noble commandment from Allah to you! Surely Allah is All-Hearing, All-Seeing.' (Quran 4:58). Two commands that together form the basis of public life.\n\n'Return trusts to their rightful owners' — an tu'addu al-amanati ila ahliha. An 'amanah' is anything placed in your keeping: money held, a secret told, a position of office, a job you were hired to do, a child in your care. Commentators including Ibn Kathir record that this verse was revealed concerning the key of the Ka'bah, which the Prophet returned to 'Uthman ibn Talhah on the day Makkah was conquered, despite the man not yet being Muslim and despite the family's earlier hostility.\n\nThat occasion sets the standard. A trust is returned because it is a trust, not because the owner deserves it.\n\n'When you judge between people, judge with fairness' — bi al-'adl. Any person who decides between two others — a judge, a manager, a teacher, a parent — is included.\n\nThe two commands are connected. Both are about handling something that is not yours: someone else's property, and someone else's rights.\n\nAbu Hurayrah reported that the Prophet, peace be upon him, said: \"The signs of a hypocrite are three: whenever he speaks he lies, whenever he promises he breaks it, and whenever he is entrusted he betrays the trust\" (Sahih al-Bukhari 33). Betraying a trust is placed among the marks of hypocrisy, not among ordinary faults.",
  "59": "'O believers! Obey Allah and obey the Messenger and those in authority among you. Should you disagree on anything, then refer it to Allah and His Messenger, if you ˹truly˺ believe in Allah and the Last Day. This is the best and fairest resolution.' (Quran 4:59). The verse that structures authority in Islam, and its grammar carries the ruling.\n\nNotice the repetition: 'Obey Allah and obey the Messenger'. The verb is repeated for the Messenger, because obeying him is obedience in its own right — his instruction does not require separate verification against the Quran.\n\nThen: 'and those in authority among you' — wa uli al-amri minkum. The verb is not repeated. Their obedience is derived, not independent. That single grammatical detail is the whole doctrine of limited authority in Islam.\n\nAbu Hurayrah reported that the Prophet, peace be upon him, said: \"Whoever obeys me obeys Allah, and whoever disobeys me disobeys Allah; and whoever obeys the ruler I appoint obeys me, and whoever disobeys him disobeys me\" (Sahih al-Bukhari 7137).\n\nAnd the limit is explicit. 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar reported that the Prophet said: \"A Muslim has to listen to and obey, whether he likes it or not, as long as he is not ordered to commit a sin. But if he is ordered to commit a sin, then there is no listening and no obeying\" (Sahih al-Bukhari 7144).\n\n'Should you disagree on anything, then refer it to Allah and His Messenger' — the mechanism for resolving disputes is a return to Quran and Sunnah, not to whoever holds power. And the condition attached — 'if you truly believe' — makes this the test of faith rather than a procedural preference.",
  "60": "'Have you ˹O Prophet˺ not seen those who claim they believe in what has been revealed to you and what was revealed before you? They seek the judgment of false judges, which they were commanded to reject. And Satan ˹only˺ desires to lead them farther away.' (Quran 4:60). A case study in what 4:59 was warning against.\n\nCommentators including Ibn Kathir record the occasion: a dispute arose between a hypocrite and a Jewish man. The Jewish man proposed taking the case to Muhammad, knowing he would judge fairly. The hypocrite insisted on going elsewhere, to an arbiter he expected would favour him.\n\n'They claim they believe' — yaz'umuna annahum amanu. 'Za'ama' is the Arabic verb for an assertion the speaker cannot back up. The Quran does not say they believe; it says they say so.\n\n'The judgment of false judges' — al-taghut. The same word used in 4:51. Anything set up as an authority in place of Allah's ruling.\n\n'Which they were commanded to reject' — wa qad umiru an yakfuru bih. Rejecting taghut is a command, and it appears in the definition of faith itself: 'whoever renounces false gods and believes in Allah has certainly grasped the firmest, unfailing hand-hold' (Quran 2:256).\n\nThe behaviour described is entirely familiar. A person accepts religious authority in general and then, in the one dispute where the ruling would go against him, finds a reason to take the matter somewhere else. The Quran identifies that as the moment the claim to faith is tested — and, here, fails.",
  "61": "'When it is said to them, \"Come to Allah's revelations and to the Messenger,\" you see the hypocrites turn away from you stubbornly.' (Quran 4:61). The reaction of those described in 4:60 when invited to the correct forum.\n\n'Come to Allah's revelations and to the Messenger' — ta'alaw ila ma anzala Allahu wa ila al-rasul. The invitation is not to a person's opinion; it is to revelation. And it is issued as an offer, not a summons.\n\n'You see the hypocrites turn away from you stubbornly' — yasuddun 'anka sududa. The doubled construction in Arabic — turning away with a full turning away — describes a deliberate, unmistakable refusal. Not a hesitation, not a request for time. A visible turning of the back.\n\nThe word 'sudud' shares its root with the verb used in 4:55, where some 'turned away' from the message given to Ibrahim's line. The behaviour repeats across communities.\n\nWhat is being refused is worth noting precisely. They are not refusing to believe in Allah — they claim that (4:60). They are refusing to have a specific matter decided by revelation, because they know how it will be decided.\n\nCompare 'When it is said to them, \"Come! The Messenger of Allah will pray for you to be forgiven,\" they turn their heads ˹in disgust˺' (Quran 63:5).\n\nThis is the practical shape of hypocrisy: not the denial of religion, but the refusal of it at the one point where it costs something. And that point is always a specific one — a contract, an inheritance, an apology owed.",
  "62": "'How ˹horrible˺ will it be if a disaster strikes them because of what their hands have done, then they come to you swearing by Allah, \"We intended nothing but goodwill and reconciliation.\"' (Quran 4:62). What happens after the refusal in 4:61 goes wrong.\n\n'A disaster strikes them because of what their hands have done' — bi ma qaddamat aydihim. The same phrase used in Surah Ali 'Imran (3:182): what your hands sent ahead. The trouble they are in is the direct result of their own conduct.\n\nThen they come back — to the Prophet, the very authority they had refused — 'swearing by Allah'. Note that the oath is invoked only now, in self-defence. Allah's name enters their speech at the point where it is useful.\n\n'We intended nothing but goodwill and reconciliation' — in aradna illa ihsanan wa tawfiqa. This is the standard defence of the person caught out: I meant well. Nobody in the Quran who is caught in wrongdoing says 'I intended harm'.\n\nThe Quran allows the excuse to be stated in full and does not bother refuting it, because the next verse says simply that Allah knows what is in their hearts.\n\nThere is something worth examining here for any believer. Good intentions are genuinely important in Islam — deeds are judged by them. But intention is also the easiest thing in the world to claim after the fact, and a person can construct a sincere-feeling account of his own motives long after the decision was actually made for other reasons.\n\nThe test is not what you say you intended. It is what you chose when the choice was live.",
  "63": "'˹Only˺ Allah knows what is in their hearts. So turn away from them, caution them, and give them advice that will shake their very souls.' (Quran 4:63). The instruction on how to deal with the people just described, and it is more subtle than it first appears.\n\n'Allah knows what is in their hearts' — ya'lamu Allahu ma fi qulubihim. This settles the question raised by their excuse in 4:62 without argument. Their claim about their own intentions is neither accepted nor refuted; it is simply placed before the One who knows.\n\nThen three instructions, and they do not sit together comfortably at first glance.\n\n'Turn away from them' — fa a'rid 'anhum. Do not punish them, do not expose them, do not pursue the matter. Let it go.\n\n'Caution them' — wa 'izhum. But do warn them. Turning away is not the same as saying nothing.\n\n'Give them advice that will shake their very souls' — wa qul lahum fi anfusihim qawlan baligha. 'Baligh' means reaching, penetrating, arriving at its target. Speak to them privately — 'fi anfusihim', in their own selves — with words that land.\n\nThe combination is a complete method. Do not humiliate them publicly, do not take revenge, but do not pretend nothing happened either. Speak to them alone, and speak in a way that actually reaches them rather than merely discharging your duty.\n\nAnyone who has had to confront a friend or relative about something serious will recognise how demanding this is. Most people manage either the confrontation or the mercy. This verse requires both.",
  "64": "'We only sent messengers to be obeyed by Allah's Will. If only those ˹hypocrites˺ came to you ˹O Prophet˺—after wronging themselves—seeking Allah's forgiveness and the Messenger prayed for their forgiveness, they would have certainly found Allah ever Accepting of Repentance, Most Merciful.' (Quran 4:64). An open door for the people the surah has just condemned.\n\n'We only sent messengers to be obeyed' — li yuta'a bi idhni Allah. This states the purpose of prophethood in one clause. A messenger who is admired but not obeyed has not been received.\n\nThen the striking part. After describing men who claimed faith falsely, sought judgment elsewhere, turned their backs and swore false oaths, the Quran says: if they had only come.\n\n'After wronging themselves' — idh zalamu anfusahum. The Quran's habitual way of describing sin. Their conduct damaged the Prophet's authority and the community's order, but the verse names the primary victim as the sinner himself.\n\n'And the Messenger prayed for their forgiveness' — wa istaghfara lahumu al-rasul. Two things are required: their own seeking of forgiveness, and his prayer on their behalf. The man they had refused as a judge is offered as their advocate.\n\n'They would have certainly found Allah ever Accepting of Repentance, Most Merciful' — la wajadu Allaha tawwaban rahima. The same two names used in 4:16, again at the end of a passage about serious failure.\n\nThe pattern of this surah is now unmistakable. Every condemnation is followed by an opening. The Quran describes what people did, states the consequence, and then tells them how to get out.",
  "65": "'But no! By your Lord, they will never be ˹true˺ believers until they accept you ˹O Prophet˺ as the judge in their disputes, and find no resistance within themselves against your decision and submit wholeheartedly.' (Quran 4:65). An oath, and a definition of faith with three conditions.\n\n'By your Lord' — fa la wa rabbik. Allah swears by Himself. This construction appears rarely, and it marks what follows as decisive.\n\n'Abdullah ibn az-Zubayr reported that a man of the Ansar quarrelled with az-Zubayr before the Prophet about the irrigation canals of the Harrah. The Prophet said, \"Irrigate, O Zubayr, then let the water pass to your neighbour.\" The Ansari became angry and said, \"Is it because he is your aunt's son?\" The face of the Messenger of Allah changed colour, and he said, \"Irrigate, O Zubayr, then withhold the water until it reaches the walls.\" Az-Zubayr said: \"By Allah, I think this verse was revealed about that\" (Sahih al-Bukhari 2359, 2360).\n\nThree conditions are given, and each is deeper than the last.\n\nFirst, 'accept you as the judge' — yuhakkimuka. Bring the dispute to the correct authority. That is an action.\n\nSecond, 'find no resistance within themselves' — thumma la yajidu fi anfusihim harajan. No tightness, no grievance, no privately held objection. That is an inward state.\n\nThird, 'submit wholeheartedly' — wa yusallimu tasliman. Full acceptance.\n\nMost people can manage the first. The verse says faith is not complete until the third.",
  "66": "'If We had commanded them to sacrifice themselves or abandon their homes, none would have obeyed except for a few. Had they done what they were advised to do, it would have certainly been far better for them and more reassuring,' (Quran 4:66). A hypothetical, used to expose the smallness of what was actually asked.\n\n'To sacrifice themselves or abandon their homes' — an uqtulu anfusakum aw ukhruju min diyarikum. These were commands given to a previous community, and the Quran mentions them to draw a comparison. Nothing on that scale has been required here.\n\nWhat was required, in 4:65, was to accept a ruling without inward resentment. Compared to being told to leave your home or lay down your life, that is a small thing — and the verse observes that even so, most would have failed.\n\n'None would have obeyed except for a few' — illa qalilun minhum. The refrain of this surah and of Surah al-Baqarah before it. There is always a minority who do what was asked.\n\n'Had they done what they were advised to do' — ma yu'azuna bih, the counsel they were given. Note the word: advice, not merely orders. The instruction in 4:59-65 was for their benefit.\n\n'Far better for them and more reassuring' — khayran lahum wa ashadda tathbita. 'Tathbit' means being made firm, steadied, planted. Obedience is described as producing stability in the person who obeys.\n\nThat is the practical claim, and it is testable. People imagine that submitting to a ruling they dislike will leave them unsettled. The verse says the opposite: it is the resistance that unsettles, and the submission that steadies.",
  "67": "'and We would have granted them a great reward by Our grace' (Quran 4:67). The second consequence of the obedience described in 4:66, and the sentence continues from the previous verse.\n\n'A great reward' — ajran 'azima. The Quran uses 'azim — immense, mighty — sparingly, and here it is attached to the return on a decision that would have cost them very little.\n\n'By Our grace' — min ladunna, from Us, from Our own presence. This phrase indicates something given directly rather than earned through a process. It appeared in Surah Ali 'Imran in the prayer for guidance: mercy 'from Yourself'.\n\nThe theological point is the same one made at 4:40. The reward is not payment. Even when a person does exactly what was asked, the return is described as grace, because the ability to obey was itself a gift, and the scale of the reward bears no proportion to the size of the act.\n\nThat framing protects the believer from two errors at once. It prevents despair, since the reward does not depend on the impressiveness of your deeds. And it prevents entitlement, since nothing you do generates a claim on Allah.\n\nNotice also the tense running through 4:66-68: 'would have'. These are the outcomes they missed. The Quran is describing, in detail, a reward that was available and was not taken — which is a more effective warning than describing a punishment, because the reader feels the loss.",
  "68": "'and guided them to the Straight Path.' (Quran 4:68). The third and final consequence, and the shortest verse in this passage.\n\n'And guided them' — wa la hadaynahum siratan mustaqima. This is the outcome that repays reading twice, because of where it sits. It comes after the obedience, not before it.\n\nThe natural assumption is that guidance is what enables you to obey. The Quran presents it in the other order as well: obedience produces further guidance. Each act of submission clarifies the next step, and each refusal blurs it.\n\nThe same principle appears throughout the Quran. Guidance is not a single event that settles the matter permanently; it is a continuing supply that responds to what you do with what you already have.\n\n'The Straight Path' — al-sirat al-mustaqim. The same phrase every Muslim asks for seventeen times a day in Surah al-Fatihah. That request is answered partly through revelation and partly through the practice of acting on it.\n\nNote the sequence across these three verses: obedience (4:66), then reward (4:67), then guidance (4:68). The reward comes before the guidance in the ordering, which is unusual. It suggests that being guided further is itself the greater gift — the reward is what you receive, but the guidance is what you become.\n\nFor the hypocrites described in this passage, all three were within reach, and the obstacle was a single ruling they did not want to accept. That is usually the size of the obstacle.",
  "69": "'And whoever obeys Allah and the Messenger will be in the company of those blessed by Allah: the prophets, the people of truth, the martyrs, and the righteous—what honourable company!' (Quran 4:69). The reward for the obedience described in 4:65-68, and it is not what a reader expects.\n\nThe reward is company. Not a place, not a possession — the people you will be with.\n\nFour ranks are named, in descending order. 'The prophets' — al-nabiyyin, the highest. 'The people of truth' — al-siddiqin, from 'sidq', truthfulness; those whose belief and conduct match so completely that the word describes them. 'The martyrs' — al-shuhada', who gave their lives. 'And the righteous' — al-salihin, the ordinary sound and upright believers.\n\nThe condition attached to reaching this company is simply obedience to Allah and His Messenger. Not prophethood, not martyrdom, not extraordinary status. The verse promises proximity to the greatest people who ever lived on terms an ordinary Muslim can meet.\n\nCommentators including Ibn Kathir record that this verse was revealed after a Companion came to the Prophet distressed, saying that he loved him more than his own family, and that when he thought about the Hereafter he feared he would never see him again, since the Prophet would be raised to the highest ranks.\n\n'What honourable company' — wa hasuna ula'ika rafiqa. 'Rafiq' is the word for the friend who travels alongside you.\n\nThe verse is a reminder of something we already know from this life: who you are with determines almost everything about the experience.",
  "70": "'This is Allah's favour, and Allah fully knows ˹who deserves it˺.' (Quran 4:70). A single short sentence, closing the promise of 4:69.\n\n'This is Allah's favour' — dhalika al-fadlu mina Allah. After a verse describing the highest company a person could hope for, the Quran immediately identifies its source. It is fadl — grace, bounty, something given beyond what is owed.\n\nThis correction is necessary, because 4:69 could be read as a transaction: obey, and receive this. The Quran closes that reading. The obedience is real and required, but the reward is not proportionate payment. Nobody earns the company of prophets.\n\n'And Allah fully knows' — wa kafa billahi 'alima. Literally, 'Allah is sufficient as a Knower'. The construction 'kafa bi' appears yet again, and it does a specific job here. He knows who is sincere, who is pretending, and what each person's obedience actually cost them.\n\nThat matters in a passage that has spent many verses distinguishing believers from those who merely say they believe. Neither group can be sorted by observation. The verse says the sorting will be accurate, without saying how.\n\nThere is also comfort here for anyone whose religious effort is invisible. The person who quietly held to a ruling that cost him money, or who forgave privately, or who kept a duty no one saw — the knowledge of it is with the One whose knowledge is sufficient.\n\nTwo short clauses: everything good is from Him, and nothing true is hidden from Him.",
  "71": "'O believers! Take your precautions and go forth either in groups or together.' (Quran 4:71). The surah turns to the defence of the community, and its first word on the subject is about preparation.\n\n'Take your precautions' — khudhu hidhrakum. 'Hidhr' means caution, wariness, guard. It covers weapons, scouts, intelligence, planning and staying alert. The command comes before any instruction to fight.\n\nThat order is deliberate and it corrects a common religious error. Trust in Allah does not replace preparation; it accompanies it. The Quran gives the general command elsewhere: 'Prepare against them what you ˹believers˺ can of ˹military˺ power' (Quran 8:60).\n\n'Go forth either in groups or together' — thubatin aw infiru jami'a. 'Thubat' means separate detachments. The verse authorises both: advance in small units, or move as one body. Which is appropriate depends on the situation, and the Quran leaves the tactical judgment to the people on the ground.\n\nThis is worth noticing about Islamic law generally. On matters of principle it is precise; on matters of method it usually leaves room. The obligation is to act competently, not to follow a fixed template.\n\nThe verse also sets up everything that follows in 4:72-77, which describes those who do not go at all. Before the Quran criticises the reluctant, it establishes what was actually being asked: prepare properly, and move — in whatever formation the situation requires.",
  "72": "'There will be some among you who will lag behind so that if you face a disaster, they will say, \"Allah has blessed us for not being there among them.\"' (Quran 4:72). The first portrait of the person who stays behind.\n\n'Some among you' — wa inna minkum. Among you. The Quran does not place these people outside the community; it locates them inside it, which is exactly what makes them difficult to deal with.\n\n'Who will lag behind' — la yubatti'anna. The verb means to be slow, to hang back, to delay. Not open refusal — delay. He does not announce that he will not go. He is simply not ready, not well, not yet.\n\nThen the revealing part: what he says when the news is bad. 'Allah has blessed us for not being there among them' — qad an'ama Allahu 'alayya idh lam akun ma'ahum shahida.\n\nHe calls his absence a favour from Allah. This is religion used as self-congratulation, and it is done at the precise moment his brothers are being buried. Note the pronoun shift in the Arabic: he says 'blessed me', not us. Even his gratitude is private.\n\nCommentators including al-Tabari connect this to the aftermath of Uhud, where those who withdrew found their choice apparently vindicated by the outcome. Surah Ali 'Imran addressed the same claim at 3:168.\n\nThe behaviour is not confined to battle. Anyone who has watched others take a risk for something right, and then heard a bystander explain how wise he was to stay out of it, has seen this verse.",
  "73": "'But if you return with Allah's bounties, they will say—as if there had been no bond between you—\"We wish we had been there with them to share the great gain!\"' (Quran 4:73). The same man, when the news is good.\n\n'As if there had been no bond between you' — ka an lam takun baynakum wa baynahu mawaddah. This clause is the heart of the verse. 'Mawaddah' is affection, the warm attachment between people who belong to each other. The Quran notes that he speaks as though it never existed.\n\nAnd it is an acute observation. A moment ago he was thanking Allah for his separation from them. Now he wants to be counted among them. The relationship is not a fact about his life; it is a position he adopts or drops depending on the outcome.\n\n'We wish we had been there with them to share the great gain' — ya laytani kuntu ma'ahum fa afuza fawzan 'azima. Note the word he uses for what he missed: 'fawz', success, and the same word Surah Ali 'Imran used for salvation itself (Quran 3:185). He is talking about spoils.\n\nRead 4:72 and 4:73 together and the man is fully drawn. When there is loss, he is grateful he was elsewhere. When there is gain, he wishes he had been there. What is constant is that his own position is the only thing he ever measures.\n\nThe Quran does not call him a disbeliever. It just shows him to you, twice, and lets the two statements sit next to each other.",
  "74": "'Let those who would sacrifice this life for the Hereafter fight in the cause of Allah. And whoever fights in Allah's cause—whether they achieve martyrdom or victory—We will honour them with a great reward.' (Quran 4:74). After the portrait of the man who calculates, the description of the man who does not.\n\n'Those who would sacrifice this life for the Hereafter' — alladhina yashruna al-hayata al-dunya bi al-akhirah. 'Yashruna' means to sell. He sells the immediate for the lasting, which is precisely the transaction the hesitant man in 4:72-73 could not bring himself to make.\n\n'In the cause of Allah' — fi sabili Allah. This qualifier is not decoration. Fighting for tribe, revenge, plunder, land or reputation is excluded by it, and the Quran will make the conditions and limits explicit in the verses that follow.\n\n'Whether they achieve martyrdom or victory' — fa yuqtal aw yaghlib. Both outcomes are named, and both receive the same reward. That single clause dismantles the calculation the hesitant man was making, because he was weighing outcomes. Here the outcomes have been made equivalent.\n\nAnas ibn Malik reported that the Prophet, peace be upon him, said: \"Nobody who dies and finds good from Allah in the Hereafter would wish to come back to this world, even if he were given the whole world and whatever is in it — except the martyr, who, on seeing the superiority of martyrdom, would like to come back to the world and be killed again\" (Sahih al-Bukhari 2795).\n\n'A great reward' — ajran 'azima. The same phrase used in 4:67 of obedience. The reward attaches to the willingness, not to the result.",
  "75": "'And what is it with you? You do not fight in the cause of Allah and for oppressed men, women, and children who cry out, \"Our Lord! Deliver us from this land of oppressors! Appoint for us a saviour; appoint for us a helper—all by Your grace.\"' (Quran 4:75). The Quran states what the fighting is actually for.\n\n'And what is it with you?' — wa ma lakum. A direct question, almost an expression of disbelief. What is wrong with you that you would hesitate over this?\n\n'The oppressed' — al-mustad'afin. The form means those who have been made weak — not weak by nature, but rendered powerless by someone else. These were the Muslims left behind in Makkah after the migration: the poor, the enslaved, women and children who had no tribe to protect them and no means of escape.\n\nThen their prayer is quoted, and quoting it is the argument. 'Our Lord! Deliver us from this land of oppressors!' They are praying, and the verse is addressed to people who could be the answer.\n\nThat is the connection the Quran draws, and it is worth sitting with. Somewhere a person is asking Allah for rescue, and He has placed the means of that rescue in the hands of someone who is deciding whether it is convenient.\n\n'Appoint for us a saviour; appoint for us a helper' — waliyyan... nasira. The two words used of Allah Himself in 4:45. They ask Him for a protector, and He sends people.\n\nThe permission to fight in Islam is grounded here: 'Permission ˹to fight back˺ is ˹hereby˺ granted to those being fought, for they have been wronged' (Quran 22:39).",
  "76": "'Believers fight for the cause of Allah, whereas disbelievers fight for the cause of the Devil. So fight against Satan's ˹evil˺ forces. Indeed, Satan's schemes are ever weak.' (Quran 4:76). The two motives set side by side.\n\n'For the cause of Allah' — fi sabili Allah, against 'fi sabili al-taghut', the cause of false authority. Everyone who fights is fighting for something. The Quran's claim is that there are only two categories, whatever the stated reasons.\n\n'So fight against Satan's evil forces' — fa qatilu awliya'a al-shaytan. His allies, those who have taken him as their patron. The Quran keeps naming the real opponent behind the visible one.\n\nThen the closing line, which changes the whole tone: 'Indeed, Satan's schemes are ever weak' — inna kayda al-shaytani kana da'ifa.\n\nThat is a remarkable thing to say to people about to face a stronger army. Satan's plan is described as feeble. Not clever, not formidable — weak.\n\nWhy weak? Because it depends entirely on persuasion. He has no power to compel, only to suggest. His method is to make things look bigger than they are, and the entire apparatus collapses the moment a person declines to be impressed.\n\nThe same reassurance appeared in Surah Ali 'Imran, where fear itself was identified as the tool: 'That ˹warning˺ was only from Satan, trying to prevent you from supporting ˹the Prophet˺' (Quran 3:175).\n\nNote the word 'da'if' — weak. It is the same word used in 4:28 of human beings. We are weak, and so is he. The difference is that we have help.",
  "77": "'Have you ˹O Prophet˺ not seen those who had been told, \"Do not fight! Rather, establish prayer and pay alms-tax.\"? Then once the order came to fight, a group of them feared those ˹hostile˺ people as Allah should be feared—or even more...' (Quran 4:77). A striking observation about people who wanted to fight and then did not.\n\nThe sequence is the point. In Makkah, Muslims were persecuted and forbidden to fight back. Some of them asked for permission and were told to pray and pay zakat instead. Then, in Madinah, permission came — and the same men hesitated.\n\n'Feared those hostile people as Allah should be feared—or even more' — ka khashyati Allahi aw ashadda khashyah. The comparison is the sharpest thing in the verse. Fear of people had grown to occupy the place that fear of Allah should hold.\n\n'Our Lord! Why have You ordered us to fight? If only You had delayed the order for us for a little while!' They are not refusing. They are asking for a postponement — which is the more honest form of refusal, and the more common one.\n\nThe reply is about proportion. 'The enjoyment of this world is so little' — mata'u al-dunya qalil. The same two words used in Surah Ali 'Imran at 3:197 of the prosperity of the disbelievers. What the delay would buy them is small.\n\n'And none of you will be wronged even by the width of the thread of a date stone' — the 'fatil' again, as in 4:49. Whatever it costs will be counted exactly.\n\nThe pattern is familiar to everyone. What we want in principle, and what we want when it is finally required of us, are frequently not the same thing.",
  "78": "'Wherever you may be, death will overcome you—even if you were in fortified towers.\" When something good befalls them, they say, \"This is from Allah,\" but when something evil befalls them, they say, \"This is from you.\" Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ \"Both have been destined by Allah.\" So what is the matter with these people? They can hardly comprehend anything!' (Quran 4:78). The answer to the request for delay in 4:77.\n\n'Even if you were in fortified towers' — burujin mushayyadah, high built towers. The image is of the safest place a person of that era could imagine. Death reaches into it.\n\nThe argument is not that danger does not exist. It is that avoiding a duty does not extend your life, because the term was fixed regardless. Surah Ali 'Imran made the same point to those who withdrew at Uhud: 'Even if you were to remain in your homes, those among you who were destined to be killed would have met the same fate' (Quran 3:154).\n\nThen a second failing: 'When something good befalls them, they say, \"This is from Allah,\" but when something evil befalls them, they say, \"This is from you.\"' Good news is credited to Allah; bad news is blamed on the Prophet.\n\nThe Quran identifies this as an ancient habit: 'In times of prosperity, they said, \"This is what we deserve,\" but in adversity, they blamed it on Moses and those with him' (Quran 7:131).\n\n'Both have been destined by Allah' — kullun min 'indi Allah. Everything comes from Him.\n\n'They can hardly comprehend anything' — la yakaduna yafqahuna hadith. Not stupidity, but an unwillingness to think a matter through, because the conclusion is unwelcome.",
  "79": "'Whatever good befalls you is from Allah and whatever evil befalls you is from yourself. We have sent you ˹O Prophet˺ as a messenger to ˹all˺ people. And Allah is sufficient as a Witness.' (Quran 4:79). A verse that appears to contradict the one before it, and does not.\n\nVerse 4:78 said everything is from Allah. This verse says the good is from Allah and the evil is from yourself. Both are true, and they are answering different questions.\n\nVerse 4:78 addresses the question of power: nothing occurs outside Allah's will and decree, including hardship. Verse 4:79 addresses the question of responsibility: when something bad happens through your own wrongdoing, the fault is yours. Commentators including al-Tabari and Ibn Kathir explain the pair in exactly this way.\n\nThe distinction is practical rather than theoretical. The believer's stance is to attribute every blessing to Allah's generosity and every failing to himself — never the reverse. The people in 4:78 had it precisely backwards.\n\nThe Quran states the same principle elsewhere: 'Whatever affliction befalls you is because of what your own hands have committed. And He pardons much' (Quran 42:30).\n\n'We have sent you as a messenger to all people' — the responsibility of the Prophet is defined against the blame being laid on him in 4:78. He was sent to deliver, not to control outcomes.\n\n'And Allah is sufficient as a Witness' — kafa billahi shahida. The formula again. Whatever people say about who is at fault, there is a witness who saw the whole thing.",
  "80": "'Whoever obeys the Messenger has truly obeyed Allah. But whoever turns away, then ˹know that˺ We have not sent you ˹O Prophet˺ as a keeper over them.' (Quran 4:80). Two statements, and together they define the whole office of prophethood.\n\n'Whoever obeys the Messenger has truly obeyed Allah' — man yuti'i al-rasula fa qad ata'a Allah. This is the theological basis for the authority of the Sunnah. The Prophet's instruction is not a second source competing with the Quran; obedience to him is obedience to the One who sent him.\n\nAbu Hurayrah reported that the Prophet, peace be upon him, said: \"Whoever obeys me obeys Allah, and whoever disobeys me disobeys Allah\" (Sahih al-Bukhari 7137).\n\nThen the limit: 'We have not sent you as a keeper over them' — hafiza. A 'hafiz' is a guard, a custodian, someone responsible for keeping a thing safe. The Prophet is not that. He delivers; he does not enforce belief.\n\nThe Quran repeats this boundary often, because the temptation to overstep it is constant: 'But if they turn away, We have not sent you ˹O Prophet˺ as a keeper over them. Your duty is only to deliver ˹the message˺' (Quran 42:48), and 'You are not ˹there˺ to compel them ˹to believe˺' (Quran 88:22).\n\nAl-Musayyab reported that when Abu Talib was dying, the Prophet said to him, \"O my uncle, say: none has the right to be worshipped except Allah — a statement with which I will defend your case before Allah.\" But he died on the religion of 'Abd al-Muttalib (Sahih al-Bukhari 3884).\n\nIf the Prophet could not secure the faith of his own uncle, no one should carry the weight of another person's refusal.",
  "81": "'And they say, \"We obey,\" but when they leave you, a group of them would spend the night contradicting what they said. Allah records all their schemes. So turn away from them, and put your trust in Allah. And Allah is sufficient as a Trustee of Affairs.' (Quran 4:81). The gap between what is said in the room and what is said outside it.\n\n'They say, \"We obey\"' — wa yaquluna ta'ah. One word in Arabic: obedience. Said in the Prophet's presence, and it costs nothing.\n\n'When they leave you, a group of them would spend the night contradicting what they said' — bayyata ta'ifatun minhum ghayra alladhi taqul. The verb 'bayyata' means to do something at night, and it carries the sense of plotting under cover. They spend the hours of darkness rearranging what was agreed in daylight.\n\nNote the precision again: 'a group of them' — ta'ifatun minhum. Not all. The Quran never generalises where a subset is meant.\n\n'Allah records all their schemes' — wa Allahu yaktubu ma yubayyitun. He writes down what they arrange by night. Whatever the room, whatever the hour, there is a record.\n\nThen the instruction to the Prophet, and it is the same one given in 4:63: 'turn away from them'. Do not chase it, do not expose them, do not build your work around managing them.\n\n'And put your trust in Allah' — wa tawakkal 'ala Allah. Trust here is not passivity; it is what allows you to stop policing what you cannot see.\n\n'Sufficient as a Trustee' — wa kafa billahi wakila. The same word from 'Hasbuna Allahu wa ni'ma al-wakil' in Surah Ali 'Imran (Quran 3:173).",
  "82": "'Do they not then reflect on the Quran? Had it been from anyone other than Allah, they would have certainly found in it many inconsistencies.' (Quran 4:82). An argument the Quran makes about itself, and an invitation to test it.\n\n'Do they not then reflect' — a fa la yatadabbaruna al-Qur'an. 'Tadabbur' means to consider the outcome of a thing, to turn it over, to look at how the parts fit. It is not casual reading and it is not recitation. It is examination.\n\nThe claim is falsifiable, which is what makes it remarkable. A book revealed piece by piece over twenty-three years, to a man moving between persecution and power, poverty and rule, responding to battles, accusations, family crises and legal questions as they arose, with no opportunity to revise the earlier parts — such a book should be full of contradictions. The Quran invites the reader to find them.\n\n'Many inconsistencies' — ikhtilafan kathira. The word 'ikhtilaf' means difference, discrepancy, things that fail to line up.\n\nThe placement of this verse is deliberate. It follows a passage about people who said one thing publicly and another privately (4:81). Human speech drifts; it says what suits the moment. The Quran is presented as the opposite kind of speech — one whose earliest and latest portions agree.\n\nThe same challenge appears elsewhere: 'Do they not then reflect on the Quran? Or are there locks upon their hearts?' (Quran 47:24).\n\nThe practical instruction is in the first word. Reflect. A Muslim who has recited the Quran for decades without ever examining it has not yet done what this verse asks.",
  "83": "'And when they hear news of security or fear, they publicize it. Had they referred it to the Messenger or their authorities, those with sound judgment among them would have validated it. Had it not been for Allah's grace and mercy, you would have followed Satan—except for a few.' (Quran 4:83). A verse about how information moves through a community, and it reads as though written for the present day.\n\n'When they hear news of security or fear' — amri mina al-amni awi al-khawf. Reports that the danger has passed, or reports that an attack is coming. Both kinds are covered, because both cause damage when wrong.\n\n'They publicize it' — adha'u bih. They spread it. Not invent it — spread it. The Quran is not addressing liars here; it is addressing ordinary people who pass along what they have heard because it is interesting and because passing it on feels like being useful.\n\n'Had they referred it to the Messenger or their authorities' — la 'alimahu alladhina yastanbitunahu minhum. 'Istinbat' is the word for drawing water up from a deep well; it is used in Islamic scholarship for extracting a ruling from evidence. Some people are equipped to assess a report, and the verse says the correct move is to bring it to them first.\n\nThe Quran states the underlying rule directly: 'O believers, if an evildoer brings you any news, verify ˹it˺ so you do not harm people unknowingly' (Quran 49:6).\n\n'You would have followed Satan' — the consequence of unchecked rumour is named as following Shaytan, which is a striking way to describe forwarding a message.\n\nThe practical test before repeating anything: do I know this is true, and does repeating it help?",
  "84": "'So fight in the cause of Allah ˹O Prophet˺. You are accountable for none but yourself. And motivate the believers ˹to fight˺, so perhaps Allah will curb the disbelievers' might. And Allah is far superior in might and in punishment.' (Quran 4:84). An instruction given at a moment when the Prophet's support was uncertain.\n\n'You are accountable for none but yourself' — la tukallafu illa nafsak. This is the key sentence. After verses describing those who lag behind (4:72), those who ask for delay (4:77) and those who plot at night (4:81), the Prophet is told that their conduct is not his burden. He is responsible for his own obedience.\n\nCommentators including Ibn Kathir connect this to the expedition to Badr al-Sughra in the year after Uhud, when Abu Sufyan had promised a return engagement and many were reluctant. The Prophet declared he would go even if he went alone.\n\n'And motivate the believers' — wa harridi al-mu'minin. Encourage them, urge them. So he is not excused from calling others; he is only excused from being answerable for their response. Those are two different things, and people habitually confuse them.\n\n'So perhaps Allah will curb the disbelievers' might' — 'asa Allahu an yakuffa ba'sa alladhina kafaru. The outcome is expressed as a hope placed with Allah, not a prediction. He commands the action and leaves the result where it belongs.\n\nThis is the working principle for anyone who carries a responsibility others will not share: do your own part fully, call others sincerely, and stop measuring yourself by whether they come.",
  "85": "'Whoever intercedes for a good cause will have a share in the reward, and whoever intercedes for an evil cause will have a share in the burden. And Allah is Watchful over all things.' (Quran 4:85). A short verse with very wide application.\n\n'Intercedes' — shafa'ah. The root means to make something even, to pair up, to add yourself to another person's cause. It covers putting in a word for someone, connecting them to an opportunity, backing a proposal, or simply encouraging a course of action.\n\n'Will have a share in the reward' — yakun lahu nasibun minha. A share of it. Not a token acknowledgement — a genuine portion of whatever good results.\n\nAbu Hurayrah reported that the Prophet, peace be upon him, said: \"He who calls people to righteousness will have a reward like the rewards of those who follow it, without their rewards being diminished in any respect. And he who calls people to error will carry a burden of sin like the sins of those who follow it, without their sins being diminished in any respect\" (Sahih Muslim 2674).\n\nThe symmetry is the warning. The same mechanism that credits you for good you helped set in motion charges you for harm you helped enable. The man who recommended a corrupt scheme, introduced two people who ruined each other, or lent his reputation to something false holds a share of it.\n\n'Allah is Watchful over all things' — muqita. A rare name, understood by commentators to mean the One who has power over everything and provides for it, and who watches and preserves.\n\nInfluence is never neutral. Every recommendation you make is entered somewhere.",
  "86": "'And when you are greeted, respond with a better greeting or at least similarly. Surely Allah is a ˹vigilant˺ Reckoner of all things.' (Quran 4:86). A rule of manners, given the same legal weight as everything around it.\n\n'When you are greeted' — idha huyyitum bi tahiyyah. The greeting in question is 'as-salamu 'alaykum', peace be upon you. The response should be better, or at least equal.\n\nThat is how the familiar exchange works. 'As-salamu 'alaykum' is answered with 'wa 'alaykum as-salam wa rahmatullah' — and the mercy of Allah — which is better. Add 'wa barakatuh' and it is better again. The Quran builds an upward pressure into the smallest social act.\n\n'Or at least similarly' — aw rudduha. The floor is equality. Returning less than you were given is not permitted.\n\nConsider where this verse sits. It comes in the middle of a passage about war, hypocrisy, rumour and killing. A rule about saying salam properly has been placed there deliberately. Islam does not treat courtesy as a lesser category of religion that can be suspended when serious matters are at hand.\n\n'Surely Allah is a Reckoner of all things' — hasiban. He counts. Even this.\n\nThe greeting itself is worth thinking about. You are not saying hello; you are praying for the person's safety and peace. Muslims exchange supplications dozens of times a day and often forget they are doing it.\n\nAnswering a greeting warmly costs nothing and is recorded. Ignoring one is also recorded.",
  "87": "'Allah, there is no god ˹worthy of worship˺ except Him. He will certainly gather ˹all of˺ you together on the Day of Judgment—about which there is no doubt. And whose word is more truthful than Allah's?' (Quran 4:87). A statement of the two foundations, placed as a pivot in the surah.\n\n'There is no god except Him' — la ilaha illa huwa. The first foundation: tawhid.\n\n'He will certainly gather you together on the Day of Judgment' — la yajma'annakum. The second: resurrection and accounting. These two beliefs carry all the rest, which is why a chapter full of detailed law pauses here to restate them.\n\n'About which there is no doubt' — la rayba fih. The same phrase used of the Book itself at the opening of Surah al-Baqarah.\n\n'And whose word is more truthful than Allah's?' — wa man asdaqu mina Allahi hadith. A question with only one answer. And note the word 'hadith' — speech, a report. It was used in 4:78 of the people who 'can hardly comprehend anything', and in 4:42 of what cannot be hidden from Allah.\n\nThe placement matters. The surah has just discussed rumours passing through the community (4:83), oaths sworn falsely (4:62), and men saying one thing and doing another (4:81). Speech everywhere is unreliable. Then this: whose speech is more truthful than Allah's?\n\nEverything the surah asks — divide inheritance justly, return trusts, accept rulings, verify news — depends on trusting the source. The verse names why the source can be trusted.",
  "88": "'Why are you ˹believers˺ divided into two groups regarding the hypocrites while Allah allowed them to regress ˹to disbelief˺ because of their misdeeds? Do you wish to guide those left by Allah to stray? And whoever Allah leaves to stray, you will never find for them a way.' (Quran 4:88). The community was arguing about what to do with a particular group, and the Quran settles it.\n\n'Divided into two groups' — fi'atayn. Commentators including Ibn Kathir report the background: a number of people had professed Islam and then left Madinah, returning to the hostile camp. Some Muslims argued they were still believers who should be protected; others said they had defected. The disagreement was sincere on both sides.\n\n'Allah allowed them to regress because of their misdeeds' — Allahu arkasahum bi ma kasabu. 'Arkasa' means to turn something upside down, to send it back head-first. Note the cause given: 'bi ma kasabu', because of what they earned. Their reversal followed their own conduct.\n\n'Do you wish to guide those left by Allah to stray?' — a question asked of well-meaning people. Sympathy is not being condemned, but the verse points out that the wish to save everyone is not always in a person's power.\n\nThis connects directly to 4:80: the Prophet is not a keeper over them. And to the case of Abu Talib, whom the Prophet loved and could not guide (Sahih al-Bukhari 3884).\n\nThe lesson is difficult. There is a point at which continuing to make excuses for someone who has clearly chosen a direction stops being mercy and becomes a refusal to see. Wanting the best for people does not entitle us to misread them.",
  "89": "'They wish you would disbelieve as they have disbelieved, so you may all be alike. So do not take them as allies unless they emigrate in the cause of Allah. But if they turn away, then seize them and kill them wherever you find them, and do not take any of them as allies or helpers,' (Quran 4:89). The ruling on the group disputed in 4:88, and it must be read with 4:90 which immediately restricts it.\n\n'They wish you would disbelieve... so you may all be alike' — the motive named in 4:27 and 4:44 returns. What is wanted is not your argument but your company in abandonment.\n\n'Do not take them as allies' — awliya'. Not 'do not deal with them', not 'do not be just toward them'. The prohibition is on alliance: making them your inner circle, your source of protection, your side in a conflict. This is the same restriction Surah Ali 'Imran placed at 3:118 with the word 'bitanah'.\n\n'Unless they emigrate in the cause of Allah' — the door back is open, and the test is action rather than words. These were people who had claimed Islam and then returned to the enemy camp; migration would prove the claim.\n\n'Then seize them and kill them wherever you find them' — this is the sentence that gets quoted without its context and without the verse that follows it. Its subject is a specific group of active combatants who had defected and joined a force at war with Madinah.\n\nVerse 4:90 then removes from this ruling everyone bound by treaty and everyone who declines to fight. Verse 4:94 forbids assuming anyone is an enemy. The exceptions are not marginal; they define the scope.",
  "90": "'except those who are allies of a people you are bound with in a treaty or those wholeheartedly opposed to fighting either you or their own people. If Allah had willed, He would have empowered them to fight you. So if they refrain from fighting you and offer you peace, then Allah does not permit you to harm them.' (Quran 4:90). The exceptions that define the previous verse.\n\nTwo categories are exempted. First, anyone connected to a people with whom the Muslims have a treaty. A treaty covers not only the signatories but those under their protection, and the Muslims are bound by it even when it is inconvenient.\n\nSecond, 'those wholeheartedly opposed to fighting either you or their own people' — hasirat suduruhum, whose chests are constricted at the thought. Neutrals. People who want no part of the conflict on either side. They are protected, and the verse goes further by noting that Allah could have made them combatants and did not.\n\nThen the general rule: 'if they refrain from fighting you and offer you peace, then Allah does not permit you to harm them' — fa ma ja'ala Allahu lakum 'alayhim sabila. Allah has given you no way against them. No route, no pretext, no argument.\n\nThat clause is decisive for reading 4:89. The condition for fighting is that they are fighting you. Remove the hostility and the permission disappears entirely.\n\nThe Quran states the same principle elsewhere: 'If the enemy is inclined towards peace, make peace with them. And put your trust in Allah' (Quran 8:61). And on those who never fought you: 'Allah does not forbid you from dealing kindly and fairly with those who have neither fought nor driven you out of your homes. Surely Allah loves those who are fair' (Quran 60:8).",
  "91": "'You will find others who wish to be safe from you and their own people. Yet they cannot resist the temptation ˹of disbelief or hostility˺. If they do not keep away, offer you peace, or refrain from attacking you, then seize them and kill them wherever you find them. We have given you full permission over such people.' (Quran 4:91). A third category, distinct from the two in 4:90.\n\nThese are not neutrals. They are people who want the appearance of safety with both sides while remaining available to attack. They come to Madinah professing peace, return to their people and take part in hostilities, and then come back again.\n\n'They cannot resist the temptation' — kullama ruddu ila al-fitnati urkisu fiha. Every time they are returned to it, they are plunged back in. 'Urkisu' is the same verb used in 4:88.\n\nThe verse then sets out three conditions, and all three must fail before any action is permitted: if they do not withdraw from you, and do not offer you peace, and do not restrain their hands. A person who does any one of these is outside the ruling.\n\nThat is a careful construction, and it is the opposite of a general licence. The Quran keeps narrowing until only the actively and repeatedly hostile remain.\n\n'We have given you full permission over such people' — sultanan mubina, a clear authority. The word 'mubin' matters: the authority is clear precisely because the conditions were spelled out. There is no vague permission in this passage.\n\nThe pattern across 4:89-91 is consistent. Fighting is permitted against those who fight, prohibited against those who do not, and the burden of establishing which is which falls on the Muslims — as 4:94 will make explicit.",
  "92": "'It is not lawful for a believer to kill another except by mistake. And whoever kills a believer unintentionally must free a believing slave and pay blood-money to the victim's family—unless they waive it charitably...' (Quran 4:92). Accidental killing, and the law is remarkably detailed.\n\n'Except by mistake' — illa khata'a. The phrasing assumes the deliberate case is unthinkable; only the accident needs legislating.\n\nTwo obligations follow, and they address different injuries. 'Free a believing slave' — tahriru raqabatin mu'minah. A life was ended, so a life is restored to freedom. Islam repeatedly used emancipation as expiation, which steadily drained the institution of slavery (see also Quran 58:3).\n\n'Pay blood-money to the victim's family' — diyatun musallamatun ila ahlih. The family has lost income and support, and compensation is owed regardless of intent.\n\n'Unless they waive it charitably' — illa an yassaddaqu. They may forgive the payment, and the Quran calls that an act of charity.\n\nThen the variations. If the victim was a believer among a hostile people, only the emancipation is due, since paying money to that community would fund the opposing side. If from a people bound by treaty, both are due — the treaty is honoured even though the deceased was not a Muslim.\n\n'Those who are unable, let them fast two consecutive months' — a route for the poor, so that expiation is never out of reach.\n\nNothing here depends on whether anyone found out. It is 'a means of repentance to Allah' — tawbatan mina Allah.",
  "93": "'And whoever kills a believer intentionally, their reward will be Hell—where they will stay indefinitely. Allah will be displeased with them, condemn them, and will prepare for them a tremendous punishment.' (Quran 4:93). The deliberate case, and the language is as severe as anything in the Quran.\n\nFour consequences are stacked: Hell, permanence, Allah's anger, and His curse. Commentators note that no other sin in the Quran receives this particular accumulation.\n\n'Their reward will be Hell' — fa jaza'uhu jahannam. The word 'jaza' means recompense, what the deed earns.\n\n'Where they will stay indefinitely' — khalidan fiha. Scholars have discussed this at length, since 4:48 established that everything short of shirk falls under possible forgiveness. Many, including Ibn Kathir, hold that this describes what the act deserves and applies in full to one who considers it lawful, while a believer who commits it remains under Allah's will, as 4:48 states.\n\nThat discussion does not soften the verse. It was revealed to make murder unthinkable, and the reader is not meant to leave it looking for the exception.\n\n'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, said: \"The blood of a Muslim who testifies that none has the right to be worshipped but Allah and that I am His Messenger cannot lawfully be shed except in three cases: a life for a life, a married person who commits adultery, and one who abandons his religion and leaves the community\" (Sahih al-Bukhari 6878).\n\nThe Quran's general statement stands behind all of this: 'whoever takes a life... it will be as if they killed all of humanity' (Quran 5:32).",
  "94": "'O believers! When you struggle in the cause of Allah, be sure of who you fight. And do not say to those who offer you ˹greetings of˺ peace, \"You are no believer!\"—seeking a fleeting worldly gain... You were initially like them then Allah blessed you ˹with Islam˺. So be sure! Indeed, Allah is All-Aware of what you do.' (Quran 4:94). The safeguard on everything in 4:89-91.\n\n'Be sure' — fa tabayyanu. Establish the facts. The command is repeated at the end of the verse, which is unusual and deliberate.\n\n'Do not say to those who offer you greetings of peace, \"You are no believer\"' — the situation is precise. A man in a combat zone says salam or declares his faith, and the fighters must accept it.\n\nUsamah ibn Zayd reported: The Messenger of Allah sent us to al-Huruqah. We attacked them in the morning and defeated them. An Ansari man and I caught up with one of them, and when we closed on him he said, \"La ilaha illa Allah.\" The Ansari held back, but I stabbed him with my spear and killed him. When we returned, the Prophet came to know of it and said, \"O Usamah! Did you kill him after he said 'La ilaha illa Allah'?\" I said, \"He said it only to save himself.\" He kept repeating it until I wished I had not become Muslim before that day (Sahih al-Bukhari 4269).\n\n'Seeking a fleeting worldly gain' — the motive exposed. His possessions would be spoils if he were an enemy.\n\n'You were initially like them then Allah blessed you' — the sentence that should end all religious arrogance. Every Muslim was once outside, and was let in by a favour.",
  "95": "'Those who stay at home—except those with valid excuses—are not equal to those who strive in the cause of Allah with their wealth and their lives...' (Quran 4:95). A ranking, and the story of how one clause entered it.\n\nAl-Bara' reported: When the verse 'Not equal are those of the believers who sit at home' was revealed, the Messenger of Allah called for Zayd ibn Thabit, who came with a shoulder-blade and wrote it. Then Ibn Umm Maktum complained of his blindness, and the revelation came: 'except those who are disabled' (Sahih al-Bukhari 2831).\n\nA blind man objected that he could not go, and the verse was amended before it was finished. The exception was not an afterthought added by scholars; it was revealed on the spot, in response to one man's genuine grievance.\n\n'Except those with valid excuses' — ghayru uli al-darar. Those with a disability or harm preventing them. They are removed from the comparison entirely.\n\n'With their wealth and their lives' — bi amwalihim wa anfusihim. Wealth is named first, and it is named consistently throughout the Quran. Funding the effort is part of it, which means the person who cannot go is not thereby excluded from participating.\n\n'Allah has promised each a fine reward' — wa kullan wa'ada Allahu al-husna. This clause protects those who stayed with legitimate reason. They are not condemned; they are simply not equal, and both facts are stated.\n\nThe verse establishes a hierarchy of effort without turning it into a hierarchy of worth.",
  "96": "'far superior ranks, forgiveness, and mercy from Him. And Allah is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.' (Quran 4:96). The completion of 4:95, specifying what the higher reward consists of.\n\nThree things are named.\n\n'Far superior ranks' — darajatin minh. Degrees, levels, from Him. The word 'darajat' has appeared in this surah's discussion of reward and in Surah Ali 'Imran at 3:163, where believers were described as being at varying ranks with Allah.\n\n'Forgiveness' — maghfirah. This is worth pausing on. The reward for striving includes being forgiven, which tells you something about the relationship between effort and sin. A person who exposes himself to hardship in Allah's cause is not thereby made sinless; he is given forgiveness as part of the reward.\n\n'And mercy' — rahmah. Not merely the erasure of what was wrong, but active kindness beyond it.\n\n'And Allah is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful' — Ghafuran Rahima. The verse ends by naming Allah with the two attributes it has just promised. What is given to the striver is a share in what Allah is.\n\nNotice how this closes the passage that began at 4:71 with 'take your precautions'. Everything between has been about who goes, who stays, who hesitates, who calculates, and who is genuinely excused. The passage ends not with a verdict on the reluctant but with the two most merciful names.\n\nThat ordering is characteristic of this surah. It states the standard clearly, and then it reminds you who is judging.",
  "97": "'When the angels seize the souls of those who have wronged themselves—scolding them, \"What do you think you were doing?\" they will reply, \"We were oppressed in the land.\" The angels will respond, \"Was Allah's earth not spacious enough for you to emigrate?\" It is they who will have Hell as their home—what an evil destination!' (Quran 4:97). A conversation at the moment of death, and it turns on a single excuse.\n\nThe people described are Muslims who stayed in Makkah after the migration was possible, continued to live under a system that prevented them from practising their faith, and in some cases were compelled to march out with the Quraysh at Badr.\n\n'What do you think you were doing?' — fima kuntum. Literally: in what were you? What state were you in? What were you doing with your life?\n\n'We were oppressed in the land' — kunna mustad'afina fi al-ard. The same word used in 4:75 of the genuinely helpless. Here it is claimed by people who could have left and did not.\n\nThe angels' reply is the whole verse: 'Was Allah's earth not spacious enough for you to emigrate?' The excuse is not rejected as a lie. It is answered with a question about whether they had an alternative — and they did.\n\nThe Quran states the principle plainly: 'O My believing servants! My earth is truly spacious, so worship Me ˹alone˺' (Quran 29:56).\n\nThe application is not confined to seventh-century Makkah. Anyone in a situation that steadily erodes their faith — a job, a circle, a place — faces the same question. Not 'was it difficult?' but 'was there a way out that you did not take?'",
  "98": "'Except helpless men, women, and children who cannot afford a way out—' (Quran 4:98). The exception, and it arrives immediately.\n\nThe Quran does not let the severity of 4:97 stand for even one verse without qualification. That is a pattern worth noticing throughout this surah.\n\n'Helpless' — al-mustad'afin. The same word the excuse-makers used in 4:97. Here it is applied to those for whom it is true, which shows the verse before was not disputing the category but the claim to belong to it.\n\nThree groups are named — men, women and children — and the condition applies to all: 'who cannot afford a way out' — la yastati'una hilatan wa la yahtaduna sabila. They have no means, and they know no route.\n\nTwo separate obstacles are described. 'Hilah' is a way of contriving something, the resources or method to arrange it. 'Sabil' is the road itself. A person might have money but no safe route; another might know the road but have no means to travel it. Either is sufficient.\n\nCommentators including Ibn Kathir record that this covered the poor, the elderly, the sick, women without protection and children — people for whom crossing the desert to Madinah was genuinely impossible.\n\nThe test is capability, and the Quran applies it with care. The general rule was stated in 4:28: Allah wills to lighten your burdens. Here that principle is applied to a specific and demanding obligation.",
  "99": "'it is right to hope that Allah will pardon them. For Allah is Ever-Pardoning, All-Forgiving.' (Quran 4:99). The verdict on those described in 4:98.\n\n'It is right to hope' — fa ula'ika 'asa Allahu an ya'fuwa 'anhum. The word ''asa' means 'perhaps', and commentators including al-Tabari note that when it comes from Allah in the Quran it signifies a certainty expressed gently.\n\nWhy express it gently? Because the verse concerns people who failed to fulfil an obligation, even with good reason. The Quran does not want anyone reading it to conclude that inability makes an obligation disappear. So it phrases the pardon as hope rather than entitlement — which keeps the person who is genuinely stuck in a posture of asking rather than assuming.\n\n'Ever-Pardoning, All-Forgiving' — 'Afuwwan Ghafura. Two names, and the difference between them is worth knowing. 'Ghafur' comes from covering: the sin is concealed and not held against you. ''Afuww' comes from erasure: the trace is removed altogether, as though it had not happened.\n\nPlacing ''Afuww' first is deliberate. For people who could not do what was asked, the record is not merely covered — it is wiped.\n\nRead 4:97-99 together and the structure is complete. A warning to those who use hardship as cover, an exception for those genuinely unable, and a pardon for the second group. The Quran distinguishes between the two with great care, because human beings are very bad at making that distinction about themselves.",
  "100": "'Whoever emigrates in the cause of Allah will find many safe havens and bountiful resources throughout the earth. Those who leave their homes and die while emigrating to Allah and His Messenger—their reward has already been secured with Allah. And Allah is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.' (Quran 4:100). The promise that answers the fear behind staying.\n\n'Will find many safe havens' — muraghaman kathiran. The word 'muragham' comes from a root meaning to rub someone's nose in the dust — to leave in a way that frustrates those who tried to hold you. Commentators including al-Tabari also give the sense of a place of refuge. Both meanings work: you will find somewhere to go, and your going will defeat those who wanted you trapped.\n\n'And bountiful resources' — wa sa'ah. Room, abundance, breadth. This addresses the practical fear: how will I live? Leaving meant abandoning property, trade and the protection of a clan.\n\nThen the deepest fear is addressed: what if I die on the way? 'Those who leave their homes and die while emigrating to Allah and His Messenger—their reward has already been secured with Allah' — fa qad waqa'a ajruhu 'ala Allah. The reward has fallen due, become binding upon Allah.\n\nThe point is that the reward attaches to setting out, not to arriving. The journey does not have to succeed.\n\nCommentators including Ibn Kathir record the case of a man who set out for Madinah from Makkah and died on the road, and that this verse was revealed concerning him.\n\nThe principle is general: Allah rewards the decision and the effort, and outcomes are not the believer's responsibility.",
  "101": "'When you travel through the land, it is permissible for you to shorten the prayer—˹especially˺ if you fear an attack by the disbelievers. Indeed, the disbelievers are your sworn enemies.' (Quran 4:101). A concession in worship, and how the Companions understood it.\n\nYa'la ibn Umayyah said: I said to 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, \"Allah says, 'You may shorten the prayer if you fear that those who disbelieve may afflict you,' yet the people are now safe.\" He replied: \"I wondered about that as you wonder, so I asked the Messenger of Allah about it, and he said: 'It is a charity which Allah has given you, so accept His charity'\" (Sahih Muslim 686a).\n\nThat exchange settles the matter. The mention of fear describes the circumstance in which the verse came down, not a condition limiting the concession. Shortening the prayer on a journey remains available in safety.\n\nAnd note the word the Prophet used: sadaqah. Charity. A gift from Allah, and the instruction is to accept it. Refusing a concession out of a desire to do more is not piety; it is declining something offered.\n\n'It is permissible for you' — laysa 'alaykum junah, there is no blame on you. The Quran phrases it as the removal of an obstacle rather than as a command, which is why some early scholars discussed whether shortening is obligatory or preferred.\n\nThe wider principle governs much of this surah: 'It is Allah's Will to lighten your burdens' (Quran 4:28). Religion tightens where principle is at stake and loosens where circumstances are hard.",
  "102": "'When you ˹O Prophet˺ are ˹campaigning˺ with them and you lead them in prayer, let one group of them pray with you—while armed. When they prostrate themselves, let the other group stand guard behind them...' (Quran 4:102). The prayer of fear, described in operational detail.\n\nRead what is happening. An enemy force is nearby, an attack is possible at any moment, and the Quran's instruction is not to postpone the prayer. It is to reorganise the battlefield around it.\n\nThe congregation splits. One group prays with the Prophet while the other stands watch, weapons in hand. Then they swap. The prayer is shortened and modified, but it remains a congregation, and it remains on time.\n\nThat tells you what the prayer is worth in Islam. If it can be maintained under those conditions, the ordinary excuses — a meeting, a commute, tiredness — collapse.\n\n'The disbelievers would wish to see you neglect your weapons and belongings, so they could launch a sweeping assault' — the reasoning is given openly. Precaution is not a failure of trust; it is part of the instruction.\n\n'But there is no blame if you lay aside your weapons when overcome by heavy rain or illness—but take precaution.' Even inside the emergency arrangement there is a further concession, and even inside the concession there is a reminder to stay careful.\n\n'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud asked the Prophet, peace be upon him, \"Which deed is dearest to Allah?\" He said, \"The prayer at its proper time.\" He asked, \"Then what?\" He said, \"Kindness to parents.\" He asked, \"Then what?\" He said, \"Jihad in the cause of Allah\" (Sahih al-Bukhari 527).",
  "103": "'When the prayers are over, remember Allah—whether you are standing, sitting, or lying down. But when you are secure, establish regular prayers. Indeed, performing prayers is a duty on the believers at the appointed times.' (Quran 4:103). The conclusion of the prayer-of-fear passage, and the source of one of the most quoted statements about salah.\n\n'Remember Allah—whether you are standing, sitting, or lying down' — qiyaman wa qu'udan wa 'ala junubikum. The identical phrase used in Surah Ali 'Imran of the people of reason (Quran 3:191). There it described contemplation of creation; here it describes what a soldier does between engagements. Remembrance fits into any posture and any situation.\n\n'When you are secure, establish regular prayers' — the concession ends when the danger ends. Emergency provisions are not to be carried into ordinary life, which is a discipline in itself. People are quick to adopt a dispensation and slow to give it up.\n\n'Indeed, performing prayers is a duty on the believers at the appointed times' — inna al-salata kanat 'ala al-mu'minina kitaban mawquta. 'Kitab' means something written and prescribed. 'Mawqut' means fixed to specified times.\n\nThat closing sentence is the legal foundation for the five daily prayers being tied to particular hours rather than performed at convenience.\n\nAnd notice where it appears: at the end of a passage about praying in combat. The Quran establishes the strictness of the timing precisely after demonstrating the flexibility of the form. The shape can bend — the schedule does not.\n\nFor a person struggling with prayer, the order of these verses is the whole lesson. It was never expected to be easy. It was expected to be done.",
  "104": "'Do not falter in pursuit of the enemy—if you are suffering, they too are suffering. But you can hope to receive from Allah what they can never hope for. And Allah is All-Knowing, All-Wise.' (Quran 4:104). A single argument about endurance, and it is psychological rather than military.\n\n'Do not falter' — wa la tahinu. The same verb used in Surah Ali 'Imran after Uhud: 'Do not falter or grieve, for you will have the upper hand' (Quran 3:139).\n\n'If you are suffering, they too are suffering' — in takunu ta'lamuna fa innahum ya'lamuna kama ta'lamun. The word means to be in pain. This is the observation that changes everything about endurance.\n\nWhen you are exhausted, the enemy appears fresh. Their fatigue is invisible to you; yours is the only fatigue you can feel. So a person concludes that he is uniquely at the end of his strength, and gives up while the other side is equally close to breaking.\n\n'But you can hope to receive from Allah what they can never hope for' — wa tarjuna mina Allahi ma la yarjun. Here is the asymmetry. The pain is shared; the hope is not. They endure for a result in this world only, and if it fails, they have nothing. You endure for something guaranteed regardless of the outcome, as 4:74 established.\n\nThe application extends well beyond a battlefield. Anyone holding out under strain — in study, in illness, in a difficult obligation — is prone to the same error of thinking their difficulty is exceptional. It usually is not. What differs is what you are holding out for.",
  "105": "'Indeed, We have sent down the Book to you ˹O Prophet˺ in truth to judge between people by means of what Allah has shown you. So do not be an advocate for the deceitful.' (Quran 4:105). The beginning of a passage revealed about a real case, and the case is extraordinary.\n\nCommentators including al-Tabari and Ibn Kathir record the incident. A man of Madinah stole a piece of armour and hid it in the house of a Jewish man. When the theft was discovered, the thief's clan came to the Prophet, argued the innocent man was guilty, and pressed the Prophet to rule accordingly. He was inclined to accept their account — until these verses came down.\n\nRead what that means. A Muslim clan attempted to use the Prophet's court to frame a non-Muslim, and Allah intervened in revelation to stop it.\n\n'To judge between people by means of what Allah has shown you' — bi ma araka Allah. The standard is what Allah has shown, not what the parties assert.\n\n'So do not be an advocate for the deceitful' — wa la takun lil kha'inina khasima. A 'khasim' is an opponent in litigation, someone arguing a side. The Prophet is told not to become counsel for the party that is lying, even though they were his own community.\n\nThe principle is stated elsewhere as a command to all believers: 'Do not let the hatred of a people lead you to injustice. Be just! That is closer to righteousness' (Quran 5:8). Here the inverse is legislated: do not let affection for a people lead you to injustice either.",
  "106": "'And seek Allah's forgiveness—indeed, Allah is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.' (Quran 4:106). A short verse addressed to the Prophet, following the case in 4:105.\n\nThe instruction to seek forgiveness comes after he was inclined to accept a false account before the truth was revealed. Nothing had yet been decided, and no injustice had been carried out. Still, the verse comes.\n\nThat sets an extraordinarily high standard. The Prophet was working from the evidence presented to him and from his natural trust in the community around him. He was corrected before any harm occurred, and told to seek forgiveness even so.\n\nFor everyone else, the lesson is about what our own near-misses deserve. Most people who almost wronged someone, and were stopped by circumstance, feel relief rather than repentance. This verse suggests the opposite response.\n\n'Indeed, Allah is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful' — Ghafuran Rahima. The pair that closes many verses in this surah. Every time the standard is raised, the same two names appear immediately after, which is the Quran's way of keeping severity and mercy in the same sentence.\n\nThere is also a practical point about istighfar. Seeking forgiveness is not reserved for the aftermath of clear sin. It is a habit — for what we got wrong, for what we nearly got wrong, and for what we do not know we got wrong.\n\nThe Prophet, whose past and future faults were forgiven, was still commanded to ask.",
  "107": "'Do not advocate for those who wrong themselves. Surely Allah does not like those who are deceitful, sinful.' (Quran 4:107). The instruction from 4:105 restated, with the guilty party named more precisely.\n\n'Those who wrong themselves' — alladhina yakhtanuna anfusahum. The verb is from 'khiyanah', betrayal. Literally: those who betray themselves. It is a striking way to describe men who framed an innocent person.\n\nThe Quran keeps insisting on this. In 4:64, sinners 'wronged themselves'. In 4:110, whoever 'wrongs themselves' may seek forgiveness. Whatever damage a wrongdoer does to others, the Quran identifies the primary casualty as the wrongdoer. The Jewish man in this case might have lost a case; the clan defending the thief lost something they will not recover so easily.\n\n'Allah does not like those who are deceitful, sinful' — khawwanan athima. Both words are intensive forms in Arabic: a habitual betrayer, a persistent sinner. The grammar describes a settled character rather than a single act, which fits a group willing to organise a conspiracy and take it to court.\n\nThe prohibition — 'do not advocate for them' — is worth applying widely. Defending a relative you know is in the wrong, providing cover for a colleague's dishonesty, or arguing a case for your own side while privately aware of the facts all fall inside it.\n\nBeing asked to help is not the same as being obliged to help. Loyalty that requires you to argue for something false has stopped being loyalty and become participation.",
  "108": "'They try to hide ˹their deception˺ from people, but they can never hide it from Allah—in Whose presence they plot by night what is displeasing to Him. And Allah is Fully Aware of what they do.' (Quran 4:108). The observation that undoes the whole scheme.\n\n'They try to hide from people, but they can never hide from Allah' — yastakhfuna mina al-nasi wa la yastakhfuna mina Allah. The two clauses use the same verb, and the contrast is the argument. They took enormous care about one audience and none at all about the other.\n\n'In Whose presence they plot by night' — wa huwa ma'ahum idh yubayyituna. 'Yubayyitun' is the same verb used in 4:81 of those who spend the night contradicting what they said. And 'ma'ahum' — He is with them. Not watching from a distance. Present in the room while the plan is made.\n\nThat image is the point. The people arranging this conspiracy were careful to meet privately. The verse describes the meeting as attended.\n\n'What is displeasing to Him' — ma la yarda mina al-qawl. Speech He does not approve of. Note that the sin named is the talk itself, not only the plan's execution.\n\nThe practical consequence is simple and hard. Anything you would not do in front of someone you respect, you are already doing in front of Allah. The privacy people rely on when they arrange something shameful does not exist.\n\n'And Allah is Fully Aware of what they do' — muhitan, encompassing. He surrounds it entirely.",
  "109": "'Here you are! You ˹believers˺ are advocating for them in this life, but who will ˹dare to˺ advocate for them before Allah on the Day of Judgment? Or who will come to their defence?' (Quran 4:109). The verse turns from the guilty to those who supported them.\n\n'Here you are' — ha antum ha'ula'. An Arabic expression of pointed address, close to 'look at you'. The tone is not gentle.\n\n'You are advocating for them in this life' — jadaltum 'anhum fi al-hayati al-dunya. 'Jidal' is argument, disputation. These were people who took up the case of a thief because he was one of theirs, and they argued it in good faith as far as their own conscience was concerned. Family and clan loyalty felt like a virtue.\n\n'But who will advocate for them before Allah on the Day of Judgment?' The question exposes the limit of what their support was worth. It can win an argument in Madinah. It cannot appear in the only court that concludes the matter.\n\n'Or who will come to their defence?' — am man yakunu 'alayhim wakila. Who will be their agent, their representative?\n\nThis is worth turning over, because the instinct being corrected is a good one. Standing by your people is not a vice. But loyalty that helps someone win an unjust case has not helped them at all; it has confirmed them in something they will answer for alone.\n\nThe genuinely loyal act, in that situation, is to tell your relative the truth and refuse to argue his case. That is what 4:135 will command when it tells believers to stand for justice 'even if it is against yourselves, your parents, or close relatives'.",
  "110": "'Whoever commits evil or wrongs themselves then seeks Allah's forgiveness will certainly find Allah All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.' (Quran 4:110). After four verses on a conspiracy to frame an innocent man, the door opens.\n\nThis is the structural signature of Surah an-Nisa. Every serious condemnation is followed by a route out, usually within a verse or two — 4:16 after the sexual offences, 4:17 after them, 4:64 after the hypocrites, 4:99 after those who failed to migrate, and now this.\n\n'Whoever commits evil or wrongs themselves' — man ya'mal su'an aw yazlim nafsah. Two categories: harm done to others, and harm done to oneself. Both are covered, which means nothing in the preceding passage is excluded.\n\n'Then seeks Allah's forgiveness' — thumma yastaghfiri Allah. That is the only condition stated. And the seeking is real: it means turning away from the act, not merely uttering a formula while continuing.\n\n'Will certainly find Allah All-Forgiving, Most Merciful' — yajidi Allaha Ghafuran Rahima. The verb is 'find'. He will discover Him to be so. It is presented as something you encounter rather than something you are promised in the abstract.\n\nNotice that the men described in 4:105-109 had committed theft, lied under oath, tried to frame an innocent man of another faith, and dragged their community into defending them. This verse is addressed to them.\n\nIf forgiveness is available after that, the question of whether it is available after whatever a reader has done answers itself.",
  "111": "'And whoever commits a sin—it is only to their own loss. Allah is All-Knowing, All-Wise.' (Quran 4:111). A short principle placed after the case of the false accusation.\n\n'It is only to their own loss' — fa innama yaksibuhu 'ala nafsih. He earns it against himself. The verb 'kasaba' is the ordinary Arabic word for earning a living, and the Quran uses it for deeds throughout: a sin is something a person acquires, and it belongs to him.\n\nThe point being made is about ownership. The clan in 4:105-109 was trying to shift the consequences of a theft onto someone else, and it partly worked in the short term. This verse states that at the level that matters, no transfer occurred. The sin remained with the one who earned it.\n\nThe Quran states the rule elsewhere: 'No soul burdened with sin will bear the burden of another' (Quran 6:164), and more starkly, 'if a sin-burdened soul cries for help with its burden, none of it will be carried—even by a close relative' (Quran 35:18).\n\n'Allah is All-Knowing, All-Wise' — 'Aliman Hakima. The knowledge means the attribution is accurate; the wisdom means the placement is right.\n\nThere is a strange comfort in this alongside the warning. Nobody can be made to carry your sin, and nobody can put theirs onto you. A person forced into a bad situation by someone else's decision does not inherit their guilt.\n\nAnd the warning stands for anyone who thinks a sin has been successfully passed on to someone else. It has not moved.",
  "112": "'And whoever commits an evil or sinful deed then blames it on an innocent person, they will definitely bear the guilt of slander and blatant sin.' (Quran 4:112). The precise description of what the thief and his clan did.\n\nTwo stages are described. First 'commits an evil or sinful deed' — khati'atan aw ithma. Then 'blames it on an innocent person' — thumma yarmi bihi bari'a. The verb 'yarmi' means to throw or hurl. He throws his sin at someone else.\n\n'Bari'' means free, clear, innocent of the charge. The word is precise: not merely someone who did not do it, but someone with no connection to it at all.\n\n'They will definitely bear the guilt of slander and blatant sin' — buhtanan wa ithman mubina. 'Buhtan' appeared in 4:20 of the man who invents a fault in his wife to reclaim her dowry. It means an accusation so brazen that it leaves the accused stunned, unable to respond.\n\nThe verse describes two burdens carried at once: the original sin, and the slander laid on top of it. The attempt to escape the first produced a second and heavier one.\n\nThat is the structure of most cover-ups. The lie told to hide a fault is almost always worse than the fault, and it always requires more lies to sustain.\n\nNote also who the victim was in this case: a man of another faith living under Muslim protection. The Quran's intervention on his behalf is the strongest possible statement that justice in Islam does not track religious membership.",
  "113": "'Had it not been for Allah's grace and mercy, a group of them would have sought to deceive you ˹O Prophet˺. Yet they would deceive none but themselves, nor can they harm you in the least. Allah has revealed to you the Book and wisdom and taught you what you never knew. Great ˹indeed˺ is Allah's favour upon you!' (Quran 4:113). The conclusion of the affair, addressed to the Prophet.\n\n'A group of them' — ta'ifatun minhum. The Quran again refuses to generalise. A group within the clan, not the community.\n\n'Would have sought to deceive you' — hammat ta'ifatun minhum an yudilluk, to lead you astray in the judgment. They nearly succeeded, because the evidence they presented was constructed and he had no reason to doubt his own people.\n\nWhat protected him was 'Allah's grace and mercy' — revelation arriving in time. Not superior insight, and the verse says so plainly. This is important for understanding prophethood: the Prophet's protection in matters of religion came from Allah, not from an inherent immunity to being misled by liars.\n\n'They would deceive none but themselves' — wa ma yudilluna illa anfusahum. The same reversal as 4:111. They set out to mislead him and misled only themselves.\n\n'Nor can they harm you in the least' — the plot failed in every direction.\n\n'Allah has revealed to you the Book and wisdom and taught you what you never knew' — al-kitab wa al-hikmah. The Book, and wisdom alongside it. This is one of the Quran's clearest indications that the Prophet received guidance beyond the recited text, which is the basis for the authority of the Sunnah.",
  "114": "'There is no good in most of their secret talks—except those encouraging charity, kindness, or reconciliation between people. And whoever does this seeking Allah's pleasure, We will grant them a great reward.' (Quran 4:114). A rule about private conversations, arising from the night-time plotting described in 4:108.\n\n'Their secret talks' — najwahum. 'Najwa' is confidential conversation, the kind held in lowered voices. The Quran does not forbid it, but it observes that most of it contains no good.\n\nThat is a fair assessment of how private talk usually goes. When people speak in confidence, the subject is often a third person, and the tone is rarely generous.\n\nThree exceptions are named, and they are worth memorising as a test for any private conversation. 'Charity' — sadaqah: arranging help for someone. 'Kindness' — ma'ruf: any recognised good. And 'reconciliation between people' — islahin bayna al-nas: repairing a broken relationship.\n\nAbu al-Darda' reported that the Prophet, peace be upon him, said: \"Shall I not inform you of something better in degree than fasting, prayer and charity?\" They said, \"Yes, O Prophet of Allah.\" He said, \"Putting things right between people. And corrupting relations between people is the shaver\" — meaning it shaves away religion (Sunan Abi Dawud 4919, graded sahih by al-Albani).\n\nReconciliation is even given a licence that lying does not otherwise have. Umm Kulthum bint 'Uqbah reported that the Prophet said: \"He who makes peace between people by conveying good or saying good is not a liar\" (Sahih al-Bukhari 2692).\n\n'Seeking Allah's pleasure' — ibtigha'a mardati Allah. The condition. A person who reconciles two people to make himself important has done the deed without the reward.",
  "115": "'And whoever defies the Messenger after guidance has become clear to them and follows a path other than that of the believers, We will let them pursue what they have chosen, then burn them in Hell—what an evil end!' (Quran 4:115). A verse of great consequence in Islamic legal theory.\n\n'After guidance has become clear to them' — min ba'di ma tabayyana lahu al-huda. The condition is knowledge. This is not about someone who never understood; it is about someone who saw and went the other way.\n\n'Follows a path other than that of the believers' — wa yattabi' ghayra sabili al-mu'minin. This clause is the basis on which classical scholars, notably al-Shafi'i, argued for the authority of consensus — ijma'. The reasoning is that the verse condemns departing from the way of the believers as a separate matter alongside opposing the Messenger, which implies their agreed way carries weight.\n\n'We will let them pursue what they have chosen' — nuwallihi ma tawalla. We will turn him toward what he turned to. This is one of the most sobering statements in the Quran. The punishment begins as abandonment: you wanted that direction, you will be left to it.\n\nCompare the same principle in Surah Ali 'Imran, where Allah gives the wrongdoers slack rope (Quran 3:178).\n\nTwo safeguards are built in. First, guidance must have become clear — nobody is condemned for honest ignorance. Second, disagreement among scholars on genuinely disputed matters is not what this verse describes; the early community disagreed constantly and openly.\n\nWhat is condemned is the deliberate departure of someone who knows, and who wants a different answer.",
  "116": "'Surely Allah does not forgive associating ˹others˺ with Him ˹in worship˺, but forgives anything else of whoever He wills. Indeed, whoever associates ˹others˺ with Allah has clearly gone far astray.' (Quran 4:116). A near-repetition of 4:48, and the repetition is purposeful.\n\nVerse 4:48 ended by calling shirk 'a grave sin' — ithman 'azima. This verse ends differently: 'has clearly gone far astray' — dalalan ba'ida, a remote straying.\n\nThe change of ending changes the emphasis. The first described shirk as a crime. This one describes it as a distance — a person walking further and further from the road. That is why it appears here, immediately after 4:115 described someone who 'follows a path other than that of the believers'. The passage is about direction.\n\nEverything else in the sentence is the same, including the enormous concession in the middle: 'but forgives anything else of whoever He wills'. Anything else.\n\nWhy state it twice in one surah? Because these are the two things a person most needs to hold together, and most often separates. Someone who remembers only the first half despairs. Someone who remembers only the second half becomes careless.\n\nThe Quran places the two halves in one sentence, twice, so they cannot be taken apart.\n\nThe verse also introduces what follows. Verses 4:117-121 will describe what shirk actually consists of in practice, and how a person is led into it — not by a decision to worship a statue, but by a series of suggestions.",
  "117": "'Instead of Allah, they only invoke female gods and they ˹actually˺ invoke none but a rebellious Satan—' (Quran 4:117). What the idolatry of the Arabs actually amounted to.\n\n'Female gods' — inathan. The major idols of the Arabs bore feminine names: al-Lat, al-'Uzza and Manat, which the Quran names elsewhere and dismisses as 'mere names that you and your forefathers have made up' (Quran 53:23). They were also described as daughters of Allah, a claim the Quran rejects repeatedly.\n\nCommentators including al-Tabari also note the sense of lifeless things — objects with no capacity to act.\n\n'They actually invoke none but a rebellious Satan' — wa in yad'una illa shaytanan marida. This is the verse's real claim, and it reframes the whole subject. Behind the stone there is nothing; the one actually being served is the one who suggested the arrangement.\n\n'Marid' means rebellious, one who has thrown off all restraint. The word is used of something that has exceeded every bound.\n\nThe relevance beyond stone idols is direct. Nobody today prays to al-Lat. But anything a person genuinely relies on in place of Allah — a system, a person, a superstition, a charm — belongs to the same category, and the same question applies: who benefits from your attachment to it?\n\nThe Quran's method here is worth noting. It does not argue that the idols are weak. It says they are absent, and it identifies who is standing in their place.",
  "118": "'cursed by Allah—who said, \"I will surely take hold of a certain number of Your servants.\"' (Quran 4:118). The Quran begins quoting Satan directly, and the quotation runs to the end of 4:119.\n\n'Cursed by Allah' — la'anahu Allah. Driven away from mercy. His entire situation is defined by that removal.\n\n'I will surely take hold of a certain number of Your servants' — la attakhidhanna min 'ibadika nasiban mafruda. Read the last two words carefully. 'Nasiban mafruda' is an appointed share, a fixed portion — and it is exactly the phrase used in 4:7 for the obligatory shares of inheritance.\n\nThe choice of vocabulary is deliberate and chilling. He speaks of human beings as an allotment owed to him.\n\nNotice also what he says in the same breath: 'Your servants'. He knows whose they are. He is not confused about ownership; he intends to take a share of what belongs to Allah.\n\nThe Quran records similar declarations elsewhere: 'For leaving me to stray I will lie in ambush for them on Your Straight Path. I will approach them from their front, their back, their right, their left' (Quran 7:16-17), and 'If you delay my end until the Day of Judgment, I will certainly take hold of his descendants, except for a few' (Quran 17:62).\n\nThe value of knowing this is practical. An opponent who announces his strategy in advance is much easier to resist. The Quran reproduces his declaration so that no one can claim they were not told.",
  "119": "'I will certainly mislead them and delude them with empty hopes. Also, I will order them and they will slit the ears of cattle and alter Allah's creation.\" And whoever takes Satan as a guardian instead of Allah has certainly suffered a tremendous loss.' (Quran 4:119). The method, stated in his own words.\n\nFour steps are named, and they run from the inward to the outward.\n\n'I will mislead them' — la udillannahum. Begin with direction.\n\n'And delude them with empty hopes' — wa la umanniyannahum. 'Tamanni' is wishful thinking: the vague expectation that things will work out, that there is time, that one is basically fine. This is his most effective tool and it requires no argument.\n\n'I will order them and they will slit the ears of cattle' — a pre-Islamic practice of marking animals dedicated to idols. Once the inward work is done, ritual follows.\n\n'And alter Allah's creation' — wa la amurannahum fa la yughayyirunna khalqa Allah. Commentators including Ibn Kathir understand this broadly: mutilation for religious purposes, and by extension any deliberate corruption of the natural form Allah gave. Al-Tabari also reports the view that it includes altering the religion of Allah — the fitrah He created people upon.\n\nThe progression is the lesson. Nobody arrives at the last step directly. It starts with wishful thinking, and wishful thinking feels harmless.\n\n'Whoever takes Satan as a guardian' — waliyyan. A guardian is someone whose direction you accept. Most people who do this never make a decision to do it.",
  "120": "'Satan only makes them ˹false˺ promises and deludes them with ˹empty˺ hopes. Truly Satan promises them nothing but delusion.' (Quran 4:120). Allah's assessment of everything just quoted.\n\nThe pattern is repeated in three short clauses: promises, empty hopes, delusion. The verse is almost entirely repetition, which is itself the technique — his method has no variety, and neither does the description of it.\n\n'Promises' — ya'iduhum. He is always offering something. Later, more, easier, safer. The offer is always in the future, which is what makes it unfalsifiable.\n\n'Delusion' — ghurur. The same word used in Surah Ali 'Imran of the world itself: 'the life of this world is no more than the delusion of enjoyment' (Quran 3:185). And the same root as the warning in 3:196: do not be deceived.\n\nSet this verse against 4:76: 'Indeed, Satan's schemes are ever weak.' Together they describe an opponent with no actual power whose entire operation runs on persuasion. He cannot compel a single act. He can only make things appear other than they are.\n\nThe counter is stated by contrast. Allah promises and delivers; He also warns. Satan only ever promises, because a promise costs nothing and a person cannot check it until afterwards.\n\nA useful test in the moment of temptation: what exactly am I being promised, and by whom, and what is the evidence that it will be delivered? The promises rarely survive being stated out loud.",
  "121": "'It is they who will have Hell as their home, and they will find no escape from it!' (Quran 4:121). The end point of the process described in 4:117-120.\n\n'Their home' — ma'wahum. The place of return and settlement, the same word used in Surah Ali 'Imran at 3:197 of those deceived by worldly prosperity. In both cases the point is permanence set against something temporary.\n\n'They will find no escape from it' — wa la yajiduna 'anha mahisa. 'Mahis' means a place to swerve aside to, a way of turning off. The image is of someone looking for an exit and finding the road has none.\n\nThat image answers the promises of 4:120 exactly. He promised, and the promise ended in a place with no way out. Every offer he made was about options — you can always change later, there is time, you can come back. This verse describes the destination as the removal of options.\n\nThe contrast with the believers is drawn immediately in 4:122, and the Quran almost always places these two outcomes side by side.\n\nThere is also a quiet warning here about the delay tactic specifically. Wishful thinking works by keeping the exit open in the imagination: I will fix this eventually. The verse describes the moment when eventually arrives.\n\nBut note where this sits in the surah. Verse 4:110 has already promised forgiveness to whoever seeks it, and 4:116 has confirmed that everything short of shirk falls within His will to forgive. The exit exists now. It is only afterwards that it does not.",
  "122": "'And those who believe and do good, We will soon admit them into Gardens under which rivers flow, to stay there for ever and ever. Allah's promise is ˹always˺ true. And whose word is more truthful than Allah's?' (Quran 4:122). The counterpart to 4:121, and it answers it clause by clause.\n\nThey had a home in Hell; these have Gardens. They found no escape; these stay 'for ever and ever' — khalidina fiha abada, and here permanence is the reward rather than the sentence.\n\nThen the clause that makes the comparison complete: 'Allah's promise is always true' — wa'da Allahi haqqa.\n\nVerse 4:120 said Satan 'promises them nothing but delusion'. This verse names the other promise. Both parties make offers; only one has ever delivered on one.\n\n'And whose word is more truthful than Allah's?' — wa man asdaqu mina Allahi qila. The same question asked in 4:87, with one word changed: there it was 'hadith', speech; here 'qil', a saying. The repetition of the question at both ends of this section frames everything between as a matter of whose word you accept.\n\nThat is the actual decision a person makes. Not between religion and irreligion in the abstract, but between two competing accounts of what will happen — one from a source with a record of delivering, and one from a source described as having nothing but talk.\n\n'Those who believe and do good' — the entry condition, unchanged throughout the Quran. Believe, and act on it.",
  "123": "'˹Divine grace is˺ neither by your wishes nor those of the People of the Book! Whoever commits evil will be rewarded accordingly, and they will find no protector or helper besides Allah.' (Quran 4:123). A verse that closes off religious complacency in every direction at once.\n\n'Neither by your wishes' — laysa bi amaniyyikum. 'Amani' is the plural of the wishful thinking named in 4:119-120 as Satan's chief tool. Here it is applied to religion itself: the hope that belonging to the right community settles the matter.\n\nAnd note that Muslims are addressed first. 'Your wishes' — the believers'. Only then 'nor those of the People of the Book'.\n\nThat order is the point. It would have been easy to read the surah's criticisms of other communities as a licence for self-satisfaction. This verse forecloses it before anyone can. Commentators including Ibn Kathir record that Muslims and others were disputing over whose community was superior, and this came down.\n\nAbu Sa'id al-Khudri reported that the Prophet, peace be upon him, said: \"You will follow the ways of those before you inch by inch and step by step, so much so that if they entered the hole of a lizard, you would follow them.\" We said, \"O Messenger of Allah, the Jews and the Christians?\" He said, \"Who else?\" (Sahih Muslim 2669a).\n\n'Whoever commits evil will be rewarded accordingly' — man ya'mal su'an yujza bih. Deeds, not labels.\n\nAbu Hurayrah reported that the Prophet said: \"Allah does not look at your appearances or your wealth, but He looks at your hearts and your deeds\" (Sahih Muslim 2564c).",
  "124": "'But those who do good—whether male or female—and have faith will enter Paradise and will never be wronged ˹even as much as˺ the speck on a date stone.' (Quran 4:124). The positive statement of the rule laid down in 4:123.\n\nIf membership does not save, what does? This verse answers: doing good, with faith.\n\n'Whether male or female' — min dhakarin aw untha. The surah has now said this twice (see also 4:32), and Surah Ali 'Imran said it too (Quran 3:195). The repetition is not accidental in a chapter named 'The Women'. Wherever reward is discussed, the Quran stops to specify that both are included.\n\n'And have faith' — wa huwa mu'min. Both conditions together. Good deeds without faith lack the orientation that makes them worship; faith without deeds is the claim the surah has spent many verses dismantling.\n\n'Will enter Paradise' — fa ula'ika yadkhuluna al-jannah. Stated plainly, without qualification.\n\n'And will never be wronged even as much as the speck on a date stone' — naqira. The tiny indentation on a date pit, the same unit used in 4:53. Alongside the 'fatil' of 4:49 and 4:77 and the atom's weight of 4:40, the surah has now used four separate images for the smallest measurable amount, each time to make the same claim about the precision of the accounting.\n\nFour times in one chapter is emphasis. Whatever else a person fears about the Day of Judgment, being short-changed is not among the possibilities.",
  "125": "'And who is better in faith than those who ˹fully˺ submit themselves to Allah, do good, and follow the Way of Abraham, the upright? Allah chose Abraham as a close friend.' (Quran 4:125). The answer to the argument about superiority in 4:123.\n\nThree things are named, and together they define the best religion any person can hold.\n\n'Submit themselves to Allah' — aslama wajhahu lillah, literally submitted his face to Allah. The face stands for the whole person and the direction he is turned. This is the meaning of islam in its root sense: handing yourself over.\n\n'Do good' — wa huwa muhsin. 'Ihsan' is doing something well, not merely doing it. Submission and excellence together.\n\n'And follow the Way of Abraham, the upright' — millata Ibrahima hanifa. 'Hanif' means one who turns away from all deviation toward the pure worship of the One. Surah Ali 'Imran made the same point: 'Abraham was neither a Jew nor a Christian; he submitted in all uprightness' (Quran 3:67).\n\nPlaced after 4:123, this is the reply to competing claims of ownership over Ibrahim. The criterion is not lineage but conduct.\n\n'Allah chose Abraham as a close friend' — wattakhadha Allahu Ibrahima khalila. 'Khalil' is a deeper word than friend: one whose love has entered every part of you. It is the title by which Ibrahim is known in Islam, and it was earned by exactly the three qualities the verse just listed.",
  "126": "'To Allah ˹alone˺ belongs whatever is in the heavens and whatever is on the earth. And Allah is Fully Aware of everything.' (Quran 4:126). A short verse following the honour given to Ibrahim.\n\nThe placement is careful. Verse 4:125 said Allah took Ibrahim as a khalil, a close friend. That could be misread as suggesting a relationship of need — as human friendship always involves need on both sides.\n\nSo this verse follows immediately: everything in the heavens and the earth belongs to Him. The friendship was pure favour, not an arrangement between parties who required each other.\n\n'To Allah belongs whatever is in the heavens and whatever is on the earth' — this exact sentence appears three times in the space of a few verses (4:126, 4:131, 4:132), which is unusual even for the Quran. Each time it corrects a possible misunderstanding about what Allah gains from anything.\n\n'And Allah is Fully Aware of everything' — wa kana Allahu bi kulli shay'in muhita. 'Muhit' means encompassing, surrounding. Ownership plus complete knowledge of what is owned.\n\nFor the reader, the verse resets perspective after a long passage about human disputes, marriage difficulties, lawsuits and rival claims. All of it takes place inside a creation owned entirely by one Owner who is aware of every part of it.\n\nThere is comfort in that as well as warning. Nothing that happens to you is outside the property, or outside the awareness, of the One you are asking.",
  "127": "'They ask you ˹O Prophet˺ regarding women. Say, \"It is Allah Who instructs you regarding them. Instruction has ˹already˺ been revealed in the Book concerning the orphan women you deprive of their due rights but still wish to marry, also helpless children, as well as standing up for orphans' rights. And whatever good you do is certainly well known to Allah.\"' (Quran 4:127). The surah returns to where it began.\n\n'They ask you regarding women' — wa yastaftunaka fi al-nisa'. 'Istifta'' is a request for a formal ruling. People were bringing cases, and the surah is now answering them.\n\n'Aisha explained this verse. 'Urwah reported that she said: an orphan girl would be under the care of a guardian who shared property with her, and being attracted by her wealth and beauty he would want to marry her without giving her a fair dowry. So they were forbidden from that unless they dealt justly with her. She added that people then asked the Messenger of Allah for further instruction after 4:3 was revealed, and Allah revealed this verse. She also explained the phrase 'yet you wish to marry them': it refers to a guardian showing no interest in an orphan girl in his care when she is lacking in property and beauty (Sahih al-Bukhari 4574).\n\nSo the abuse ran both ways. If she was rich and attractive, he married her and underpaid. If she was poor and plain, he left her unmarried and kept control of her property.\n\nThree protected categories are listed: orphan women, 'helpless children' — al-mustad'afina mina al-wildan, and orphans generally. The whole surah has kept returning to the same people.\n\n'And whatever good you do is certainly well known to Allah' — no fair dealing goes unrecorded.",
  "128": "'If a woman fears indifference or neglect from her husband, there is no blame on either of them if they seek ˹fair˺ settlement, which is best. Humans are ever inclined to selfishness. But if you are gracious and mindful ˹of Allah˺, surely Allah is All-Aware of what you do.' (Quran 4:128). A marriage in trouble, viewed from the wife's side.\n\n'Indifference or neglect' — nushuzan aw i'rada. Note that 'nushuz' is the same word used in 4:34 of a wife's serious ill-conduct. The Quran applies it to husbands too, which means the fault is not assigned to one sex.\n\n'I'rad' means turning away — the husband who has not left, is not cruel, and is simply no longer present in the marriage.\n\n'There is no blame on either of them if they seek a settlement' — an yuslaha baynahuma sulha. A negotiated arrangement. She might waive part of her rights — a share of his time, some maintenance — in order to preserve the marriage rather than lose it.\n\n'Aisha reported that Sawdah bint Zam'ah gave up her turn to her, and the Prophet used to give 'Aisha both her day and Sawdah's day (Sahih al-Bukhari 5212). Sawdah made exactly this kind of arrangement, voluntarily, and it stands as the model.\n\n'Humans are ever inclined to selfishness' — wa uhdirati al-anfusu al-shuhh. One of the most honest lines in the Quran. 'Shuhh' is a grasping tightness, and the verse says souls are permanently exposed to it. Neither party in a failing marriage is likely to be generous by instinct.\n\n'But if you are gracious and mindful of Allah' — the way out is named, and it is aimed at both of them.",
  "129": "'You will never be able to maintain ˹emotional˺ justice between your wives—no matter how keen you are. So do not totally incline towards one leaving the other in suspense. And if you do what is right and are mindful ˹of Allah˺, surely Allah is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.' (Quran 4:129). A statement of what is impossible, and a rule about what is required.\n\n'You will never be able to maintain justice between your wives' — lan tastati'u an ta'dilu bayna al-nisa'i wa law harastum. The Arabic 'lan' is an absolute negation of the future. Never, even if you desperately want to.\n\nThis concerns the heart. Affection cannot be distributed by decision, and the Quran does not pretend otherwise. Read together with 4:3 — 'if you are afraid you will fail to maintain justice, then one' — the two verses have led many scholars to conclude that the Quran's preference is plainly for monogamy.\n\n'Aisha reported that the Prophet, peace be upon him, used to divide his time between his wives justly, and he would say: \"O Allah, this is my division in what I control, so do not blame me for what You control and I do not control\" (Jami' at-Tirmidhi 1140, graded sahih by Darussalam). Abu 'Isa noted that the scholars explained the last phrase as referring to love and affection.\n\nEven the Prophet, who was perfectly just in everything measurable, prayed about the part that was not in his hands.\n\n'Do not totally incline towards one leaving the other in suspense' — ka al-mu'allaqah, like something suspended, hanging. Neither married nor free. What is forbidden is not the inequality of feeling but acting on it until the other woman's life is left in limbo.",
  "130": "'But if they choose to separate, Allah will enrich both of them from His bounties. And Allah is Ever-Bountiful, All-Wise.' (Quran 4:130). What Islam says when a marriage cannot be saved.\n\n'If they choose to separate' — wa in yatafarraqa. After 4:128 recommended settlement and 4:129 set the limits of what can be required, this verse acknowledges that some marriages end.\n\nThere is no condemnation here, and no suggestion of failure. Divorce is permitted in Islam, and the Quran treats it as a real option rather than a shameful last resort.\n\n'Allah will enrich both of them from His bounties' — yughni Allahu kullan min sa'atih. Both of them. Not the wronged party only, and not the man only. The promise is symmetrical.\n\n'Min sa'atih' — from His breadth, His abundance. The same word used in 4:100 of the emigrant who leaves his home and is promised space and provision. The parallel is instructive: leaving a place, and leaving a marriage, both feel like walking into a void, and both are met with the same promise.\n\nThat promise addresses the real fear. People stay in marriages that have genuinely ended because they cannot see what comes after — financially, socially, emotionally. The verse says the provision does not depend on the marriage.\n\n'Ever-Bountiful, All-Wise' — Wasi'an Hakima. 'Wasi'' means vast, unrestricted. His resources are not limited to the arrangement that just ended.",
  "131": "'To Allah ˹alone˺ belongs whatever is in the heavens and whatever is on the earth. Indeed, We have commanded those given the Scripture before you, as well as you, to be mindful of Allah. But if you disobey, then ˹know that˺ to Allah belongs whatever is in the heavens and the earth. And Allah is Self-Sufficient, Praiseworthy.' (Quran 4:131). The ownership statement returns, now attached to a command.\n\n'We have commanded those given the Scripture before you, as well as you, to be mindful of Allah' — an ittaqu Allah. Taqwa is named as the single instruction given to every community that received revelation. Whatever the differences in law between them, the core command has never changed.\n\n'But if you disobey, then to Allah belongs whatever is in the heavens and the earth.' The sentence structure is striking. The consequence of disobedience is not stated as a punishment but as a restatement of ownership — which is the more devastating reply. Your disobedience does not diminish Him by anything.\n\n'And Allah is Self-Sufficient, Praiseworthy' — Ghaniyyan Hamida. 'Ghani' means free of need. 'Hamid' means praiseworthy in Himself, whether or not anyone praises Him.\n\nThe two names together close every avenue for thinking that religion is something Allah needs from us. Surah Ali 'Imran said the same of Hajj: 'whoever disbelieves, then surely Allah is not in need of ˹any of His˺ creation' (Quran 3:97).\n\nThe practical effect of understanding this is a change in motive. If He gains nothing from your obedience, then every command was issued for your benefit, which is exactly what 4:26 and 4:28 said.",
  "132": "'To Allah ˹alone˺ belongs whatever is in the heavens and whatever is on the earth. And Allah is sufficient as a Trustee of Affairs.' (Quran 4:132). The third statement of ownership in seven verses.\n\nEach occurrence has ended differently, and the endings are the argument. In 4:126: He is fully aware of everything. In 4:131: He is self-sufficient and praiseworthy. Here: 'sufficient as a Trustee of Affairs' — wa kafa billahi wakila.\n\n'Wakil' is the one you appoint to handle your affairs, the agent who acts on your behalf. It is the word from 'Hasbuna Allahu wa ni'ma al-wakil' (Quran 3:173), and it was used in 4:81 when the Prophet was told to leave the plotters to Allah.\n\nSo the three endings move from knowledge, to independence, to availability. He owns everything and knows it; He needs nothing from you; and He will nevertheless handle your affairs if you place them with Him.\n\nThat last step is the one worth sitting with. An owner who needs nothing has no obligation to manage anyone's problems. The verse says He is sufficient for exactly that.\n\nThe repetition also does something structural. This part of the surah has moved through marital breakdown, divorce, competing religious claims and human selfishness. Between each subject, the Quran restates who owns the field. The disputes are real, and they are all taking place inside His property.",
  "133": "'If it is His Will, He can remove you altogether, O humanity, and replace you with others. And Allah is Most Capable to do so.' (Quran 4:133). The consequence of the self-sufficiency stated in 4:131.\n\n'He can remove you altogether' — yudhhibkum. Take you away. The address is 'ayyuha al-nas' — O humanity, not O believers. Nobody is outside it.\n\n'And replace you with others' — wa ya'ti bi akharin. The replacement is mentioned casually, as a simple matter of substitution. The Quran says the same elsewhere: 'If you ˹still˺ turn away, He will replace you with another people. And they will not be like you' (Quran 47:38).\n\nThis is aimed squarely at a particular kind of religious self-importance — the assumption that Allah's cause depends on us, that our community is indispensable, that He will overlook our failures because He needs us to carry the message.\n\nVerse 4:123 already dismantled the idea that belonging saves a person. This verse dismantles the idea that a community's existence is guaranteed.\n\n'And Allah is Most Capable to do so' — wa kana Allahu 'ala dhalika qadira. It is not an idle threat, and it is not hypothetical. Communities entrusted with revelation have been replaced before, which is much of what Surah Ali 'Imran and Surah al-Baqarah were about.\n\nThe right response is not anxiety but seriousness. Being given a role is not the same as owning it. It is a trust — and this surah has already said, in 4:58, what must be done with trusts.",
  "134": "'Whoever desires the reward of this world, then ˹let them know that˺ with Allah are the rewards of this world and the Hereafter. And Allah is All-Hearing, All-Seeing.' (Quran 4:134). An argument aimed at the person whose real ambition is worldly.\n\nThe logic is disarming. You want the reward of this world? Fine — but notice where both rewards are kept. 'With Allah are the rewards of this world and the Hereafter' — fa 'inda Allahi thawabu al-dunya wa al-akhirah.\n\nSo the person chasing worldly gain by worldly means is not merely aiming low; he is going to the wrong counter. Both are held by the same One, and asking Him for the greater does not forfeit the lesser.\n\nThe Quran elsewhere states the trade more starkly: 'Whoever desires the harvest of the Hereafter, We will increase their harvest. And whoever desires ˹only˺ the harvest of this world, We will give them some of it, but they will have no share in the Hereafter' (Quran 42:20). Note 'some of it' — the worldly seeker does not even get all of what he asked for.\n\nSurah Ali 'Imran divided people the same way at Uhud: 'some of you desire this world, while others desire the Hereafter' (Quran 3:152).\n\n'And Allah is All-Hearing, All-Seeing' — Sami'an Basira. He hears what you ask for and sees what you are actually working toward, which are frequently two different things.\n\nThe correction being offered is not that a person should want nothing from this life. It is that he should ask the right party, and ask for the whole thing.",
  "135": "'O believers! Stand firm for justice as witnesses for Allah even if it is against yourselves, your parents, or close relatives. Be they rich or poor, Allah is best to ensure their interests. So do not let your desires cause you to deviate ˹from justice˺. If you distort the testimony or refuse to give it, then ˹know that˺ Allah is certainly All-Aware of what you do.' (Quran 4:135). The high point of the surah on justice.\n\n'Stand firm for justice' — kunu qawwamina bi al-qist. The word 'qawwamin' is the same root used in 4:34 of a husband's responsibility: one who stands over a thing and maintains it. Justice requires someone standing over it, or it collapses.\n\nThen the three hardest cases, in ascending order of difficulty: 'against yourselves, your parents, or close relatives.' Against yourself first. This is the verse that answers the clan in 4:105-109 who argued their thief's case.\n\n'Be they rich or poor' — the two temptations named together. Favour the rich man because he has power; favour the poor man because you pity him. Both are corruptions, and the Quran forbids both in one clause. 'Allah is best to ensure their interests' — He is closer to both of them than you are.\n\n'Do not let your desires cause you to deviate' — fa la tattabi'u al-hawa an ta'dilu. Injustice is traced to hawa, personal inclination, rather than to ignorance of the right answer.\n\nThe companion verse addresses the opposite bias: 'Do not let the hatred of a people lead you to injustice. Be just! That is closer to righteousness' (Quran 5:8).\n\nJustice is required against those you love and in favour of those you do not.",
  "136": "'O believers! Have faith in Allah, His Messenger, the Book He has revealed to His Messenger, and the Scriptures He revealed before. Indeed, whoever denies Allah, His angels, His Books, His messengers, and the Last Day has clearly gone far astray.' (Quran 4:136). Believers are told to believe — which sounds redundant until you look at it.\n\n'O believers! Have faith' — ya ayyuha alladhina amanu aminu. Address them as believers, then command them to believe. Commentators including al-Tabari and Ibn Kathir explain the meaning as: continue in it, deepen it, hold to it. Faith is not a status acquired once; it is a state that must be maintained.\n\nThat reading is confirmed by 4:137, which describes people who believed, disbelieved, believed and disbelieved again. Faith can be lost by someone who had it.\n\nThen the six articles are listed: Allah, His angels, His Books, His messengers, the Last Day — and in this verse the Book revealed to Muhammad alongside those revealed before. Belief in the earlier scriptures is not optional; a Muslim affirms that Allah sent them, without accepting later alterations.\n\n'Has clearly gone far astray' — dalalan ba'ida. The same phrase used of shirk in 4:116. Denying any one of these articles places a person at the same distance.\n\nThe practical weight of this verse falls on the first phrase. Anyone who assumes his faith is settled because he once declared it is being addressed here. The command is present tense: believe — now, and keep believing.",
  "137": "'Indeed, those who believed then disbelieved, then believed and again disbelieved—˹only˺ increasing in disbelief—Allah will neither forgive them nor guide them to the ˹Right˺ Way.' (Quran 4:137). A description of a specific pattern rather than a single act.\n\nRead the sequence: believed, disbelieved, believed, disbelieved, and then 'increasing in disbelief' — thumma izdadu kufra. Four movements and a settled direction.\n\nWhat is being condemned is not a person who left and came back, nor someone who struggled and returned. Islam accepts repentance repeatedly, and 4:110 promised forgiveness to whoever seeks it. This verse describes something different: a person for whom faith has become a garment put on and taken off according to circumstance.\n\nCommentators including Ibn Kathir connect it to hypocrites in Madinah who moved with the political weather, professing Islam when it was advantageous and abandoning it when it was not.\n\nThe final clause is the diagnosis: 'increasing in disbelief'. Each cycle left him further out than the last. That is why the verse ends with no forgiveness and no guidance — not because the door was shut on him, but because he has walked past it so many times that he no longer looks for it.\n\nThe point for an ordinary believer is not to fear his own lapses. It is to notice the direction of travel. Everyone falls; the question is whether each fall leaves you closer to the road or further from it.",
  "138": "'Give good news of a painful punishment to hypocrites,' (Quran 4:138). A sentence built on a deliberate contradiction.\n\n'Give good news' — bashshir. The verb means to bring news that makes a face light up; it is the word used throughout the Quran for glad tidings of Paradise. Here it is attached to 'a painful punishment' — 'adhaban alima.\n\nThe mismatch is the message. It is bitter irony, and the Quran uses it precisely because irony is the appropriate register for hypocrisy. A hypocrite's whole existence is a mismatch between the outside and the inside, and the verse answers him in his own idiom.\n\n'Hypocrites' — al-munafiqin. The word comes from 'nafaq', the tunnel of a jerboa, which has a hidden second exit. The animal enters at one hole and leaves by another. That is the image Arabic chose for the person who keeps an escape route from every commitment.\n\nThis begins a long passage — running to 4:145 — that describes the hypocrites in detail: their prayer, their alliances, their wavering, and their end.\n\nNote that the surah has just described, in 4:137, people who cycled in and out of faith. This verse names them. The description came first, the label second, which is the Quran's usual method: show the behaviour, then say what it is called.\n\nAnd observe who is addressed: the Prophet is told to deliver the news, not to hunt for them.",
  "139": "The exposure of seeking false honor through hostile alliances and the reality of divine power are proclaimed in 'who choose disbelievers as allies instead of the believers. Do they seek honour and power through that company? Surely all honour and power belongs to Allah.'\n\nConnecting directly to the condemnation of hypocrites in the previous verse, the Quran exposes their core political strategy: 'who choose disbelievers as allies instead of the believers' ('Alladhina yattakhidhuna al-kafirina awliya'a min duni al-mu'minin'). Driven by an inferiority complex and political insecurity, the hypocrites secretly sought alliances with hostile pagan and scriptural adversaries, believing that aligning with dominant worldly powers would secure their status and protection.\n\nThe Quran challenges their motive with a piercing question: 'Do they seek honour and power through that company?' ('A-yabtaghuna 'indahumu al-'izzah?'). The word 'al-'izzah' denotes true honor, dignity, strength, and security.\n\nGod delivers the absolute theological reality: 'Surely all honour and power belongs to Allah' ('fa-inna al-'izzata lillahi jami'a'). True honor and power belong exclusively to the Creator of the universe. Aligning with those who oppose God to gain worldly status is a pathetic delusion that brings only humiliation, for worldly empires crumble, but God's honor is eternal.\n\nIn an authentic narration, Caliph Umar ibn al-Khattab declared: 'We were the most disgraced people, and Allah honored us through Islam; if we ever seek honor through anything else, Allah will humiliate us!'\n\nThis verse inspires believers to seek dignity exclusively through faith and obedience to God.\n\nCommentators point out that seeking dignity through the compromise of truth is a fatal delusion. All power, honor, and security belong exclusively to the Almighty. Believers are inspired to hold proudly to their faith, knowing that true nobility in this life and the next is found solely in obedience to God. True honor is an eternal divine gift bestowed upon those who stand with truth, liberating the believer from all dependence upon corrupt worldly powers.",
  "140": "The strict prohibition against sitting in gatherings where sacred revelation is mocked is detailed in 'He has already revealed to you in the Book that when you hear Allah’s revelations being denied or ridiculed, then do not sit in that company unless they engage in a different topic, or else you will be like them. Surely Allah will gather the hypocrites and disbelievers all together in Hell.'\n\nThe verse refers to the earlier command revealed in Surah al-An'am: 'when you hear Allah’s revelations being denied or ridiculed, then do not sit in that company unless they engage in a different topic' ('fa-la taq'udu ma'ahum hatta yakhudu fi hadithin ghayrih').\n\nWhen sitting in gatherings, media spaces, or private conversations where the verses of God, the character of the Prophet, or sacred moral values are openly mocked, blasphemed, or denied, believers are strictly forbidden from participating or remaining passively present. Remaining silent in the presence of blasphemy normalizes sin and corrupts the heart.\n\nThe Quran delivers a startling legal and moral verdict: 'or else you will be like them' ('innakum idhan mithluhum'). Passive tolerance and voluntary association with those who mock sacred truths make a person complicit in the sin!\n\nThe verse reveals their shared eternal destination: 'Surely Allah will gather the hypocrites and disbelievers all together in Hell' ('inna Allaha jami'u al-munafiqina wa-al-kafirina fi Jahannama jami'a').\n\nClassical scholars emphasize that this verse establishes the sacred duty of guarding our environments, media consumption, and friendships, ensuring that we honor divine revelation with profound reverence.\n\nClassical scholars emphasize that guarding our gatherings and media environments from the mockery of sacred values is a vital spiritual discipline. Refusing to normalize cynicism protects the purity of the heart, ensuring that our lives are dedicated to honoring divine revelation with profound reverence and love. Protecting our environments from the mockery of sacred values preserves the purity of the soul, ensuring that our strivings remain pleasing to the Almighty.",
  "141": "The exposure of two-faced opportunism in wartime and the final triumph of believers are detailed in '˹The hypocrites are˺ those who wait to see what happens to you. So if Allah grants you victory, they say ˹to you˺, “Were we not on your side?” But if the disbelievers have a share ˹of victory˺, they say ˹to them˺, “Did we not have the advantage over you, yet we protected you from the believers?” Allah will judge between ˹all of˺ you on the Day of Judgment. And Allah will never grant the disbelievers a way over the believers.'\n\nThe verse diagnoses the parasitic behavior of the hypocrites: 'those who wait to see what happens to you' ('Alladhina yatarabbasuna bikum'). They sit on the sidelines of history, committing to neither side, waiting to see who wins before declaring loyalty.\n\nThe Quran exposes their double rhetoric:\nIf Muslims achieve victory and spoils: 'they say ˹to you˺, “Were we not on your side?”', claiming shared brotherhood to seize a share of wealth;\nIf adversaries achieve a temporary advantage: 'they say ˹to them˺, “Did we not have the advantage over you, yet we protected you from the believers?”', boasting of how their secret intelligence and internal sabotage aided the enemy!\n\nThe verse affirms divine resolution: 'Allah will judge between ˹all of˺ you on the Day of Judgment.'\n\nGod delivers an unshakeable promise: 'And Allah will never grant the disbelievers a way over the believers' ('wa lan yaj'ala Allahu li-al-kafirina 'ala al-mu'minina sabila'). The disbelievers will never achieve ultimate spiritual, moral, or eternal dominance over the faithful.\n\nThis passage teaches believers to build community strength upon sincere, tested loyalty.\n\nScholars of tafsir highlight the parasitic nature of opportunism, contrasting it with the steadfast loyalty of true believers. God's promise that disbelievers will never achieve ultimate dominance over the faithful brings profound reassurance, motivating the community to build solidarity upon sincere faith and mutual sacrifice.",
  "142": "The psychological anatomy of hypocritical prayer and the delusion of deceiving God are unveiled in 'Surely the hypocrites seek to deceive Allah, but He outwits them. When they stand up for prayer, they do it half-heartedly only to be seen by people—hardly remembering Allah at all.'\n\nThe verse exposes the foolish arrogance of hypocrisy: 'Surely the hypocrites seek to deceive Allah, but He outwits them' ('Inna al-munafiqina yukhadi'una Allaha wa Huwa khadi'uhum'). They imagine that by putting on an outward show of Islam, they can trick God; in reality, God requites their deceit with absolute justice, exposing their plots on earth and leaving them trapped in spiritual blindness.\n\nThe Quran provides the ultimate behavioral test of their worship: 'When they stand up for prayer, they do it half-heartedly' ('wa idha qamu ila al-salati qamu kusala'). They perform prayer with heavy reluctance, spiritual laziness ('kusala'), and inner resentment, viewing worship as an exhausting social chore.\n\nThe verse identifies their corrupt motive: 'only to be seen by people' ('yura'una al-nas'), praying solely for public applause, social acceptance, and political status.\n\nThe verse reveals their spiritual emptiness: 'hardly remembering Allah at all' ('wa la yadhkuruna Allaha illa qalila'). Their prayers are mechanical, rushed, and completely devoid of presence of heart.\n\nIn an authentic hadith in Sahih Muslim, the Prophet Muhammad described this as the 'prayer of the hypocrite' who waits until sunset, rushes through four quick cycles like a bird pecking the ground, and barely remembers God.\n\nThis verse calls believers to perform prayer with vitality, joy, and deep spiritual presence.\n\nCommentators warn against the spiritual danger of sluggish, ostentatious prayer. True worship is an intimate meeting with the Creator that demands vitality, joy, and deep presence of heart. Cultivating active remembrance during prayer transforms worship into an ongoing fountain of peace, moral strength, and spiritual elevation.",
  "143": "The tragic state of spiritual wavering and the paralysis of indecision are portrayed in 'Torn between belief and disbelief—belonging neither to these ˹believers˺ nor those ˹disbelievers˺. And whoever Allah leaves to stray, you will never find for them a way.'\n\nThe verse delivers a masterclass in spiritual psychology, describing the chronic instability of the hypocrite: 'Torn between belief and disbelief' ('Mudhabdhabina bayna dhalik'). The Arabic word 'mudhabdhab' evokes the image of a pendulum swinging continuously back and forth in the wind, never settling upon a stable foundation.\n\nThe Quran describes their total alienation: 'belonging neither to these ˹believers˺ nor those ˹disbelievers˺' ('la ila ha'ula'i wa la ila ha'ula'i'). The sincere believers do not trust them because of their duplicity, and the open disbelievers do not respect them because of their cowardice. They are trapped in an exhausting spiritual no-man's-land, lacking the courage to fully commit to truth and lacking the honesty to declare their disbelief.\n\nThe verse concludes with the law of spiritual abandonment: 'And whoever Allah leaves to stray, you will never find for them a way' ('wa man yudlili Allahu fa-lan tajida lahu sabila'). When a person persistently suppresses their own conscience and plays games with sacred truth, God allows them to remain in their self-inflicted confusion.\n\nClassical scholars observe that this verse urges believers to cultivate decisive, wholehearted commitment to faith, avoiding the paralyzing trap of double-mindedness and spiritual hypocrisy.\n\nClassical authorities describe the chronic wavering of the hypocrite as an exhausting spiritual trap. Lacking the courage to commit to truth leaves the soul alienated and lost. Believers are urged to cultivate wholehearted, decisive commitment to divine guidance, finding true peace in unconditional surrender to God. Wholehearted, decisive commitment to divine guidance dispels all spiritual confusion, anchoring the human soul in profound peace, clarity, and unshakeable purpose.",
  "144": "The direct prohibition against choosing hostile adversaries as allies and providing evidence against oneself are commanded in 'O believers! Do not take disbelievers as allies instead of the believers. Would you like to give Allah solid proof against yourselves?'\n\nAddressing the community of faith with protective love ('O believers!'), God repeats this crucial political and spiritual boundary: 'Do not take disbelievers as allies instead of the believers' ('la tattakhidhu al-kafirina awliya'a min duni al-mu'minin').\n\nBelievers are forbidden from prioritizing political, financial, or strategic alliances with hostile enemies who actively oppose the faith over loyalty to their own community of believers. Doing so fractures communal solidarity, leaks sensitive vulnerabilities, and demonstrates a severe deficiency of faith.\n\nThe Quran poses a piercing rhetorical challenge: 'Would you like to give Allah solid proof against yourselves?' ('A-turiduna an taj'alu lillahi 'alaykum sultanan mubina?').\n\nThe term 'sultan mubin' refers to clear, decisive, and undeniable legal evidence. Siding with the enemies of the community against your own brethren provides undeniable proof of hypocrisy and disloyalty, warranting divine punishment.\n\nClassical commentators like al-Sa'di observe that this verse teaches the essential Islamic principle of communal loyalty (al-Wala'): believers must stand united in mutual love, protection, and brotherhood, refusing to compromise the security of their community for worldly alliances.\n\nThis passage calls believers to strengthen their internal solidarity, ensuring that their loyalty belongs exclusively to God, His Messenger, and the faithful.\n\nScholars emphasize that communal loyalty (al-Wala') is essential for the preservation of faith and justice. Prioritizing alliances with hostile adversaries undermines communal safety and provides evidence of disloyalty. Believers are called to strengthen internal brotherhood, anchoring their loyalty in God, His Messenger, and the faithful. Fostering internal brotherhood and loyalty to the community of faith preserves the integrity of Islam, establishing a resilient sanctuary of justice and truth.",
  "145": "The terrifying final destination of the hypocrites in the lowest depths of Hell is proclaimed in 'Surely the hypocrites will be in the lowest depths of the Fire—and you will never find for them any helper—'\n\nThe verse reveals the ultimate, terrifying hierarchy of punishment in the Hereafter: 'Surely the hypocrites will be in the lowest depths of the Fire' ('Inna al-munafiqina fi al-darki al-asfali mina al-nar').\n\nHell consists of descending tiers ('darak'), and the Quran reveals that the very lowest, hottest, and most agonizing floor of Hell ('al-dark al-asfal') is reserved not for ordinary open disbelievers, but specifically for the hypocrites!\n\nClassical scholars like Ibn Abbas explain the profound justice behind this placement: the hypocrite committed a compounded crime—they possessed knowledge of the truth, claimed faith with their tongues, enjoyed the brotherhood of the believers, and then used that intimate trust to betray the community from within, inflict psychological harm, and mock divine revelation. Their crime of calculated betrayal is far more destructive than open opposition, warranting the most severe punishment in existence.\n\nThe verse emphasizes their total abandonment: 'and you will never find for them any helper' ('wa lan tajida lahum nasira'). No worldly connection, false oath, or patron will have the power to rescue them from the lowest depths.\n\nThis passage delivers the ultimate warning against hypocrisy, urging every soul to seek absolute sincerity before God, ensuring that our lives are dedicated to truth, righteousness, and eternal peace.\n\nCommentators observe that the placement of hypocrites in the lowest depths of Hell reflects the catastrophic destructiveness of internal betrayal. Sincere faith requires total transparency before God, urging every soul to cleanse the heart from deceit and seek absolute sincerity in all strivings. Cleansing the heart from deceit and living with transparent integrity protect the soul from spiritual ruin, ensuring eternal safety in the presence of God.",
  "146": "The four-part pathway to complete redemption, spiritual restoration, and the company of the faithful is proclaimed in 'except those who repent, mend their ways, hold fast to Allah, and are sincere in their devotion to Allah; they will be with the believers. And Allah will grant the believers a great reward.'\n\nImmediately following the terrifying announcement of the lowest depths of Hell in the preceding verse, the Quran opens the glorious gateway of salvation through four progressive spiritual conditions:\nFirst, remorse: 'except those who repent' ('Illa alladhina tabu'), abandoning hypocrisy and returning to God with genuine sorrow;\nSecond, practical reform: 'mend their ways' ('wa aslahu'), rectifying their public behavior, repairing community trust, and replacing treachery with honesty;\nThird, reliance: 'hold fast to Allah' ('wa-i'tasamu billah'), gripping God's guidance like an unbreakable lifeline;\nFourth, total sincerity: 'and are sincere in their devotion to Allah' ('wa akhlasu dinahum lillah'), purifying their worship and intentions from all desire for human applause and worldly status.\n\nWhen someone fulfills these four steps of comprehensive redemption, God grants them total restoration: 'they will be with the believers' ('fa-ula'ika ma'a al-mu'minin'). They are no longer classified as hypocrites; they are elevated into the noble company of the faithful.\n\nThe verse concludes with an inspiring promise: 'And Allah will grant the believers a great reward' ('wa sawfa yu'ti Allahu al-mu'minina ajran 'azima').\n\nThis verse proves that divine mercy can elevate a soul from the lowest depths of Hell to the highest ranks of Paradise.\n\nClassical scholars celebrate the glorious four-part path of redemption: repentance, moral reform, holding fast to God, and purifying devotion. Divine grace lovingly restores the penitent, elevating the soul from the lowest depths of Hell into the noble fellowship of the faithful and the eternal bliss of Paradise. Sincere repentance, moral reform, and purified devotion elevate the human spirit, unlocking the boundless treasures of divine forgiveness and everlasting Paradise.",
  "147": "The profound revelation of divine appreciation, the purpose of human existence, and the elimination of punishment are proclaimed in 'Why should Allah punish you if you are grateful and faithful? Allah is ever Appreciative, All-Knowing.'\n\nThe verse closes this extensive discourse on hypocrisy with one of the most comforting, sublime theological declarations in all of scripture: 'Why should Allah punish you if you are grateful and faithful?' ('Ma yaf'alu Allahu bi-'adhabikum in shakartum wa amantum?').\n\nGod reveals that He derives zero benefit, pleasure, or necessity from punishing His creation. God did not create humanity to torture, torment, or oppress them; punishment is solely the deserved consequence of human arrogance, ingratitude, and persistent injustice. When a human being embraces 'faith' ('amantum') in the Creator and lives in continuous 'gratitude' ('shakartum')—thanking God with the heart, praising Him with the tongue, and using His blessings in righteous service—all reason for punishment is completely eliminated!\n\nThe verse concludes by revealing two magnificent divine names: 'Allah is ever Appreciative, All-Knowing' ('wa kana Allahu Shakiran 'Alima'). God is 'Shakir' (Appreciative)—He values, cherishes, and multiplies the smallest sincere effort, good deed, and moment of gratitude from His servants—and 'Alim' (All-Knowing), fully aware of the sincerity behind our actions.\n\nThis magnificent verse brings Batch 2 to a sublime close, reassuring every human soul that God desires our guidance, peace, and eternal elevation in Paradise.\n\nScholars conclude that God derives zero benefit from punishment; divine grace is showered upon those who live in faith and gratitude. Thanking God with the heart, tongue, and righteous deeds eliminates all cause for punishment, filling life with divine peace, joy, and eternal honors before the Appreciative Creator. Living in continuous gratitude and faith fulfills our highest purpose, enveloping the soul in divine love, profound peace, and eternal honors before our Creator across all stages of existence.",
  "148": "The general prohibition against publicizing evil speech and the sacred exception for the oppressed are ordained in 'Allah does not like negative thoughts to be voiced—except by those who have been wronged. Allah is All-Hearing, All-Knowing.'\n\nThe verse establishes a foundational ethical principle of speech and public communication in Islam: 'Allah does not like negative thoughts to be voiced' ('La yuhibbu Allahu al-jahra bi-al-su'i mina al-qawl'). God detests the public broadcasting of vulgar language, private faults, slander, gossip, and destructive rumors, commanding believers to maintain modesty and preserve social dignity.\n\nHowever, the Quran introduces an essential, compassionate legal exception: 'except by those who have been wronged' ('illa man zulim'). A victim of injustice, oppression, domestic abuse, theft, or financial fraud is granted full legal and moral right to speak out publicly, name the oppressor, present their grievances before judicial courts, and seek assistance from the community to recover their rights.\n\nIn an authentic hadith in Sunan Abi Dawud, when a neighbor was harassed, the Prophet Muhammad advised him to place his furniture in the street; when passersby asked why, he explained his neighbor's harm, and the public rebuke forced the oppressor to cease his harassment.\n\nThe verse closes: 'Allah is All-Hearing, All-Knowing' ('wa kana Allahu Sami'an 'Alima'). God hears the cry of the oppressed and knows the true motives of the speaker.\n\nThis passage teaches that while Islam protects public decency, it fiercely empowers the oppressed to speak truth and demand justice without fear.\n\nScholars observe that this balanced legislation protects both public modesty and personal justice. While society must remain free from vulgar gossip and public malice, victims of oppression are divinely empowered to demand their rights openly. This ensures that legal systems remain accountable, empowering the weak and restraining oppressors under the vigilant gaze of the All-Hearing, All-Knowing God.",
  "149": "The spiritual superiority of gracious pardon and the imitation of divine forbearance are proclaimed in 'Whether you reveal or conceal a good or pardon an evil—surely Allah is Ever-Pardoning, Most Capable.'\n\nFollowing the permission granted to the oppressed to voice their grievances in the preceding verse, the Quran invites the human soul to the higher station of spiritual magnanimity: 'Whether you reveal or conceal a good or pardon an evil' ('In tubdu khayran aw tukhfuhu aw ta'fu 'an su'').\n\nThe verse outlines three noble moral choices:\nFirst, doing good publicly to inspire others;\nSecond, doing good secretly to preserve pure sincerity;\nThird, 'pardon an evil' ('ta'fu 'an su''), choosing to forgive an offender, overlook personal injury, and withhold vengeance when one possesses the power to retaliate.\n\nThe verse concludes with a profound theological reflection upon divine character: 'surely Allah is Ever-Pardoning, Most Capable' ('fa-inna Allaha kana 'Afuwwan Qadira').\n\nGod pairs His attribute of complete pardon ('Afuww') with His absolute power ('Qadir'). God possesses infinite, uncontested power to seize and punish every wrongdoer, yet He exercises magnificent forbearance and pardons billions of human sins daily. When a human being forgives others despite possessing the power to retaliate, they reflect this sublime divine attribute of merciful restraint.\n\nClassical scholars like al-Sa'di observe that this verse inspires believers to make forgiveness their default posture, knowing that pardoning others is the surest pathway to qualifying for God's eternal pardon and grace in the Hereafter, guiding the human conscience toward authentic righteousness and spiritual peace, moral clarity, and everlasting divine favor.\n\nClassical commentators highlight that exercising gracious pardon when possessing the power to retaliate is the pinnacle of spiritual strength. Forgiving others purifies the soul from bitterness, aligning human character with divine mercy. Believers are encouraged to cultivate magnanimity, knowing that pardoning human faults opens the boundless fountains of divine forgiveness in the Hereafter, guiding the human conscience toward authentic righteousness and spiritual peace, moral clarity, and everlasting divine favor. Choosing forgiveness during interpersonal conflicts reflects true spiritual nobility, ensuring that our lives are enveloped in the boundless love, peace, and mercy of our Lord.",
  "150": "The exposure of selective belief in prophets and the fallacy of compromising divine revelation are detailed in 'Surely those who deny Allah and His messengers and wish to make a distinction between Allah and His messengers, saying, “We believe in some and disbelieve in others,” desiring to forge a compromise,'\n\nThe verse exposes a fatal theological distortion practiced by various historical factions: 'Surely those who deny Allah and His messengers and wish to make a distinction between Allah and His messengers' ('yuriduna an yufarriqu bayna Allahi wa rusulih').\n\nCertain religious groups claimed they loved God while rejecting His chosen messengers, or accepted certain prophets while rejecting others, declaring: 'We believe in some and disbelieve in others' ('nu'minu bi-ba'din wa nakfuru bi-ba'd'). For example, some accepted Moses but rejected Jesus and Muhammad, while others accepted Moses and Jesus but rejected Muhammad.\n\nThe Quran diagnoses their underlying motive: 'desiring to forge a compromise' ('wa yuriduna an yattakhidhu bayna dhalika sabila'), attempting to manufacture a middle path between faith and disbelief that accommodates their tribal pride and cultural biases.\n\nIn Islamic theology, divine revelation is indivisible: all genuine prophets were sent by the exact same God with the exact same core message of monotheism. Rejecting a single legitimate prophet is an act of total rejection of the God who sent him.\n\nClassical commentators like Ibn Kathir observe that true faith requires wholehearted acceptance of all divine messengers, warning that cherry-picking prophets is an act of spiritual rebellion.\n\nCommentators point out that selective faith in prophets is a fatal theological error. All authentic messengers were emissaries of the same divine truth, calling humanity to pure monotheism. Rejecting any single prophet shatters the integrity of faith, reminding believers that true submission requires unconditional acceptance of all divine revelations. Embracing the entire lineage of prophetic guidance with wholehearted sincerity is the non-negotiable foundation of faith, protecting the soul from the destructive traps of sectarian pride.",
  "151": "The definitive verdict classifying selective believers as true disbelievers is declared in 'they are indeed the true disbelievers. And We have prepared for the disbelievers a humiliating punishment.'\n\nBuilding directly upon the theological exposure in the preceding verse, the Quran delivers the definitive divine judgment: 'they are indeed the true disbelievers' ('Ula'ika humu al-kafiruna haqqa').\n\nGod clarifies that attempting to compromise by accepting some prophets while rejecting others is not a valid form of partial faith; it is absolute, undeniable disbelief ('al-kafiruna haqqa'). Disbelieving in the final Prophet Muhammad nullifies any claimed adherence to Moses or Jesus, because both Moses and Jesus explicitly commanded their followers to believe in and support the final Messenger when he arrived.\n\nThe verse announces their eternal recompense: 'And We have prepared for the disbelievers a humiliating punishment' ('wa a'tadna li-al-kafirina 'adhaban muhina'). Because they arrogantly placed their subjective tribal preferences above divine revelation, divine justice requites them with a punishment that utterly strips away their false pride and exposes their hypocrisy before all creation.\n\nClassical scholars like al-Tabari observe that this verse provides total theological clarity: salvation requires universal prophetic faith without discrimination.\n\nThis passage teaches that authentic faith demands unconditional submission to all divine revelation, protecting the soul from the dangerous trap of selective religious adherence.\n\nClassical authorities explain that attempting to compromise between faith and disbelief is an act of spiritual deception that earns severe divine retribution. True religion demands wholehearted submission to the complete lineage of prophecy. Believers are called to stand firmly upon uncompromised truth, confident in the eternal rewards promised to the faithful. Wholehearted adherence to all divine messengers establishes authentic spiritual security, ensuring that our strivings are crowned with divine favor and eternal peace in the Hereafter, guiding the human conscience toward authentic righteousness and spiritual peace, moral clarity, and everlasting divine favor.",
  "152": "The glorious divine promise of full reward for those who embrace all messengers is proclaimed in 'As for those who believe in Allah and His messengers—accepting all; rejecting none—He will surely give them their rewards. And Allah is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.'\n\nIn radiant contrast to the selective deniers, the Quran portrays the comprehensive faith of the righteous: 'As for those who believe in Allah and His messengers—accepting all; rejecting none' ('Wa-alladhina amanu billahi wa rusulihi wa lam yufarriqu bayna ahadin minhum').\n\nThe Muslim community honors every genuine prophet sent by God throughout human history—from Adam, Noah, and Abraham to Moses, Jesus, and Muhammad—recognizing them as a sacred brotherhood of truth.\n\nGod delivers an absolute guarantee of salvation: 'He will surely give them their rewards' ('ula'ika sawfa yu'tihim ujurahum'). God will multiply their good deeds, elevate their spiritual stations, and grant them eternal dwelling in the gardens of Paradise.\n\nThe verse concludes with two comforting divine attributes: 'And Allah is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful' ('wa kana Allahu Ghafuran Rahima'). God pardons their human shortcomings and wraps them in boundless celestial mercy.\n\nClassical commentators like al-Sa'di observe that this verse celebrates the universal breadth of Islam: by honoring all prophets, believers inherit the blessings of the entire prophetic lineage.\n\nThis passage inspires believers to maintain unshakeable confidence in their comprehensive faith, looking forward to the glorious rewards prepared by the All-Forgiving, Most Merciful Creator.\n\nScholars of tafsir celebrate the expansive, inclusive nature of Islam. Honoring all legitimate prophets without discrimination unites the believer with the entire sacred history of humanity. The divine guarantee of full reward reassures the faithful that every righteous deed is eternally cherished by the All-Forgiving, Most Merciful Lord. Sincere belief in all prophets unites the human heart with the unbroken chain of divine truth, inspiring a life lived in profound gratitude, moral excellence, and dedicated service.",
  "153": "The historical recollection of obstinate demands and the golden calf is detailed in 'The People of the Book demand that you ˹O Prophet˺ bring down for them a revelation in writing from heaven. They demanded what is even greater than this from Moses, saying, “Make Allah visible to us!” So a thunderbolt struck them for their wrongdoing. Then they took the calf for worship after receiving clear signs. Still We forgave them for that ˹after their repentance˺ and gave Moses compelling proof.'\n\nCritics in Medina challenged the Prophet Muhammad, demanding that God send down a physical, leather-bound book directly from the heavens into their hands before they would believe.\n\nGod comforts the Prophet by revealing their historical pattern of unreasonable demands: 'They demanded what is even greater than this from Moses, saying, “Make Allah visible to us!”' When seventy elders demanded to see God with their physical eyes, a thunderbolt ('al-sa'iqah') struck them dead for their insolence.\n\nFurthermore, after witnessing the parting of the sea and deliverance from Pharaoh, 'they took the calf for worship after receiving clear signs.'\n\nYet the Quran highlights divine forbearance: 'Still We forgave them for that ˹after their repentance˺ and gave Moses compelling proof' ('sultanan mubina').\n\nClassical scholars explain that this historical review offered deep comfort to the Prophet: their obstinate demands were not a reflection of a lack of proof, but an ancient habit of arrogance.\n\nThis verse teaches that those who demand theatrical miracles are seeking excuses to delay obedience, while sincere hearts recognize the abundant proofs already present in revelation.\n\nCommentators observe that demanding theatrical, physical signs is an ancient symptom of spiritual arrogance. Sincere hearts recognize the profound wisdom and rational proofs already present in divine scripture. Sincere believers approach revelation with humility, avoiding unreasonable demands and finding certainty in the clear guidance of God.",
  "154": "The solemn covenant at Mount Sinai and the historical transgressions are recounted in 'We raised the Mount over them ˹as a warning˺ for ˹breaking˺ their covenant and said, “Enter the gate ˹of Jerusalem˺ with humility.” We also warned them, “Do not break the Sabbath,” and took from them a firm covenant.'\n\nThe verse recalls the awe-inspiring setting of their covenant: 'We raised the Mount over them ˹as a warning˺ for ˹breaking˺ their covenant' ('Wa rafa'na fawqahumu al-Tura bi-mithaqihim'). Mount Sinai was lifted above them like a towering canopy, emphasizing the sacred seriousness of their pledge to uphold the Torah.\n\nGod gave them clear moral commands:\nFirst: 'Enter the gate ˹of Jerusalem˺ with humility' ('sujjadan'), demanding humble bowing and seeking forgiveness ('hittah') upon entering the holy city, yet they insolently crawled and mocked the prayer;\nSecond: 'Do not break the Sabbath' ('la ta'du fi al-sabt'), commanding them to dedicate Saturday to worship, yet they devised deceitful fishing loopholes to violate the sacred day.\n\nThe verse emphasizes the solemnity of their pledge: 'and took from them a firm covenant' ('mithaqan ghaliza').\n\nClassical commentators observe that this historical review illustrates how easily human communities can betray solemn covenants when they allow worldly greed and legalistic manipulation to override sincere devotion.\n\nThis passage calls all believers to honor their sacred obligations with absolute fidelity and humility before God.\n\nClassical scholars highlight that the solemn covenant at Mount Sinai stands as an eternal reminder of the sacred weight of religious commitments. Betraying divine laws through legalistic loopholes brings moral decay. Believers are urged to uphold their sacred pledges with absolute integrity, approaching divine commandments with sincere humility, reverence, and steadfast devotion before the Lord of all creation. Approaching divine commandments with sincere reverence protects the soul from hypocrisy, ensuring that our covenants with God are fulfilled with absolute faithfulness and love.",
  "155": "The divine condemnation for chronic covenant-breaking, murdering prophets, and claiming closed hearts is declared in '˹They were condemned˺ for breaking their covenant, rejecting Allah’s signs, killing the prophets unjustly, and for saying, “Our hearts are unreceptive!”—it is Allah Who has sealed their hearts for their disbelief, so they do not believe except for a few—'\n\nThe verse lists four catastrophic historical crimes that brought divine condemnation:\nFirst, chronic treachery: 'for breaking their covenant' ('Fa-bi-ma naqdihim mithaqahum');\nSecond, ideological obstinacy: 'rejecting Allah’s signs' ('wa kufrihim bi-ayati Allah');\nThird, murdering moral leadership: 'killing the prophets unjustly' ('wa qatlihimu al-anbiya'a bi-ghayri haqq'), executing messengers like Zechariah and John the Baptist;\nFourth, arrogant dismissal of guidance: 'and for saying, “Our hearts are unreceptive!”' ('qulubuna ghulf'), claiming their minds were impervious to new revelation.\n\nThe Quran shatters this false excuse: 'it is Allah Who has sealed their hearts for their disbelief' ('bal taba'a Allahu 'alayha bi-kufrihim'). Their inability to absorb truth was not a natural defect, but the direct spiritual consequence of their own persistent rebellion.\n\nThe verse closes: 'so they do not believe except for a few,' honoring the sincere minority who embraced truth.\n\nThis passage delivers an urgent warning against spiritual arrogance, reminding every soul to keep the heart soft through continuous repentance and humility.\n\nScholars emphasize that persistent rebellion and the rejection of divine reminders cause severe spiritual hardening. When individuals close their minds to truth, they seal their own hearts against guidance. This passage calls believers to maintain spiritual tenderness through continuous repentance, prayer, and humble reflection upon divine signs. Guarding the heart against arrogance through regular remembrance of God keeps the soul receptive to divine light, protecting the believer from spiritual decay across all times, ensuring that our lives are governed by sincere devotion and moral integrity, unshakeable faith, and sincere humility before God.",
  "156": "The severe condemnation of slandering the virgin Mary with monstrous falsehood is declared in 'and for their denial and outrageous accusation against Mary,'\n\nConnecting directly to the list of historical crimes in the preceding verse, the Quran highlights their heinous slander against the most pure woman of creation: 'and for their denial and outrageous accusation against Mary' ('Wa bi-kufrihim wa qawlihim 'ala Maryama buhtanan 'azima').\n\nWhen Mary miraculously gave birth to Jesus without a human father through divine command, the corrupt religious establishment rejected the miracle out of arrogance, falsely and maliciously accusing the chaste, devout Mary of fornication!\n\nThe Quran characterizes this slander with the heaviest legal and moral term: 'an outrageous accusation' ('buhtanan 'azima')—a monstrous, fabricated lie that attacked the honor of a saintly woman chosen by God above all women of the world.\n\nClassical commentators like Ibn Kathir and al-Tabari observe that this malicious slander earned them severe divine wrath, demonstrating the extreme lengths to which corrupt elites will stoop to discredit divine miracles that challenge their authority.\n\nThis verse stands as an eternal divine defense of the immaculate honor and purity of Mary, establishing that slandering the chaste is a catastrophic sin before God.\n\nCommentators celebrate the divine defense of Mary as an eternal monument to her immaculate purity and spiritual elevation. Slandering the righteous is a monstrous sin that invites divine wrath. Islam fiercely honors Mary as a saintly role model of faith and devotion, protecting her noble legacy across all generations, establishing that the chaste are protected by divine justice and eternal honor. Sincere defense of the righteous and honoring the sacred purity of saintly figures reflects the deep moral integrity of Islam, establishing a culture of truth, honor, justice, and sincere devotion before the All-Seeing Creator across all generations, establishing that the chaste are protected by divine justice and eternal honor.",
  "157": "The celebrated Quranic refutation of the crucifixion and the historical illusion of Jesus's death are proclaimed in 'and for boasting, “We killed the Messiah, Jesus, son of Mary, the messenger of Allah.” But they neither killed nor crucified him—it was only made to appear so. Even those who argue for this ˹crucifixion˺ are in doubt. They have no knowledge whatsoever—only making assumptions. They certainly did not kill him.'\n\nThe verse addresses the historical boast of the corrupt leaders: 'We killed the Messiah, Jesus, son of Mary, the messenger of Allah.'\n\nThe Quran delivers the definitive, unshakeable divine refutation: 'But they neither killed nor crucified him—it was only made to appear so' ('wa ma qataluhu wa ma salabuhu wa lakin shubbiha lahum').\n\nGod thwarted their assassination plot through a miraculous rescue: God cast the physical resemblance ('shubbiha') of Jesus upon another man (such as the traitor who led the soldiers), who was seized and crucified in Jesus's place, while Jesus was miraculously delivered and elevated into the heavens alive.\n\nThe Quran exposes the uncertainty of all conflicting narratives: 'Even those who argue for this ˹crucifixion˺ are in doubt. They have no knowledge whatsoever—only making assumptions' ('illa ittiba'a al-zann'). The historical accounts of the crucifixion are riddled with theological contradictions, panic, and guesswork.\n\nThe verse concludes with absolute certainty: 'They certainly did not kill him' ('wa ma qataluhu yaqina').\n\nThis verse establishes the pristine Islamic truth: God never abandoned His beloved prophet to a humiliating death.\n\nClassical authorities explain that the Quranic refutation of the crucifixion establishes the absolute truth of divine protection. God never abandoned His beloved Messiah to a humiliating execution; rather, He miraculously thwarted the conspiracy of his enemies. This pure monotheistic truth liberates the human mind from theological confusion and false assumptions. The divine protection granted to Jesus reinforces our certainty in God's power, inspiring believers to trust that truth will always triumph over deception in divine timing.",
  "158": "The miraculous bodily ascension of Jesus into the heavens is proclaimed in 'Rather, Allah raised him up to Himself. And Allah is Almighty, All-Wise.'\n\nBuilding directly upon the denial of the crucifixion in the preceding verse, the Quran reveals the true reality of what occurred to Jesus: 'Rather, Allah raised him up to Himself' ('Bal rafa'ahu Allahu ilayh').\n\nClassical commentators like al-Tabari, Ibn Kathir, and al-Qurtubi explain that 'raised him up' ('rafa'ah') refers to a physical, bodily ascension: God elevated Jesus alive, in both body and soul, into the celestial realm, preserving him in direct divine hospitality, completely unharmed by the assassination conspiracy of his enemies.\n\nThe verse closes with two magnificent divine names: 'And Allah is Almighty, All-Wise' ('wa kana Allahu 'Azizan Hakima').\n\nGod is 'al-'Aziz' (the Almighty), possessing uncontested power to rescue His beloved messengers effortlessly and shatter the plots of mighty empires, and 'al-Hakim' (the All-Wise), executing every historical event with perfect timing, justice, and purpose.\n\nThis passage honors Jesus with the highest dignity, proving that divine omnipotence easily overcomes human conspiracies and establishing that the Messiah remains alive in the presence of God until his appointed return.\n\nScholars of tafsir observe that the bodily ascension of Jesus into the heavens is a radiant manifestation of divine omnipotence. God possesses limitless power to elevate His servants and protect truth. Believing in Jesus's living ascension strengthens the faith of believers, assuring humanity that divine wisdom effortlessly overcomes all human conspiracies. Believing in the miraculous ascension of Jesus reminds the faithful of God's limitless creative power, filling the soul with unshakeable hope and serene trust in divine providence, assuring humanity that the righteous are perpetually protected by the Almighty, and that his message of monotheism remains an eternal light for all seekers of truth across all generations, guiding humanity toward authentic peace and salvation.",
  "159": "The prophecy of universal faith in Jesus before his death and his role as a witness are proclaimed in 'Every one of the People of the Book will definitely believe in him before his death. And on the Day of Judgment Jesus will be a witness against them.'\n\nThe verse presents a profound, unbroken prophecy regarding the future return of Jesus: 'Every one of the People of the Book will definitely believe in him before his death' ('Wa in min ahli al-kitabi illa la-yu'minanna bihi qabla mawtih').\n\nIn an authentic hadith in Sahih al-Bukhari and Sahih Muslim, the Prophet Muhammad explained: 'By the One in Whose Hand is my soul, the son of Mary will soon descend among you as a just judge; he will break the cross, kill the swine, abolish the jizyah, and wealth will become so abundant that no one will accept charity!' When Jesus descends before the Day of Judgment, all remaining People of the Book will recognize his true identity as a mortal prophet of God and embrace Islam.\n\nThe verse describes his role on the Day of Resurrection: 'And on the Day of Judgment Jesus will be a witness against them' ('wa yawma al-qiyamati yakunu 'alayhim shahida'). Jesus will testify that he delivered pure monotheism, commanded worship solely for God, and never claimed divinity or crucifixion.\n\nThis verse provides profound prophetic clarity, uniting the final chapters of human history upon the triumph of monotheism.\n\nCommentators highlight the unbroken prophecy of Jesus's future descent as a just ruler who will unite humanity upon pure monotheism. Testifying against false doctrines on the Day of Judgment will bring ultimate clarity to sacred history, proving that Jesus's lifelong mission was dedicated exclusively to the worship of the one true God. The prophetic return of Jesus stands as a glorious beacon of universal monotheism, assuring the faithful that truth, justice, and peace will permanently triumph on earth.",
  "160": "The disciplinary prohibition of certain wholesome foods for past transgressions is detailed in 'We forbade the Jews certain foods that had been lawful to them for their wrongdoing, and for hindering many from the Way of Allah,'\n\nThe verse explains the historical origin of certain stringent dietary restrictions found in the Torah: 'We forbade the Jews certain foods that had been lawful to them for their wrongdoing' ('Fa-bi-zulmin mina alladhina hadu harramna 'alayhim tayyibatin uhillat lahum').\n\nWholesome foods—such as animal fats and certain birds—were originally completely lawful for all humanity; but God placed disciplinary restrictions upon the Israelites as a direct consequence of their persistent moral oppression ('zulm') and social injustice.\n\nThe Quran identifies their second major crime: 'and for hindering many from the Way of Allah' ('wa bi-saddihim 'an sabili Allahi kathira'). They actively blocked seekers from embracing truth, distorted scriptural prophecies, and created artificial obstacles to prevent people from following divine guidance.\n\nClassical commentators like al-Sa'di observe that this verse corrects a major misconception: stringent religious restrictions were not a sign of divine favoritism, but a disciplinary consequence for past rebellions. Islam came through the Prophet Muhammad to remove these historical burdens and restore the original, wholesome ease of divine law.\n\nThis passage teaches that persistent wrongdoing restricts blessings, while sincere obedience unlocks divine ease and abundance.\n\nClassical scholars point out that disciplinary prohibitions in past scriptures were consequences of moral oppression and injustice. Islam came through the Prophet Muhammad to lift these historical burdens, restoring wholesome ease and spiritual balance. Sincere obedience preserves divine blessings, ensuring that our lives are governed by mercy and justice. Following the uncorrupted guidance of Islam restores spiritual ease, reminding believers that divine laws are designed with compassion to nurture human flourishing and peace, guiding humanity toward moral elevation and eternal salvation in the presence of our Lord.",
  "161": "The condemnation of practicing usury and financial corruption is declared in 'taking interest despite its prohibition, and consuming people’s wealth unjustly. We have prepared for the disbelievers among them a painful punishment.'\n\nThe verse lists two further economic crimes that brought disciplinary restrictions and divine wrath:\nFirst: 'taking interest despite its prohibition' ('wa akhdhihimu al-riba wa qad nuhu 'anh'). The Torah explicitly forbade usury and interest, yet corrupt lenders devised deceitful loopholes to exploit debtors and enrich themselves through interest-bearing loans.\nSecond: 'and consuming people’s wealth unjustly' ('wa aklihim amwala al-nasi bi-al-batil'), embezzling orphan property, taking bribes, and seizing assets through fraudulent contracts.\n\nThe Quran delivers the divine sentence: 'We have prepared for the disbelievers among them a painful punishment' ('wa a'tadna li-al-kafirina minhum 'adhaban alima'). Those who build their livelihoods on financial exploitation, usury, and theft will face agonizing torment in the Hereafter, guiding the human conscience toward authentic righteousness and spiritual peace, moral clarity, and everlasting divine favor.\n\nClassical scholars emphasize that economic justice is a universal, non-negotiable pillar of all prophetic revelations. Exploiting the poverty of fellow human beings for profit is a grave sin that destroys societal peace and brings divine retribution.\n\nThis passage calls all believers to ensure that their financial earnings are purely lawful, transparent, and grounded in mutual fairness and compassion.\n\nScholars emphasize that financial exploitation and usury are universally forbidden across all prophetic traditions. Building wealth upon the suffering of the poor brings severe divine retribution. Believers are called to conduct commerce with transparency, fairness, and mutual compassion, ensuring that our earnings are purely lawful and pleasing to God. Purifying our financial earnings from all forms of interest and deceit is a sacred duty, establishing a just economic system where wealth brings true barakah and communal harmony, protecting human dignity and establishing absolute justice across all financial interactions.",
  "162": "The profound celebration of grounded scholars and the promise of great reward are proclaimed in 'But those of them well-grounded in knowledge, the faithful ˹who˺ believe in what has been revealed to you ˹O Prophet˺ and what was revealed before you—˹especially˺ those who establish prayer— and those who pay alms-tax and believe in Allah and the Last Day, to these ˹people˺ We will grant a great reward.'\n\nDemonstrating its trademark justice and nuance, the Quran immediately praises the sincere scholars among the People of the Book: 'But those of them well-grounded in knowledge' ('Lakini al-rasikhuna fi al-'ilmi minhum').\n\nIndividuals like Abdullah ibn Salam and his companions possessed deep scriptural insight that enabled them to recognize the truth of Islam:\nFirst, comprehensive faith: 'believe in what has been revealed to you ˹O Prophet˺ and what was revealed before you';\nSecond, devout worship: '˹especially˺ those who establish prayer' ('wa-al-muqimina al-salah');\nThird, financial purification: 'and those who pay alms-tax' ('wa-al-mu'tuna al-zakah');\nFourth, certainty in the Hereafter: 'and believe in Allah and the Last Day.'\n\nGod guarantees their ultimate honor: 'to these ˹people˺ We will grant a great reward' ('ula'ika sa-nu'tihim ajran 'azima').\n\nIn an authentic hadith, the Prophet Muhammad explained that those among the People of the Book who believe in their original prophet and then embrace Islam receive a double reward!\n\nThis verse honors sincere scholarship, proving that authentic knowledge naturally leads to humility, monotheism, and righteous action, uniting human hearts in the loving service of the Creator across all eras.\n\nCommentators celebrate sincere scholars who combine deep knowledge with humble worship and active charity. Sincere learning naturally leads to monotheism, prayer, and social responsibility. God promises double rewards to those who honor past scriptures by embracing the final revelation, establishing authentic scholarship as a sacred beacon of guidance. Sincere scholars who practice their knowledge with devotion are the true heirs of the prophets, guiding society toward moral elevation, intellectual clarity, and eternal salvation.",
  "163": "The magnificent universal lineage of divine revelation from Noah to Muhammad is proclaimed in 'Indeed, We have sent revelation to you ˹O Prophet˺ as We sent revelation to Noah and the prophets after him. We also sent revelation to Abraham, Ishmael, Isaac, Jacob, and his descendants, ˹as well as˺ Jesus, Job, Jonah, Aaron, and Solomon. And to David We gave the Psalms.'\n\nThe verse establishes the absolute continuity of divine guidance: 'Indeed, We have sent revelation to you ˹O Prophet˺ as We sent revelation to Noah and the prophets after him' ('Inna awhayna ilayka kama awhayna ila Nuhin wa-al-nabiyyina min ba'dih').\n\nThe Prophet Muhammad is not an unprecedented novelty; he received divine inspiration through the Archangel Gabriel in the exact same manner as the great prophets of history.\n\nThe Quran recites a breathtaking roll-call of sacred history: Abraham, Ishmael, Isaac, Jacob, the twelve tribes, Jesus, Job (Ayyub), Jonah (Yunus), Aaron (Harun), Solomon (Sulayman), and 'And to David We gave the Psalms' ('wa atayna Dawuda Zabura').\n\nAll of these holy messengers received divine revelation, called humanity to the worship of the one true God, and established moral justice on earth.\n\nClassical commentators like Ibn Kathir explain that this verse dismantles all skepticism regarding Muhammad's prophethood, demonstrating that the Quran is the authentic, glorious culmination of the unbroken global chain of divine revelation.\n\nClassical authorities highlight the majestic continuity of divine revelation from Noah and Abraham to David, Jesus, and Muhammad. The Quran is the glorious culmination of this unbroken global lineage, proving that all prophets taught the same eternal truth: sincere submission and pure worship of the one true Creator. The sublime harmony of all revealed scriptures proves that Islam is the eternal religion of humanity, calling all nations to unite upon pure monotheism and righteous action, uniting human hearts in the loving service of the Creator across all eras.",
  "164": "The vastness of prophetic history and the direct divine speech to Moses are proclaimed in 'There are messengers whose stories We have told you already and others We have not. And to Moses Allah spoke directly.'\n\nThe verse reveals the immense breadth of divine guidance throughout human history: 'There are messengers whose stories We have told you already and others We have not' ('Wa rusulan qad qasasnahum 'alayka min qablu wa rusulan lam naqsushum 'alayk').\n\nIn an authentic narration in Musnad Ahmad, the Prophet Muhammad explained that God sent 124,000 prophets across human history to every nation and tribe on earth, among whom 313 were messengers with scriptures, of whom only twenty-five are mentioned by name in the Quran. God never left any civilization without guidance.\n\nThe verse highlights a unique distinction granted to Moses: 'And to Moses Allah spoke directly' ('wa kallama Allahu Musa taklima'). The emphatic cognate 'taklima' confirms that God spoke directly to Moses with real, uncreated divine speech at Mount Sinai without an angelic intermediary, granting him the exalted title 'Kalimullah.'\n\nClassical scholars observe that this verse affirms the authentic attributes of God, proving that divine communication manifests in diverse, profound ways throughout history.\n\nThis passage inspires deep reverence for the vast history of divine care for humanity.\n\nScholars of tafsir observe that the vast history of 124,000 prophets reflects God's boundless care for all human civilizations. Direct divine speech to Moses at Mount Sinai demonstrates the diverse, profound ways God communicates with His chosen messengers, inspiring deep reverence for the vast tapestry of sacred prophetic history. Reflecting upon the vast tapestry of prophetic history deepens our gratitude for divine care, inspiring believers to follow the timeless guidance of revelation with unwavering dedication, ensuring that our lives remain constantly illuminated by the light of truth, moral excellence, and sincere submission.",
  "165": "The ultimate purpose of sending messengers to eliminate human excuses is proclaimed in '˹All were˺ messengers delivering good news and warnings so humanity should have no excuse before Allah after ˹the coming of˺ the messengers. And Allah is Almighty, All-Wise.'\n\nThe verse defines the supreme purpose of all prophetic missions: '˹All were˺ messengers delivering good news and warnings' ('Rusulan mubashshirina wa mundhirin').\n\nProphets brought glad tidings ('mubashshirin') of divine love, peace, and eternal Paradise to the righteous, and compassionate warnings ('mundhirin') of divine justice and retribution to the wicked.\n\nThe Quran articulates a profound legal and moral principle: 'so humanity should have no excuse before Allah after ˹the coming of˺ the messengers' ('li-alla yakuna li-al-nasi 'ala Allahi hujjatun ba'da al-rusul'). God in His absolute justice never punishes a human being without first sending clear, accessible guidance. The sending of prophets establishes the definitive divine proof ('al-hujjah') upon humanity, removing all excuses of ignorance on the Day of Judgment.\n\nThe verse concludes: 'And Allah is Almighty, All-Wise' ('wa kana Allahu 'Azizan Hakima'). God's power is absolute, and every revelation He sent was crafted with perfect wisdom for human elevation.\n\nThis passage teaches that divine guidance is a supreme gift of mercy that carries profound personal accountability, calling every soul to respond with sincere obedience.\n\nCommentators emphasize that the sending of messengers eliminates all human excuses of ignorance on the Day of Judgment. Divine guidance is a supreme gift of mercy that carries profound personal responsibility. Sincere believers are urged to respond to prophetic warnings with immediate obedience, dedicating life to righteous action and faith. Responding to prophetic guidance with immediate obedience fulfills our highest purpose, ensuring that our earthly journey concludes in eternal honor and divine pleasure in Paradise, enveloped in the eternal mercy and grace of our Lord, protected from all fear and sorrow.",
  "166": "The supreme celestial testimony of God and the angels to the authenticity of the Quran is proclaimed in 'Yet ˹if you are denied, O Prophet,˺ Allah bears witness to what He has sent down to you—He has sent it with His knowledge. The angels too bear witness. And Allah ˹alone˺ is sufficient as a Witness.'\n\nWhen critics in Medina denied the Prophet Muhammad's mission, God provided the ultimate reassurance: 'Allah bears witness to what He has sent down to you—He has sent it with His knowledge' ('Lakini Allahu yash-hadu bi-ma anzala ilayka anzalahu bi-'ilmih').\n\nThe Quran is not human literature; it was revealed directly from God's infinite, all-encompassing knowledge ('bi-'ilmih'), containing flawless scientific truths, historical precision, moral perfection, and cosmic wisdom that no human mind could manufacture.\n\nThe verse adds the celestial testimony: 'The angels too bear witness' ('wa-al-mala'ikatu yash-hadun'). The archangels and celestial hosts testify to the divine origin of the revelation.\n\nThe verse concludes with supreme authority: 'And Allah ˹alone˺ is sufficient as a Witness' ('wa kafa billahi Shahida'). The testimony of the Creator of the universe far outweighs the skepticism of all human deniers combined.\n\nClassical commentators like al-Sa'di observe that this verse fills the heart of the Prophet and the believers with unshakeable certainty and peace, proving that divine truth needs no human validation.\n\nClassical scholars celebrate the supreme celestial testimony of God and His holy angels to the divine authorship of the Quran. The unshakeable truth of revelation needs no human validation. This divine testimony fills the believer's heart with profound peace, dignity, and unwavering confidence in the eternal word of God. The celestial testimony of God and His angels fills the believer's soul with profound tranquility, empowering the faithful to walk the straight path with dignity and unshakeable courage, knowing that God's truth is an eternal beacon of guidance for all humanity.",
  "167": "The severe spiritual diagnosis of persistent disbelief and actively blocking others from truth is declared in 'Those who disbelieve and hinder ˹others˺ from the Way of Allah have certainly strayed far away.'\n\nThe verse addresses those who commit a compounded crime against truth: 'Those who disbelieve and hinder ˹others˺ from the Way of Allah' ('Inna alladhina kafaru wa saddu 'an sabili Allah').\n\nThey were not content with their own personal skepticism; driven by malice and arrogance, they actively campaigned to block others, spread disinformation, harass believers, and prevent seekers from discovering the light of Islam.\n\nThe Quran delivers the definitive spiritual verdict: 'have certainly strayed far away' ('qad dallu dalalan ba'ida'). The phrase 'dalal ba'id' describes a state of total, catastrophic misguidance, wandering deep into a dark wilderness far removed from sound reason, human conscience, and divine mercy. When someone actively works to extinguish divine light, they blind their own soul beyond return.\n\nClassical scholars observe that this verse warns against being an obstacle to goodness, reminding us that misguiding others carries a double burden of guilt on the Day of Judgment.\n\nThis passage calls humanity to open their hearts to truth, urging believers to remain steadfast champions of divine guidance and universal justice.\n\nScholars conclude that actively obstructing others from divine truth carries a devastating double burden of guilt. Leading people into misguidance corrupts the human soul beyond return. Believers are called to remain courageous champions of guidance, sharing divine light with love and defending universal justice across all human society. Upholding the light of truth and inviting humanity to goodness protects society from corruption, ensuring that our strivings are dedicated to establishing universal peace, justice, and moral dignity across all communities in the service of God, ensuring that our strivings are crowned with divine peace, honor, and eternal salvation.",
  "168": "The divine judgment upon persistent disbelief and deliberate oppression is declared in 'Those who disbelieve and wrong themselves—surely Allah will neither forgive them nor guide them to any path'\n\nThe verse identifies individuals who combine two destructive spiritual crimes:\nFirst, ideological rebellion: 'Those who disbelieve' ('Inna alladhina kafaru'), rejecting the clear signs of the Creator and denying His messengers;\nSecond, active oppression: 'and wrong themselves' ('wa zalamu'), oppressing others through injustice, persecution, and blocking seekers from divine truth.\n\nThe Quran proclaims the severe spiritual consequence of this persistent rebellion: 'surely Allah will neither forgive them nor guide them to any path' ('lam yakuni Allahu li-yaghfira lahum wa la li-yahdiyahum tariqa').\n\nWhen a person intentionally suppresses their own conscience, hardens their heart against all rational evidence, and persists in hostility until death, they cut off the internal capacity for genuine repentance. In divine justice, God withholds guidance from those who choose spiritual blindness.\n\nClassical commentators like al-Sa'di observe that this verse describes those who die in persistent rebellion without repentance, warning that unrepentant injustice leads to total spiritual abandonment.\n\nThis passage calls every human being to maintain moral humility, seek divine forgiveness with urgency, and avoid the destructive trap of persistent defiance before the Lord of all creation, ensuring that our lives are governed by truth and justice.\n\nScholars observe that the combination of disbelief and active oppression is the most destructive path a human soul can take. When individuals knowingly suppress truth and harm others, they seal their own hearts against divine light. Sincere believers are urged to maintain continuous moral vigilance, seeking divine forgiveness and ensuring that their lives are governed by justice and truth. Sincere repentance unlocks the boundless treasures of divine mercy, ensuring that the human soul remains protected from persistent injustice and eternal spiritual loss, guiding human hearts toward lasting peace and salvation.",
  "169": "The eternal reality of Hell for the obstinate and the effortless justice of God are proclaimed in 'except that of Hell, to stay there for ever and ever. And that is easy for Allah.'\n\nContinuing directly from the preceding verse regarding the path of the persistent wrongdoers, the Quran reveals their sole remaining destination: 'except that of Hell, to stay there for ever and ever' ('Illa tariqa Jahannama khalidina fiha abada').\n\nThe Arabic phrasing 'khalidina fiha abada' emphasizes the absolute permanence and eternity of their recompense. In divine justice, an individual whose internal heart permanently rejected truth throughout their entire worldly existence faces an enduring recompense that matches their permanent spiritual orientation.\n\nThe verse closes with a powerful reminder of divine supremacy: 'And that is easy for Allah' ('wa kana dhalika 'ala Allahi yasira'). Holding every oppressor accountable, executing absolute justice, and maintaining the realm of retribution require zero physical effort, hesitation, or fatigue for the Almighty Creator of the universe.\n\nClassical scholars like Ibn Kathir explain that this verse removes all illusions of escape for tyrants and oppressors: worldly power, wealth, and legal evasion vanish completely in the Hereafter.\n\nThis sobering verse calls humanity to reflect on the eternal consequences of our daily choices, inspiring believers to seek divine mercy and live in steadfast obedience to the Lord of the worlds.\n\nClassical commentators highlight that the permanence of Hell reflects the irreversible choice of those who permanently aligned themselves against divine truth. The absolute ease with which God executes justice reminds humanity that earthly power and evasion are completely powerless before the Almighty, urging every soul to seek divine mercy before death arrives. Turning to the Almighty with sincere remorse brings eternal peace, assuring the faithful that God's grace is always ready to receive and elevate the repentant heart.",
  "170": "The universal proclamation of prophetic truth and the call to sincere belief for humanity's own benefit are delivered in 'O humanity! The Messenger has certainly come to you with the truth from your Lord, so believe for your own good. But if you disbelieve, then ˹know that˺ to Allah belongs whatever is in the heavens and the earth. And Allah is All-Knowing, All-Wise.'\n\nThe Quran addresses all of humankind with a loving, urgent appeal: 'O humanity! The Messenger has certainly come to you with the truth from your Lord' ('Ya ayyuha al-nasu qad ja'akumu al-rasulu bi-al-haqqi min Rabbikum'). The Prophet Muhammad was sent not as a regional or tribal leader, but as a universal messenger bearing the uncorrupted truth from the Lord who created, sustains, and nurtures all humanity.\n\nThe verse explains the personal benefit of faith: 'so believe for your own good' ('fa-aminu khayran lakum'). Sincere faith does not benefit God in the slightest; it benefits human beings by purifying character, providing inner peace, establishing social justice, and securing eternal Paradise.\n\nThe Quran shatters human arrogance: 'But if you disbelieve, then ˹know that˺ to Allah belongs whatever is in the heavens and the earth.' God is completely Self-Sufficient.\n\nThe verse concludes: 'And Allah is All-Knowing, All-Wise' ('wa kana Allahu 'Aliman Hakima').\n\nThis passage establishes the universal relevance of Islam, calling every human being to embrace divine guidance for their own ultimate welfare and eternal peace.\n\nCommentators emphasize that prophetic guidance is a universal gift of mercy designed for human flourishing. Sincere faith brings inner tranquility, moral dignity, and eternal salvation to the believer, while disbelievers only harm their own souls. This passage calls all humanity to embrace the light of Islam with gratitude and joy. Sincere adherence to the Prophet Muhammad transforms human character, guiding our choices with profound wisdom, moral balance, and unshakeable hope in eternal Paradise.",
  "171": "The celebrated theological charter refuting religious extremism, the Trinity, and affirming the true prophetic identity of Jesus is proclaimed in 'O People of the Book! Do not go to extremes regarding your faith; say nothing about Allah except the truth. The Messiah, Jesus, son of Mary, was no more than a messenger of Allah and the fulfilment of His Word through Mary and a spirit ˹created by a command˺ from Him. So believe in Allah and His messengers and do not say, “Trinity.” Stop!—for your own good. Allah is only One God. Glory be to Him! He is far above having a son! To Him belongs whatever is in the heavens and whatever is on the earth. And Allah is sufficient as a Trustee of Affairs.'\n\nThis monumental verse provides the definitive Quranic refutation of theological excess:\nFirst, condemning extremism: 'Do not go to extremes regarding your faith' ('la taghlu fi dinikum'). Religious extremism ('al-ghuluww') includes exaggerating the status of mortal prophets to make them divine.\nSecond, defining Jesus with pristine precision: 'The Messiah, Jesus, son of Mary, was no more than a messenger of Allah and the fulfilment of His Word through Mary and a spirit ˹created by a command˺ from Him' ('Innama al-Masihu 'Isa ibnu Maryama rasulu Allahi wa kalimatuhu alqaha ila Maryama wa ruhun minh').\n- He is a 'Messenger' ('rasul');\n- He is God's 'Word' ('kalimatuh') because he was created without a father through the direct command 'Be!' ('Kun');\n- He is a 'spirit from Him' ('ruhun minh')—an honored soul created directly by God and breathed into Mary by Gabriel, just as God breathed a created spirit into Adam.\nThird, the absolute rejection of the Trinity: 'and do not say, “Trinity.” Stop!—for your own good. Allah is only One God' ('wa la taqulu thalathah, intahu khayran lakum, innama Allahu Ilahun Wahid').\nFourth, transcendent perfection: 'Glory be to Him! He is far above having a son!' ('Subhanahu an yakuna lahu walad').\n\nThe verse concludes: 'And Allah is sufficient as a Trustee of Affairs,' establishing pure monotheism as the universal truth of existence.",
  "172": "The humble servitude of the Messiah and the archangels before the Creator is proclaimed in 'The Messiah would never be too proud to be a servant of Allah, nor would the angels nearest to Allah. Those who are too proud and arrogant to worship Him will be brought before Him all together.'\n\nThe verse presents a profound theological and psychological reality: 'The Messiah would never be too proud to be a servant of Allah' ('Lan yastankifa al-Masihu an yakuna 'abdan lillah').\n\nThe Arabic verb 'yastankif' denotes turning away in disdain, arrogance, or pride. The Messiah Jesus celebrated his servanthood ('ubudiyyah') to God with deep joy, humility, and love, never claiming divinity or seeking human worship. In authentic prophetic history, Jesus lived a life of fasting, prostration, and total dependence upon his Creator.\n\nThe Quran adds the celestial elite: 'nor would the angels nearest to Allah' ('wa la al-mala'ikatu al-muqarrabun'). The Archangel Gabriel, Michael, and the bearers of the Throne submit in continuous, joyful worship before the Almighty without the slightest arrogance.\n\nThe verse delivers a stern warning to arrogant mortals who refuse worship: 'Those who are too proud and arrogant to worship Him will be brought before Him all together' ('wa man yastankif 'an 'ibadatihi wa yastakbir fa-sa-yahshuruhum ilayhi jami'a').\n\nClassical commentators observe that if the Messiah and the mighty angels view servanthood to God as their supreme honor, how can any fragile human being act with arrogance?\n\nThis passage teaches that true human nobility is found exclusively in humble, willing servitude to the Creator.\n\nClassical scholars celebrate the humble servitude of Jesus and the angels as the supreme model of spiritual nobility. True greatness is not found in claiming divine status, but in willing, joyful submission to the Creator. Acknowledging our servanthood before God fills the soul with deep peace, purpose, and eternal honor.",
  "173": "The contrasting destinies of humble believers and arrogant deniers are proclaimed in 'As for those who believe and do good, He will reward them in full and increase them out of His grace. But those who are too proud and arrogant, He will subject them to a painful punishment. And besides Allah they will find no protector or helper.'\n\nBuilding directly upon the division of human responses in the previous verse, the Quran details their eternal outcomes:\n\nFirst, the glorious reward of the faithful: 'As for those who believe and do good, He will reward them in full and increase them out of His grace' ('Fa-amma alladhina amanu wa 'amilu al-salihati fa-yuwaffihim ujurahum wa yaziduhum min fadlih'). God guarantees full compensation for their good deeds and multiplies their rewards out of His boundless grace ('fadl'), granting them eternal dwellings in Paradise and the supreme joy of divine pleasure.\n\nSecond, the punishment of the arrogant: 'But those who are too proud and arrogant, He will subject them to a painful punishment' ('wa amma alladhina istankafu wa-istakbaru fa-yu'adh-dhibuhum 'adhaban alima'). Those who refused to humble their hearts before God will face agonizing torment and eternal disgrace.\n\nThe verse concludes: 'And besides Allah they will find no protector or helper' ('wa la yajiduna lahum min duni Allahi waliyyan wa la nasira').\n\nClassical scholars like al-Sa'di observe that this verse provides the ultimate motivation for spiritual life: humility and righteous action unlock boundless divine grace, while arrogance leads to total helplessness and ruin before the All-Just Lord.\n\nScholars of tafsir highlight that divine grace multiplies the reward of the righteous far beyond their deeds. Sincere faith and active charity unlock the boundless treasures of Paradise, while arrogance leads to eternal loss. Believers are motivated to persevere in good works, confident in the supreme generosity and justice of their Lord.",
  "174": "The universal advent of conclusive divine evidence and the brilliant light of the Quran are proclaimed in 'O humanity! There has come to you conclusive evidence from your Lord. And We have sent down to you a brilliant light.'\n\nThe Quran addresses all of humankind with majestic authority: 'O humanity! There has come to you conclusive evidence from your Lord' ('Ya ayyuha al-nasu qad ja'akum burhanun min Rabbikum').\n\nThe Arabic word 'burhan' refers to an overwhelming, decisive, and indisputable proof that leaves no room for doubt or confusion. The Prophet Muhammad—with his flawless moral character, his fulfilled prophecies, and his miraculous life—stands as living, conclusive evidence of God's truth for all humanity.\n\nThe verse highlights the divine revelation: 'And We have sent down to you a brilliant light' ('wa anzalna ilaykum nuran mubina'). The Quran is a 'brilliant light' ('nur mubin') that illuminates the human intellect, dispels the suffocating darkness of polytheism, superstition, and moral confusion, and clarifies the path to eternal peace.\n\nClassical commentators like al-Tabari explain that just as physical light illuminates a dark room and allows human eyes to see clearly, the Quran illuminates human hearts and consciences, allowing souls to distinguish truth from falsehood and justice from oppression.\n\nThis passage invites every human being to step into the radiant light of divine revelation, discovering true purpose, dignity, and spiritual clarity.\n\nCommentators describe the Quran as a radiant, living light that dispels the darkness of ignorance, superstition, and moral confusion. Stepping into this divine light illuminates the human conscience, empowering the soul to distinguish truth from falsehood and live with profound clarity and peace. Walking in the radiant light of the Quran dispels all spiritual confusion, providing an unshakeable anchor of peace, dignity, and divine truth across all areas of life, leading human souls directly to the eternal presence of God.",
  "175": "The divine guarantee of mercy, boundless grace, and guidance along the Straight Path is proclaimed in 'As for those who believe in Allah and hold fast to Him, He will admit them into His mercy and grace and guide them to Himself through the Straight Path.'\n\nThe verse reveals the glorious destiny of those who respond to the 'brilliant light' of the Quran: 'As for those who believe in Allah and hold fast to Him' ('Fa-amma alladhina amanu billahi wa-i'tasamu bih').\n\nThe concept of 'i'tisam billah' means gripping God's guidance like an unbreakable lifeline—placing total trust in His promises, following His revelation, and aligning all personal and communal decisions with His commands.\n\nGod promises three sublime spiritual gifts:\nFirst, 'admit them into His mercy' ('fa-sa-yudkhiluhum fi rahmatin minhu'), granting forgiveness, peace of mind, and entrance into Paradise;\nSecond, 'and grace' ('wa fadl'), bestowing boundless, unearned celestial honors, spiritual elevation, and divine pleasure;\nThird, 'and guide them to Himself through the Straight Path' ('wa yahdihim ilayhi siratan mustaqima'), illuminating their earthly journey with moral clarity, wisdom, and steadfastness until they arrive directly at the presence of their Lord.\n\nClassical scholars observe that the Straight Path leads not merely to an abstract destination, but 'to Himself' ('ilayhi')—achieving ultimate closeness, love, and intimacy with the Creator.\n\nThis verse fills the believer's heart with profound joy, reassuring the faithful of God's continuous care and eternal guidance.\n\nClassical authorities explain that holding firmly to God (i'tisam billah) is the ultimate key to spiritual safety. The Straight Path leads directly to divine intimacy and eternal mercy in Paradise, reassuring the faithful that God's loving protection surrounds all who place their trust in Him. Wholehearted reliance upon God fills life with serene purpose, ensuring that our strivings are crowned with divine intimacy, boundless grace, and eternal salvation.",
  "176": "The final inheritance ruling of Surah an-Nisa governing childless estates (Kalalah) is detailed in 'They ask you ˹for a ruling, O Prophet˺. Say, “Allah gives you a ruling regarding those who die without children or parents.” If a man dies childless and leaves behind a sister, she will inherit one-half of his estate, whereas her brother will inherit all of her estate if she dies childless. If this person leaves behind two sisters, they together will inherit two-thirds of the estate. But if the deceased leaves male and female siblings, a male’s share will be equal to that of two females. Allah makes ˹this˺ clear to you so you do not go astray. And Allah has ˹perfect˺ knowledge of all things.'\n\nIn an authentic hadith in Sahih al-Bukhari, when the noble Companion Jabir ibn Abdillah fell critically ill in Medina and was childless with multiple sisters, the Prophet visited him, and God revealed this final, crowning verse of Surah an-Nisa to resolve the inheritance of 'Kalalah' (a person who dies leaving neither surviving parents nor children).\n\nThe Quran details the precise shares of full and paternal siblings:\n1. An only sister inherits one-half (1/2) of her deceased brother's estate;\n2. An only brother inherits the entire estate of his deceased sister (or the remainder after fixed shares);\n3. Two or more sisters inherit two-thirds (2/3) collectively;\n4. If male and female siblings exist together, the estate is divided proportionally: 'a male’s share will be equal to that of two females' ('li-al-dhakari mithlu hazzi al-unthayayn'), reflecting the male's legal obligation of financial maintenance.\n\nThe chapter concludes with divine purpose: 'Allah makes ˹this˺ clear to you so you do not go astray. And Allah has ˹perfect˺ knowledge of all things.' This brings Surah an-Nisa to a triumphant legal and moral close.\n\nScholars conclude that the comprehensive inheritance laws of Surah an-Nisa represent the pinnacle of divine equity. Ordaining precise shares for siblings in childless estates protects family harmony and prevents disputes, establishing an enduring legal framework that reflects the perfect wisdom and justice of God."
};
